"use strict";
/* Multi-angle teaching per sign: vignette, trap, analogy, number, history, pair, patient, document, teach-back. Keyed by sign id. */
const ANGLES={"resp-rate":{"vignette":"A 72-year-old woman with a urinary infection looks comfortable, with pulse 96 and BP 128/78, but the nurse has written a respiratory rate of 28. You count it yourself over a full minute and get 30. That single number moves her from oral antibiotics and home to blood cultures, lactate and admission.","trap":"Respiratory rate is the most often estimated or copied vital sign; a rate written as 16 or 18 without a timed count is a guess. Count for 60 seconds while pretending to take the pulse, and treat any rate above 24 in an adult as a deterioration sign until proven otherwise.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the respiratory rate rise before the blood pressure falls in sepsis and acidosis, and what does that mean for how early it warns you?","a":"Chemoreceptors respond within minutes to acidosis, hypoxaemia and cytokine-driven fever, raising minute ventilation long before compensatory vasoconstriction fails and blood pressure drops. Respiratory rate is therefore an early, upstream signal, while hypotension is a late sign of exhausted compensation."},"analogy":"The respiratory rate is the smoke detector of the vital signs: it goes off while the fire is still small, whereas blood pressure is the fire alarm that sounds when the roof is already burning.","number":"A respiratory rate above 27 per minute was the single strongest predictor of cardiopulmonary arrest on general wards, outperforming pulse and blood pressure (Fieselmann et al, J Gen Intern Med 1993; respiratory rate scores from 21 to 24 upward in NEWS2 and is one of the three qSOFA items at 22 or more).","patient":"You are breathing faster than normal, which tells me your body is working harder than it should to fight this infection. I want to keep you in and give you fluids and antibiotics through a drip while we watch that settle.","document":"RR 30/min, counted over 60 s at rest, SpO2 95% on air; NEWS2 respiratory component 3.","pair":{"with":"kussmaul-breathing","why":"A high rate with shallow breaths points to pain, sepsis or lung disease, while deep sighing breaths at a modest rate point to metabolic acidosis and send you to the blood gas."},"history":"Ask whether the breathlessness came on over minutes, hours or days, because a rate that has crept up over days in a frail patient is sepsis or heart failure until proven otherwise."},"postural":{"vignette":"A 34-year-old man with two days of black stools has a lying pulse of 88 and BP 118/74. Standing for one minute, his pulse climbs to 124 and he has to sit back down because the room spins. That is a large bleed until proven otherwise, so you start two large-bore cannulas, cross-match blood and call the endoscopist before his supine numbers change.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on a fall in standing blood pressure; postural hypotension is common in healthy older people and insensitive for moderate blood loss. The useful finding is the pulse rising by 30 or more, or dizziness so severe the patient cannot stand to finish the test, measured after lying for 2 minutes and standing for 1.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the pulse increment detect blood loss that supine vital signs miss, and why must the patient stand rather than sit?","a":"Standing pools 500 to 1000 mL of blood in the legs, which unmasks a reduced circulating volume that supine venous return had hidden; the baroreflex compensates first with tachycardia, so the pulse rises before pressure falls. Sitting pools far less blood and misses the response, which is why the validated test requires standing."},"analogy":"Supine vital signs are like reading a fuel gauge on a flat road; standing the patient tilts the car and shows how little is really in the tank.","number":"Postural pulse increment of 30 or more, or severe postural dizziness, has sensitivity 97% and specificity 98% for large blood loss (over 630 mL) but only 22% sensitivity for moderate loss (McGee, Abernethy and Simel, JAMA 1999, Is this patient hypovolemic?).","patient":"When you stood up your heart had to race to keep your blood pressure up, which tells me you have lost a fair amount of blood. We are going to replace it and find where it is coming from.","document":"Lying P 88 BP 118/74; standing 1 min P 124 BP 100/60 with severe dizziness (postural pulse increment 36/min, positive).","pair":{"with":"shock-index","why":"The shock index uses supine numbers already present and works in anyone, whereas the postural test needs a patient who can stand but detects a smaller bleed."},"history":"Ask about beta-blockers, rate-limiting calcium channel blockers and pacemakers, which blunt the pulse response and can make a large bleed look reassuring."},"cap-refill":{"vignette":"A 19-year-old with 24 hours of vomiting and diarrhoea has a pulse of 108 and capillary refill of 4 seconds at the fingertip in a cool department. On its own that refill time would not tell you much, but together with the tachycardia, dry axilla and inability to tolerate oral fluids it tips you towards intravenous rather than oral rehydration.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat 2 seconds as a universal cut-off and a single refill time as proof of dehydration; refill lengthens with age, cold rooms, female sex and peripheral vascular disease, and the JAMA review found it had no diagnostic value for vomiting-and-diarrhoea hypovolaemia in adults. Use it as one strand of a cluster, warm the hand first, and press for 5 seconds at the fingertip held at heart level.","teachback":{"q":"What physiology makes capillary refill a marker of perfusion, and what besides volume changes it?","a":"Blanching empties the capillary bed and refill depends on arteriolar inflow, which sympathetic vasoconstriction slows when the body defends central pressure. Ambient temperature, age, sex, local vascular disease and the pressure applied all alter the time, so it is a perfusion sign that is heavily confounded in adults."},"analogy":"Capillary refill is like watching how fast a squeezed sponge re-swells: it tells you about the tap feeding it, but a cold sponge re-swells slowly whatever the tap is doing.","number":"Upper limit of normal is about 2 s in children and young men, 3 s in adult women and 4.5 s in the elderly (Schriger and Baraff, Ann Emerg Med 1988); for adults with vomiting or diarrhoea the JAMA Rational Clinical Examination found capillary refill had no diagnostic value (McGee et al, JAMA 1999).","patient":"When I press on your fingertip the colour is slow to come back, which fits with you being short of fluid. Everything else I have found points the same way, so we will give you fluid through a drip.","document":"CRT 4 s at fingertip, hand at heart level, warm room; with tachycardia 108 and dry axilla, consistent with hypovolaemia.","pair":{"with":"passive-leg-mottling","why":"Refill time at the finger is noisy in a warm, well patient, while knee mottling and a cool knee are a reliable sign of established shock."},"history":"Ask how many times they have vomited or passed stool and whether they have passed urine in the last 6 hours, since the history of losses predicts volume depletion better than refill time."},"dry-axilla":{"vignette":"An 84-year-old man from a care home is drowsy with a sodium of 152. His skin turgor is meaningless at his age and his mucous membranes are hard to interpret through mouth breathing, but when you slip a gloved finger into his armpit it is completely dry. That finding, with a creatinine twice his baseline, makes you treat this as hypovolaemic hypernatraemia and start fluid before the sodium result is even formally reviewed.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to read a moist axilla as reassuring; dry axilla is specific but only half-sensitive, so its absence excludes nothing. It is also meaningless in a patient who has recently used antiperspirant or is in a very cold room.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the axilla a better dryness sign than the tongue in an elderly patient?","a":"Axillary sweating depends on sympathetic cholinergic drive and is reduced when extracellular volume falls, and the axilla is not confounded by mouth breathing, anticholinergic drugs on salivary glands or dentures. The tongue dries with mouth breathing alone, which is common in the drowsy elderly."},"analogy":"The axilla is a hidden lawn that is only watered when the reservoir is full; when it is dry, the mains pressure has dropped.","number":"Dry axilla has sensitivity 50% and specificity 82%, positive LR 2.8, for hypovolaemia in elderly patients (Eaton et al 1994, cited in McGee et al, JAMA 1999).","patient":"Your armpit is dry, which is a small clue that your body is short of fluid. Together with your blood tests it tells me you need fluid through a drip.","document":"Axillae dry bilaterally (gloved finger), mucous membranes dry; with sodium 152 and AKI, consistent with hypovolaemia.","pair":{"with":"skin-turgor","why":"Turgor is lost with age regardless of hydration, whereas axillary sweating is preserved in the hydrated elderly and is the sign that still carries information."},"history":"Ask about anticholinergic drugs and whether the patient has been drinking normally, since reduced intake over days in the elderly is the usual cause of the dry axilla you are feeling."},"skin-turgor":{"vignette":"A 78-year-old woman with falls is referred as dehydrated because a tent of skin on the back of her hand stays up for several seconds. Her axillae are moist, her pulse is 72 and her urea is normal. You conclude the turgor is simply her age, cancel the planned litre of saline that would have strained her heart, and look elsewhere for the cause of the falls.","trap":"The commonest trap is to test turgor on the hand of an elderly patient, where elastin loss makes tenting universal and meaningless. If you test it at all in an adult, use the subclavicular skin or the inner thigh, and in children the abdominal wall.","teachback":{"q":"Why does skin turgor work in children but not in older adults?","a":"Recoil of pinched skin depends on dermal elastin and on interstitial fluid content; in children elastin is abundant so a slow recoil reflects lost interstitial water, but in the elderly elastin is degraded and recoil is slow whatever the hydration. The sign therefore has sensitivity in children and little diagnostic value in adults."},"analogy":"Skin turgor is like judging a tyre by pressing it: a new tyre springs back when inflated, but an old perished tyre stays dented however much air it holds.","number":"Poor skin turgor had no demonstrated diagnostic value for hypovolaemia in adults with vomiting, diarrhoea or poor intake in the JAMA Rational Clinical Examination (McGee et al, JAMA 1999); in children it remains one of the three best dehydration signs with capillary refill and abnormal breathing (Steiner et al, JAMA 2004).","patient":"Your skin stays pinched up for a moment, but at your age that is normal and does not mean you are dry. The other checks show you have enough fluid on board.","document":"Subclavicular skin turgor normal; hand turgor reduced, attributed to age; axillae moist, no clinical hypovolaemia.","pair":{"with":"dry-axilla","why":"Both are tested in seconds, but the axilla retains diagnostic value in the elderly while turgor loses it."},"history":"Ask the age: in a patient over 65 the sign is almost uninterpretable before you start."},"shock-index":{"vignette":"A 26-year-old woman with lower abdominal pain has a pulse of 112 and BP 108/70, which the triage note calls normal. Her shock index is 1.04. With a positive pregnancy test that number is enough for you to ask for group and save, a bedside scan and the gynaecology registrar at once rather than waiting for her pressure to fall.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to be reassured by a normal blood pressure alone; young patients vasoconstrict and hold their pressure until they have lost a third of their volume. The index also fails in patients on beta-blockers, with pacemakers, or in hypertensives whose normal pressure is high.","teachback":{"q":"Why does dividing pulse by systolic pressure reveal shock that neither number shows alone?","a":"Early compensated haemorrhage raises heart rate and holds systolic pressure, so each remains inside its own normal range while their ratio drifts up. The index captures the compensation itself, which is the physiological signature of a falling stroke volume."},"analogy":"Shock index is like watching engine revs against road speed: when the revs climb and the speed stays flat, the clutch is slipping even though the speedometer looks normal.","number":"Normal shock index is 0.5 to 0.7; an index above 0.9 identified emergency patients needing immediate treatment or admission whom conventional vital signs had called stable (Rady et al, Ann Emerg Med 1994), and in the TraumaRegister DGU analysis mortality and transfusion need rose stepwise across classes from below 0.6 to 1.4 and above (Mutschler et al, Crit Care 2013).","patient":"Your heart is beating fast for the blood pressure you have, which is an early warning that you may be bleeding inside. We are going to get blood ready and scan you straight away.","document":"HR 112, SBP 108, shock index 1.04 (raised), no beta-blockade; treated as occult haemorrhagic shock.","pair":{"with":"postural","why":"Both unmask compensated hypovolaemia, but the shock index needs no standing and the postural test is more sensitive for a smaller bleed."},"history":"Ask about beta-blockers, pacemakers and known hypertension, which each break the assumptions behind the ratio."},"conj-pallor":{"vignette":"A 68-year-old man presents with breathlessness on stairs and no chest pain. Pulling down his lower lid, the anterior rim of the conjunctiva is the same pale pink as the posterior fornix instead of being distinctly redder. You send a full blood count before anything else and the haemoglobin comes back at 7.4, redirecting the whole work-up towards a gastrointestinal bleed.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to inspect the palms or nail beds, which vary with skin tone and temperature, or to call pallor absent and feel reassured; no examination finding rules anaemia out. Look specifically for loss of the colour contrast between the anterior rim and the posterior conjunctiva.","teachback":{"q":"What are you comparing when you look for conjunctival rim pallor, and why does that comparison beat simply judging the colour?","a":"The anterior rim of the inferior palpebral conjunctiva normally has a richer vascular bed than the posterior part, so it looks redder; with anaemia both pale and the contrast disappears. Comparing two surfaces in the same eye removes variation in lighting, skin tone and the examiner's memory of what normal looks like."},"analogy":"It is like checking a two-tone paint sample: you are not judging the shade in isolation but whether the stripe still stands out from the background.","number":"Conjunctival rim pallor has a positive LR of 16.7 for anaemia, the highest of any pallor sign, but no finding has a useful negative LR, so absence does not exclude anaemia (Sheth et al, J Gen Intern Med 1997, cited in McGee Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis).","patient":"The inside of your lower eyelid is paler than it should be, which usually means the blood is low in iron-carrying red cells. I am going to check a blood count to confirm it and find out why.","document":"Conjunctival rim pallor present bilaterally (loss of anterior-posterior colour contrast); Hb requested.","pair":{"with":"jaundice","why":"Both are read on the eye, but pallor is a judgement of contrast while scleral icterus is a yellow tint that follows bilirubin above about 35 to 50 micromol/L."},"history":"Ask about black or bloody stools, heavy periods and NSAID or anticoagulant use, since the pallor only becomes a decision when it is tied to a source of loss."},"passive-leg-mottling":{"vignette":"A 59-year-old man with pneumonia has a blood pressure of 102/60 after two litres of fluid and a lactate of 3.1. His knees are cool and show a purplish lace extending halfway up the thigh. The mottling score of 3 tells you his tissues are not perfused despite the acceptable pressure, so you start noradrenaline and refer to intensive care now.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to wait for hypotension and to treat a normal blood pressure as proof of adequate perfusion; mottling and a cool knee are peripheral perfusion signs that precede and outlast changes in pressure. It is also misread in cold rooms and in patients with chronic livedo, so warm the patient and look for change over time.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the skin around the knee mottle in shock, and what does its extent tell you?","a":"In circulatory failure the sympathetic system diverts blood from skin to heart and brain, and skin perfusion falls earliest in the dependent, poorly collateralised region around the knee; stagnant deoxygenated blood in dilated venules produces the purple net. The further the mottling spreads up the thigh, the greater the degree and duration of shunting, which is why extent tracks mortality."},"analogy":"Mottled knees are the first street lights to go out when the grid is overloaded: the power company cuts the suburbs to keep the hospital lit.","number":"A mottling score of 2 to 3 at 6 hours carried an odds ratio of 16 for 14-day death in septic shock, and a score of 4 to 5 an odds ratio of 74, independent of blood pressure and vasopressor dose (Ait-Oufella et al, Intensive Care Med 2011).","patient":"The blotchy purple skin on your knees tells me that your body is pulling blood away from the skin to protect your vital organs. That means the infection is serious and you need stronger support in the intensive care unit.","document":"Mottling score 3 (extending to mid-thigh), knees cool to touch, CRT at knee 5 s; lactate 3.1; vasopressor started.","pair":{"with":"cap-refill","why":"Fingertip refill is confounded by age, sex and temperature, whereas knee mottling and knee refill reflect central redistribution and predict mortality."},"history":"Ask the nurse what the skin looked like an hour ago, because spreading mottling is a dynamic sign and its direction of travel is the decision."},"qsofa":{"vignette":"A 70-year-old woman with a chest infection is slightly confused, breathing at 24 and has a systolic pressure of 98. All three qSOFA criteria are present, so the department protocol moves her into the sepsis pathway with lactate, cultures and antibiotics inside the hour. Had she scored 0 you would still have treated her pneumonia, but the score tells you which bed she needs.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat a qSOFA under 2 as excluding sepsis; the score was derived to predict death, not to diagnose infection, and it misses about 40% of patients who die. Use it for escalation and prognosis, and keep screening for sepsis with clinical judgement or NEWS2.","teachback":{"q":"What was qSOFA designed to predict, and why does that limit its use as a screening test?","a":"It was derived from the Sepsis-3 data to identify infected patients outside the ICU at risk of in-hospital death or prolonged ICU stay, using three bedside items. Because it was tuned for specificity in predicting mortality, its sensitivity is low, so a negative score cannot be used to reassure or to delay antibiotics."},"analogy":"qSOFA is a tripwire set high on the hillside: when it is tripped the danger is real, but plenty of the enemy walk underneath it.","number":"qSOFA of 2 or more has pooled sensitivity 61% and specificity 72% for mortality in suspected infection (Fernando et al, Ann Intern Med 2018 meta-analysis); the 2021 Surviving Sepsis guideline recommends against using it as a single screening tool.","patient":"Your confusion, fast breathing and low blood pressure together tell us this infection is affecting your whole body. We are going to treat it urgently with antibiotics and fluids and watch you very closely.","document":"qSOFA 3 (RR 24, SBP 98, new confusion); sepsis pathway initiated, lactate and cultures sent, antibiotics given 14:10.","pair":{"with":"resp-rate","why":"Respiratory rate is the qSOFA item that changes first and is the one most often recorded wrongly, so the score is only as good as the count behind it."},"history":"Ask relatives whether the confusion is new, because altered mentation is the item that is most often mistaken for baseline dementia."},"purpura-glass":{"vignette":"A 4-year-old with fever and lethargy has a scattering of dark spots on the legs. Pressed under a clear glass they do not fade. Non-blanching spots in a febrile child mean possible meningococcal disease, so you give ceftriaxone within minutes rather than waiting for the bloods.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to be reassured by a sparse or small petechial rash, or to wait for the classic spreading purpura; early meningococcal rash can be a few pinpoint spots or even blanching macules. Press firmly with a glass, undress the child fully, and check the conjunctivae and palate.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a purpuric rash not blanch, and what does that tell you about the vessel wall?","a":"Blanching erythema is blood inside dilated vessels that pressure can push away, whereas purpura is blood that has leaked out through damaged capillaries into the dermis and cannot be displaced. In meningococcal sepsis the leak comes from endotoxin-driven endothelial injury and consumption of clotting factors, so the rash is a direct sign of the vasculitic and coagulopathic process."},"analogy":"Pressing a glass on the rash is like pressing on a wet carpet: a puddle on top moves away under your hand, but a stain that has soaked into the fibres stays put.","number":"Among 218 febrile children with a non-blanching rash, 11% had meningococcal disease, and the risk was higher with purpura over 2 mm, ill appearance or raised CRP (Wells et al, Arch Dis Child 2001).","patient":"These spots do not fade when I press on them, which can be a sign of a serious infection in the blood. We are giving antibiotics straight away, before the tests are back, because minutes matter.","document":"Non-blanching petechial rash on both shins and one 3 mm purpuric lesion, glass test positive; ceftriaxone given 10:42.","pair":{"with":"neck-stiffness","why":"The rash marks septicaemia and the stiff neck marks meningitis; either alone is enough to treat, and the rash is the one that cannot wait for a lumbar puncture."},"history":"Ask how quickly the spots appeared and whether the child has been unusually drowsy or cold-handed, because speed of evolution separates meningococcal purpura from a benign viral petechial rash."},"pulsus-paradoxus":{"vignette":"A 55-year-old woman with breast cancer and a known pericardial effusion presents with breathlessness and a pulse of 118. Inflating the cuff and letting it down slowly, the first Korotkoff sounds appear only in expiration at 112 and become continuous at 94, a paradox of 18 mmHg. That number converts a known effusion into tamponade and you call for urgent pericardiocentesis.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to apply the sign to patients without a known effusion, where severe asthma, COPD and massive pulmonary embolism also produce a large paradox. In tamponade it also disappears in aortic regurgitation, atrial septal defect and severe left ventricular dysfunction, so absence does not exclude tamponade in those patients.","teachback":{"q":"Explain why systolic pressure falls more than 10 mmHg on inspiration in tamponade.","a":"In tamponade the total heart volume is fixed by the pericardial fluid, so when inspiration increases venous return to the right ventricle the septum bulges left and the left ventricle fills less; its stroke volume and systolic pressure therefore fall with each breath in. Normal inspiration causes the same ventricular interdependence to a tiny degree, which is why a paradox under 10 mmHg is physiological."},"analogy":"Two people sharing a sleeping bag zipped tight: when one draws a deep breath and expands, the other is squeezed, and in tamponade the bag cannot stretch at all.","number":"Pulsus paradoxus over 12 mmHg has sensitivity 98% and specificity 83% for tamponade in patients with a pericardial effusion, positive LR 5.9 and negative LR 0.03 (Roy et al, JAMA 2007, Does this patient with a pericardial effusion have cardiac tamponade?).","patient":"Your blood pressure drops noticeably every time you breathe in, which tells me the fluid around your heart is now squeezing it. The treatment is to drain that fluid with a needle, and we need to do it today.","document":"Pulsus paradoxus 18 mmHg by cuff (sounds intermittent 112, continuous 94), JVP raised, known effusion; tamponade physiology.","pair":{"with":"tamponade-signs","why":"Beck's triad is rare and late, whereas a measured paradox over 12 mmHg is present in nearly every tamponade and can be quantified at the bedside."},"history":"Ask about asthma or COPD symptoms, because airway obstruction alone can produce a paradox over 10 mmHg and would make the sign uninterpretable."},"jvp":{"vignette":"A 76-year-old man arrives breathless with crackles at both bases and a chest X-ray that could be either infection or oedema. At 45 degrees the top of the internal jugular pulsation sits 6 cm above the sternal angle and rises further on abdominal pressure. That raised venous pressure makes heart failure the working diagnosis and you give furosemide before the natriuretic peptide is back.","trap":"The commonest error is to look for the external jugular or to mistake the carotid for the venous pulse; the JVP has a double waveform, is non-palpable, falls on inspiration and sitting up, and is obliterated by light pressure at the clavicle. Another trap is to call a non-visible JVP normal when it is in fact so high it is above the angle of the jaw.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the sternal angle used as the reference, and what does a JVP of 3 cm above it correspond to in the right atrium?","a":"The sternal angle sits roughly 5 cm above the centre of the right atrium in most positions, so adding 5 cm to the vertical height of the venous column gives an estimate of right atrial pressure in cm of water. A column 3 cm above the angle is about 8 cm of water, the upper limit of normal, and anything higher means raised right-sided filling pressure."},"analogy":"The jugular vein is a glass manometer tube connected to the right atrium; you are reading the height of the water level, not the pulse of a pipe.","number":"Elevated JVP has a positive LR of 5.1 (sensitivity 39%, specificity 92%) for heart failure in dyspnoeic emergency patients (Wang et al, JAMA 2005, Does this dyspneic patient in the emergency department have congestive heart failure?).","patient":"The vein in your neck is fuller than it should be, which shows that your heart is struggling to pump fluid through and it is backing up into your lungs. The water tablet through the drip will help you breathe.","document":"JVP 6 cm above sternal angle at 45 degrees, positive abdominojugular reflux, double waveform, falls on inspiration.","pair":{"with":"ajr","why":"The JVP reads the resting pressure while the abdominojugular reflux stresses the system with a volume load and catches heart failure the resting column misses."},"history":"Ask about orthopnoea and the number of pillows, because a raised JVP in a patient who cannot lie flat is heart failure, while a raised JVP with a flat-sleeping patient should make you think of pericardial disease or right-sided problems."},"ajr":{"vignette":"A 64-year-old woman with COPD is breathless and wheezy, and her resting JVP is hard to see. With firm pressure over the mid-abdomen for 10 seconds the venous column rises 4 cm and stays up until you release. That positive reflux tells you her right-sided filling pressure is abnormal and shifts the diagnosis from an exacerbation towards heart failure, so you add a diuretic and echocardiogram.","trap":"The commonest error is to press for 2 or 3 seconds and call a transient rise positive; in health the column rises briefly and falls within a few beats despite continued pressure. A positive test is a sustained rise of over 3 cm for the full 10 to 15 seconds of pressure, with the patient breathing normally and not performing a Valsalva.","teachback":{"q":"What does abdominal pressure do to venous return, and why does the normal heart cope while the failing heart does not?","a":"Pressing the abdomen squeezes blood from the splanchnic veins into the right atrium, a sudden preload challenge of a few hundred millilitres. A compliant right ventricle with normal filling pressure accepts and pumps it within seconds so the neck veins fall back, whereas a ventricle already on the steep part of its pressure-volume curve cannot, and the pressure stays elevated."},"analogy":"It is like pouring a jug of water into a sink: a clear drain swallows it with only a brief rise, but a partly blocked drain holds the water level up for as long as you keep pouring.","number":"A positive abdominojugular reflux has a positive LR of 6.4 (sensitivity 24%, specificity 96%) for heart failure in dyspnoeic emergency patients (Wang et al, JAMA 2005).","patient":"When I pressed on your tummy the vein in your neck filled up and stayed full, which shows your heart cannot cope with extra blood coming back to it. That is why your breathing is worse and why we are treating your heart as well as your lungs.","document":"Abdominojugular reflux positive (sustained rise 4 cm with 10 s mid-abdominal pressure), resting JVP 3 cm.","pair":{"with":"jvp","why":"The reflux is a provocation test with higher specificity that remains useful when the resting JVP is borderline or hard to see."},"history":"Ask whether the patient has recently increased their fluid intake or stopped a diuretic, since a positive reflux in that setting is almost always decompensation rather than lung disease."},"apex-displaced":{"vignette":"A 58-year-old man with a new systolic murmur and mild breathlessness has an apex beat you find in the sixth intercostal space, 3 cm lateral to the midclavicular line. A displaced apex means a dilated ventricle rather than an innocent flow murmur, so you request an urgent echocardiogram and start heart failure treatment rather than reassuring him.","trap":"The commonest error is to search for the apex with the patient supine only and call it impalpable or normal; roll the patient to the left to find it, then record position from the supine or sitting position, since the left lateral position shifts it laterally in everyone. Also, a displaced apex can be due to a right-sided chest problem pushing the heart, not just a big heart.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a dilated left ventricle move the apex beat laterally and inferiorly, and what does that separate it from?","a":"The apex beat is the point where the ventricle strikes the chest wall at the start of systole; as the ventricle enlarges its tip moves downward and outward along the axis of the heart. Hypertrophy without dilatation keeps the apex in place but makes it forceful and sustained, so position tells you about volume and character tells you about pressure."},"analogy":"A balloon tied inside a box pushes on a different corner when it is over-inflated; the apex beat is the corner the heart is pressing on.","number":"An apical impulse lateral to the midclavicular line has a positive LR of 10.3 for a reduced ejection fraction (McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis, cited in The Hospitalist 2013 review).","patient":"The tip of your heart is beating further out to the side of your chest than it should, which usually means the heart has become enlarged. We need a heart scan to see how well it is pumping.","document":"Apex beat 6th intercostal space, 3 cm lateral to MCL (supine), diffuse, not sustained.","pair":{"with":"s3","why":"The displaced apex tells you the ventricle is dilated, while the S3 tells you it is also under high filling pressure right now."},"history":"Ask about previous myocardial infarction and heavy alcohol use, the two commonest reasons for a dilated ventricle in an emergency patient."},"s3":{"vignette":"A 69-year-old woman with breathlessness and an unremarkable chest X-ray has a low-pitched extra sound just after S2 at the apex, heard with the bell in the left lateral position. In a 69-year-old a third heart sound is never innocent, so you treat as heart failure and ask for an echocardiogram while awaiting the natriuretic peptide.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to listen with the diaphragm at the left sternal edge and declare no gallop; S3 is low-frequency and is heard only with the bell lightly applied at the apex, with the patient rolled to the left. A gallop is also physiological under 40, so its meaning depends on age.","teachback":{"q":"What produces the third heart sound, and why does it imply high filling pressure in an adult?","a":"S3 is generated in early diastole when rapid inflow of blood is abruptly decelerated by a ventricle that is already full or stiff, setting the ventricular walls vibrating. In a young person with vigorous suction it can occur normally, but in an adult it means that the ventricle is operating at a high end-diastolic pressure."},"analogy":"Pouring water fast into a nearly full bucket makes a thud that an empty bucket never makes; S3 is that thud.","number":"A third heart sound has a positive LR of 11 (sensitivity 13%, specificity 99%) for heart failure in dyspnoeic emergency patients (Wang et al, JAMA 2005) and predicts a left ventricular end-diastolic pressure over 15 mmHg.","patient":"I can hear an extra beat in your heart that means it is being overfilled and is struggling to pump the fluid on. The water tablets will take that pressure off while we scan the heart.","document":"S3 gallop at apex with bell in left lateral position, no S4; consistent with raised LV filling pressure.","pair":{"with":"s4","why":"S3 is early diastolic and means a volume-overloaded or failing ventricle, while S4 is presystolic and means a stiff ventricle with preserved function."},"history":"Ask the patient's age first: the same sound is normal at 25 and heart failure at 65."},"carotid-upstroke":{"vignette":"An 81-year-old man presents after a syncopal episode while walking to the bus. He has a harsh systolic murmur, and when you lay your fingers on his carotid the pulse rises slowly and feels small, peaking late. That slow upstroke tells you the murmur is severe aortic stenosis, and he gets an echocardiogram and admission rather than discharge with a holter.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on the loudness of the murmur, which falls as stenosis becomes critical and output drops; the pulse character and timing of the murmur peak tell you about severity. In the elderly, stiff arteries can normalise the upstroke, so a brisk pulse does not exclude severe stenosis.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the carotid upstroke slow and small in severe aortic stenosis?","a":"The ventricle must eject through a fixed narrow orifice, so the pressure wave transmitted to the arteries rises gradually over the whole of systole rather than in a sharp early jet. The reduced stroke volume per beat makes the pulse small and the delayed peak makes it late, giving the slow-rising, low-amplitude pulse."},"analogy":"Pushing water through a pinched hose produces a steady weak trickle that takes time to build rather than the sharp squirt of an open nozzle.","number":"A slow rate of rise of the carotid pulse has positive LRs of 2.8 to 130 across studies for aortic stenosis, and absence of murmur radiation to the right carotid has a negative LR of 0.05 to 0.10 (Etchells et al, JAMA 1997, Does this patient have an abnormal systolic murmur?).","patient":"The pulse in your neck rises slowly, which tells me the valve leaving your heart has narrowed and is making it hard to pump blood out. That is probably why you blacked out, and we need a heart scan and a specialist to decide about the valve.","document":"Carotid upstroke slow-rising and reduced in volume, late-peaking ESM radiating to both carotids, A2 soft; severe AS until echo.","pair":{"with":"valsalva-murmur","why":"Both are harsh systolic murmurs with syncope, but the aortic stenosis pulse is slow while the HOCM pulse is brisk, and the HOCM murmur gets louder with Valsalva."},"history":"Ask about exertional syncope, angina or breathlessness, because symptomatic severe stenosis is a surgical referral and not a watch-and-wait."},"irregular-pulse":{"vignette":"A 74-year-old woman presents with a transient episode of word-finding difficulty that has resolved. Her radial pulse is irregularly irregular at 96 with a variable volume. That pulse turns a TIA into probable cardioembolic TIA and she leaves with an ECG-confirmed atrial fibrillation, a CHA2DS2-VASc score and anticoagulation planned rather than just antiplatelets.","trap":"The commonest error is to count the apex or pulse for 15 seconds and miss a paroxysm, or to call frequent ectopics atrial fibrillation; ectopics produce a regular rhythm with pauses, whereas fibrillation has no underlying pattern and a pulse deficit. Palpate for a full minute and always confirm with an ECG.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the pulse irregularly irregular in atrial fibrillation, and why does the volume vary beat to beat?","a":"Hundreds of chaotic atrial impulses bombard the AV node, which conducts them unpredictably, so ventricular beats come at random intervals. Each short interval leaves the ventricle less time to fill, so stroke volume and pulse volume vary, and the weakest beats fail to open the aortic valve enough to be felt, creating the pulse deficit."},"analogy":"It is like rain on a tin roof: no two drops land the same distance apart and the small ones you barely hear.","number":"An irregular pulse on palpation has sensitivity 94% and specificity 72% for atrial fibrillation, so a regular pulse largely excludes it (negative LR 0.1) while an irregular pulse must be confirmed by ECG (Cooke et al, Br J Gen Pract 2006 meta-analysis).","patient":"Your pulse has no steady rhythm, which usually means the top chambers of your heart are quivering rather than beating. That can let small clots form, and blood thinners can lower the chance of a stroke.","document":"Pulse irregularly irregular, rate 96 radial, apex 108 (pulse deficit 12), variable volume; ECG confirms AF.","pair":{"with":"befast","why":"A new neurological deficit is the stroke; the irregular pulse found alongside it is the mechanism and the decision about anticoagulation."},"history":"Ask about palpitations in the preceding days and whether this is a known rhythm, because new-onset fibrillation under 48 hours changes the rate-versus-rhythm decision."},"bp-arms":{"vignette":"A 61-year-old hypertensive man has sudden severe chest pain radiating to the back that began at maximal intensity. His left radial pulse is weak and the right arm systolic is 160 while the left is 118. That inter-arm difference of over 20 mmHg with his story is enough to send him for CT aortography and to hold the heparin that the chest pain protocol would otherwise have given.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on a pulse deficit to exclude dissection; it is present in a minority of dissections, so a normal examination excludes nothing. It is also commonly over-called, since inter-arm differences of 10 to 20 mmHg occur in many healthy people and in atherosclerotic subclavian disease.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a dissection produce a pulse deficit, and why can the deficit come and go?","a":"The dissection flap can extend into or compress the origin of a branch artery such as the subclavian, so the false lumen steals or blocks flow to that limb. Because the flap moves with each beat and the true and false lumens can re-communicate, the obstruction and the pulse deficit may fluctuate over minutes."},"analogy":"A dissection is like a loose flap of wallpaper peeling down a corridor and flopping across doorways: whichever door it covers loses its traffic, and it can shift with each gust.","number":"A pulse deficit has a positive LR of 5.7 for acute aortic dissection, but sensitivity is only about 30%, and the absence of all three of tearing pain, mediastinal widening and pulse or pressure difference gives a negative LR of 0.07 (Klompas, JAMA 2002, Does this patient have an acute thoracic aortic dissection?).","patient":"The blood pressure in your two arms is very different, which can mean the main artery from your heart has torn along its wall. That is an emergency, so we are doing a special scan right now.","document":"R arm BP 160/90, L arm 118/70 (inter-arm difference 42 mmHg), L radial pulse weak; ADD-RS 2; CT aortogram requested.","pair":{"with":"carotid-upstroke","why":"Both read the carotid and radial pulses, but asymmetry between sides points to dissection while symmetrical slow rising points to aortic stenosis."},"history":"Ask whether the pain was maximal at onset and tearing or ripping, since abrupt onset is the history item with the greatest weight in the aortic dissection detection risk score."},"ar-murmur":{"vignette":"A 44-year-old man with Marfanoid features has new breathlessness and a wide pulse pressure of 150/50. Sitting him forward with breath held in expiration, you hear a soft high-pitched blowing murmur immediately after S2 down the left sternal edge. An early diastolic murmur in that context is aortic regurgitation until an echocardiogram says otherwise, and with chest pain you add CT to look for a dissected root.","trap":"The commonest error is to listen with the patient supine and relaxed with the bell; the murmur is high-pitched and soft and needs the diaphragm pressed firmly at the left sternal edge, with the patient sitting forward in held expiration. Many clinicians also stop listening at S2 and miss diastole altogether.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the aortic regurgitation murmur decrescendo and loudest immediately after S2?","a":"Regurgitant flow is driven by the pressure gradient between the aorta and the left ventricle, which is greatest at the instant the aortic valve closes and falls throughout diastole as aortic pressure drops and ventricular pressure rises. The murmur therefore starts at maximum with S2 and fades, and in acute severe regurgitation it becomes short because the ventricle fills and equalises rapidly."},"analogy":"It is the hiss from a punctured tyre: loudest the moment you stop pumping and fading as the pressure difference across the hole leaks away.","number":"An early diastolic murmur heard by a cardiologist has a positive LR of 8.8 to 32 for aortic regurgitation, and its absence has a negative LR of 0.1 for moderate or severe regurgitation (Choudhry and Etchells, JAMA 1999, Does this patient have aortic regurgitation?).","patient":"The valve at the exit of your heart is leaking, so some blood falls back into the heart between beats and the heart has to pump it twice. We need a heart scan to see how bad the leak is and whether the main artery is involved.","document":"Early diastolic decrescendo murmur grade 2/4 at LLSE, loudest sitting forward in expiration; BP 150/50, collapsing pulse.","pair":{"with":"mr-murmur","why":"Both are regurgitant murmurs but one is in diastole at the sternal edge and the other in systole at the apex, so timing against the carotid pulse separates them."},"history":"Ask about sudden chest or back pain, because a new regurgitant murmur with pain is a dissected aortic root until proven otherwise."},"mr-murmur":{"vignette":"A 70-year-old man five days after an inferior myocardial infarction becomes acutely breathless. At the apex there is a new harsh pansystolic murmur radiating into the axilla, louder when he grips your hands. A new mitral regurgitation murmur after infarction means papillary muscle rupture until an echocardiogram shows otherwise, and you call cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery at once.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to grade severity by loudness: acute severe regurgitation into a small, non-compliant atrium can produce a soft, short murmur, and ischaemic regurgitation varies beat to beat. Timing, radiation and the clinical context matter more than the grade.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the mitral regurgitation murmur fill all of systole and radiate to the axilla?","a":"Left ventricular pressure exceeds left atrial pressure from the moment the mitral valve closes until it opens again, so regurgitant flow and its murmur run from S1 to beyond S2 without pause. The jet is directed posteriorly and laterally into the atrium, and sound travels along that jet to the axilla and back."},"analogy":"It is the steady roar of a hose on full for the whole of systole, pointing out of the side of the heart towards the armpit.","number":"A systolic murmur that gets louder with sustained handgrip has sensitivity 68% and specificity 92% for mitral regurgitation or ventricular septal defect (Lembo et al, N Engl J Med 1988), the manoeuvre that separates it from aortic stenosis and HOCM, which soften.","patient":"The valve between the two chambers on the left side of your heart has started leaking, which is why fluid is backing into your lungs. We need a heart scan today to see whether the valve needs repairing.","document":"New grade 3/6 pansystolic murmur at apex radiating to axilla, louder with handgrip, S1 soft; post-MI, urgent echo.","pair":{"with":"ar-murmur","why":"Both are regurgitant murmurs but one is in diastole at the sternal edge and the other in systole at the apex, so timing against the carotid pulse separates them."},"history":"Ask about recent myocardial infarction, fever or a previous valve lesion, which decide whether a new murmur is a surgical emergency, endocarditis or a known chronic leak."},"oedema":{"vignette":"A 79-year-old woman is referred breathless with ankle swelling. Her oedema is pitting to the mid-shin on both sides, but her JVP is not raised, there are no crackles and she started amlodipine a month ago. The symmetrical oedema without venous congestion is a drug effect, not heart failure, so you stop the amlodipine rather than start a diuretic that would drop her pressure.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to equate bilateral ankle oedema with heart failure; it is more often venous insufficiency, calcium channel blockers, immobility or low albumin, and heart failure can exist without it. Oedema only means heart failure when it comes with a raised venous pressure.","teachback":{"q":"Which Starling forces produce pitting oedema, and which one is raised in heart failure versus nephrotic syndrome?","a":"Fluid leaves capillaries when hydrostatic pressure exceeds the oncotic pull of plasma proteins, and it accumulates when lymphatic drainage cannot keep pace. Heart failure raises venous hydrostatic pressure, whereas nephrotic syndrome and cirrhosis lower plasma oncotic pressure; both pit, and the JVP tells you which."},"analogy":"Pitting oedema is a waterlogged field: the cause may be a blocked drain raising the water table or soil that has lost the salt that held water in the channels.","number":"Peripheral oedema has a positive LR of 2.3 (sensitivity 50%, specificity 78%) for heart failure in dyspnoeic emergency patients, far weaker than the JVP or S3 (Wang et al, JAMA 2005).","patient":"Your ankles are swollen because fluid is collecting under the skin, and in your case the likely cause is the new blood pressure tablet rather than your heart. We will swap the tablet and expect the swelling to settle over a few weeks.","document":"Bilateral pitting oedema to mid-shin, symmetrical, non-tender, no erythema; JVP not raised; on amlodipine.","pair":{"with":"wells-dvt","why":"Bilateral symmetrical oedema is systemic, whereas unilateral swelling of more than 3 cm calf difference is a DVT question."},"history":"Ask when any calcium channel blocker, NSAID or glitazone was started, because a drug started in the past 2 months explains a large share of new bilateral oedema."},"pedal-pulses":{"vignette":"A 66-year-old smoker with diabetes has a sluggish ulcer on the lateral foot. Neither the dorsalis pedis nor posterior tibial pulse is palpable on that side, the foot is cool and there is a bruit over the femoral artery. The absent pulses and bruit mean this is an ischaemic ulcer, so you arrange ankle-brachial index and vascular referral rather than another course of dressings.","trap":"The commonest error is to be reassured by a palpable dorsalis pedis pulse alone, which is congenitally absent in about 1 in 10 people and can be felt despite proximal disease, or to palpate with cold hands and read your own pulse. Check both pedal pulses, compare temperature, and confirm with the ankle-brachial index.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a femoral bruit raise the probability of peripheral arterial disease, and why is a bruit absent in both normal and critically stenosed arteries?","a":"Turbulent flow through a narrowed segment generates audible vibrations in the artery wall. A normal smooth artery has laminar flow and no sound, and an artery that is nearly occluded carries too little flow to create turbulence, so the bruit is a sign of moderate stenosis rather than a measure of severity."},"analogy":"A bruit is the whistle of wind through a partly open window: wide open or firmly shut it is silent.","number":"Any palpable pulse abnormality has a positive LR of 4.7 and any limb bruit 5.6 in symptomatic patients, while a normal pulse examination gives a negative LR of 0.38 (Khan et al, JAMA 2006, Does the clinical examination predict lower extremity peripheral arterial disease?).","patient":"I cannot feel the pulses in your foot and the blood vessel at the top of your leg is making a whooshing noise, which means the artery to your foot is narrowed. Poor blood supply is why this ulcer will not heal, and the vascular team can look at improving the flow.","document":"R DP and PT pulses absent, L present; R foot cool with 2-second-delayed refill; R femoral bruit; ABI requested.","pair":{"with":"abi","why":"Pulses and bruits screen, but the ankle-brachial index of 0.9 or less is what confirms and grades the disease."},"history":"Ask about pain in the calf on walking a fixed distance relieved by rest, and about rest pain at night relieved by hanging the foot down, which grade the urgency."},"tamponade-signs":{"vignette":"A 48-year-old man stabbed in the left parasternal chest arrives agitated with a pressure of 82/60, distended neck veins and heart sounds you can barely hear over the noise of the resuscitation bay. The triad of hypotension, raised JVP and muffled sounds after a penetrating chest wound is tamponade, so you call for immediate bedside ultrasound and prepare for thoracotomy or pericardiocentesis without waiting for a chest X-ray.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to wait for Beck's triad in medical tamponade, where it is present in a minority; hypotension is a late sign, and most patients have a normal or even raised pressure with tachycardia, dyspnoea and pulsus paradoxus. In the hypovolaemic trauma patient the neck veins may also be flat, so neither feature excludes tamponade.","teachback":{"q":"Why does cardiac output fall in tamponade even though the heart muscle is normal?","a":"Fluid in the stiff pericardial sac raises intrapericardial pressure until it equals or exceeds diastolic filling pressure, so the chambers cannot fill despite a normal myocardium and venous pressure rises as blood backs up. Stroke volume falls with each beat, and compensatory tachycardia and vasoconstriction hold the blood pressure until the sac is tense, at which point the pressure collapses abruptly."},"analogy":"It is a pump sitting inside a water-filled plastic bag pulled tight: the pump still works but cannot draw anything in because the bag squeezes it shut.","number":"Hypotension is present in only 26% of patients with tamponade, whereas tachycardia is present in 77%, raised JVP in 76% and pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg has a positive LR of 3.3 (Roy et al, JAMA 2007).","patient":"Blood has collected in the sac around your heart and is squeezing it so it cannot fill properly. We are going to drain it straight away, which should bring your blood pressure back up.","document":"BP 82/60, JVP to angle of jaw, heart sounds muffled, penetrating L parasternal wound; eFAST pericardial fluid; tamponade.","pair":{"with":"pulsus-paradoxus","why":"Beck's triad is the late, dramatic picture while a measured paradox detects tamponade in a stable-looking patient with an effusion."},"history":"Ask about recent cardiac surgery, catheter procedures, anticoagulation, malignancy or a penetrating wound, which set the pretest probability that turns these signs into a decision."},"valsalva-murmur":{"vignette":"A 23-year-old footballer collapsed during a match and has a systolic murmur at the left sternal edge. When he bears down the murmur gets louder, and when he squats it almost disappears. A murmur that intensifies with Valsalva in a young person with exertional syncope is hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy until an echocardiogram says otherwise, so he does not go back to training and gets an urgent cardiology referral.","trap":"The commonest error is to treat any systolic murmur in a young athlete as a flow murmur and to skip dynamic manoeuvres; aortic stenosis and innocent murmurs get quieter with Valsalva, so a murmur that gets louder is the single most useful finding. Make sure the patient actually strains for 10 seconds and listen during the strain, not after release.","teachback":{"q":"Why does Valsalva make the HOCM murmur louder when it makes almost every other murmur quieter?","a":"Straining raises intrathoracic pressure and cuts venous return, so the left ventricle becomes smaller. In HOCM a smaller cavity brings the hypertrophied septum and the mitral leaflet closer together, worsening the dynamic outflow obstruction and the murmur, whereas fixed obstructions and regurgitant lesions simply have less blood to make noise with."},"analogy":"The HOCM outflow tract is a tent whose walls flap inward when the air is let out; the less you fill the ventricle, the more the walls touch.","number":"A systolic murmur that increases with Valsalva has sensitivity 65% and specificity 96% for hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, and an increase on standing from squatting has sensitivity 95% and specificity 84% (Lembo et al, N Engl J Med 1988).","patient":"Your murmur gets louder when you strain, which points to a thickened heart muscle that narrows the exit of the heart during exercise. That can be dangerous during sport, so please do not train until the heart specialist has scanned you.","document":"Grade 3/6 ESM at LLSE, louder with Valsalva and on standing from squat, softer on squatting; brisk carotid upstroke; HOCM suspected.","pair":{"with":"carotid-upstroke","why":"Both cause a harsh systolic murmur with exertional syncope, but the HOCM murmur rises with Valsalva and comes with a brisk pulse, while aortic stenosis softens and comes with a slow pulse."},"history":"Ask about sudden death or cardiomyopathy in relatives under 50, because a family history converts a murmur into a genetic emergency."},"plr":{"vignette":"A 67-year-old woman with septic shock on noradrenaline has already had three litres of crystalloid and a borderline lactate. Instead of another bolus you raise her legs to 45 degrees from a semi-recumbent position and watch the cardiac output monitor: it rises by only 4% within a minute. That tells you more fluid will not help and may harm, so you optimise vasopressors and look for the source instead.","trap":"The commonest error is to judge the response by blood pressure or heart rate alone or to lift the legs from a flat position; the validated test starts from a 45-degree semi-recumbent position, lowers the trunk while raising the legs, and needs a real-time cardiac output measure read within 60 to 90 seconds. Pain, cough or a tight cuff cause a sympathetic surge that fakes a response.","teachback":{"q":"Explain why passive leg raising predicts fluid responsiveness without giving any fluid.","a":"Tilting the legs up and the trunk down shifts roughly 300 mL of venous blood from the legs and splanchnic bed into the chest, a reversible internal fluid bolus. If the heart is on the steep part of the Frank-Starling curve the stroke volume rises by 10% or more; if it is on the flat part it does not, and the legs can simply be lowered with no fluid left behind."},"analogy":"It is a test drive with a loan of the patient's own blood: you borrow 300 mL from the legs, see what the heart does with it, and hand it back.","number":"A passive leg raise-induced rise in cardiac output of 10% or more predicts fluid responsiveness with pooled sensitivity 85% and specificity 91% (Monnet et al, Intensive Care Med 2016 meta-analysis).","patient":"By lifting your legs we moved some of your own blood towards your heart to see whether it would pump more. It did not change much, which tells us that more fluid through the drip would not help and we should support your circulation a different way.","document":"PLR from 45-degree semi-recumbent: cardiac output rise 4% at 60 s (non-responder); further fluid withheld, vasopressor titrated.","pair":{"with":"jvp","why":"The JVP is a static pressure that correlates poorly with responsiveness, whereas the leg raise is a dynamic preload challenge that answers the actual question."},"history":"Ask how much fluid has already been given and whether there is known heart failure, since the question is only worth asking once the obvious deficit has been replaced."},"wells-dvt":{"vignette":"A 52-year-old woman has a painful swollen left calf three weeks after a knee arthroscopy. Her calf is 4 cm larger than the right and she has recent surgery, giving a Wells score of 3. With a high score you do not bother with D-dimer and go straight to compression ultrasound, anticoagulating if the scan is delayed.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to use the score to rule out DVT on its own; a low score needs a negative D-dimer before DVT is excluded. The other error is forgetting to subtract 2 when another diagnosis is at least as likely, which is a judgement item that must be considered actively.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the Wells score combined with D-dimer rather than used alone, and what happens to the D-dimer's value as pretest probability rises?","a":"The score sorts patients into probability bands, and D-dimer is a sensitive but non-specific test whose negative result can safely exclude disease only when the prior probability is low. In a high-probability patient a negative D-dimer still leaves a post-test probability too high to stop, so imaging is required regardless of the result."},"analogy":"The score sets how suspicious you are before opening the box; the D-dimer is a smoke sniff that is only reassuring when you were not expecting a fire.","number":"In the original validation, DVT prevalence was 3% with a low score, 17% moderate and 75% high (Wells et al, Lancet 1997), and in the 2003 study a DVT-unlikely score with negative D-dimer had a 3-month venous thromboembolism rate of 0.4% (Wells et al, N Engl J Med 2003).","patient":"Your swollen calf, together with the recent operation, makes a blood clot in the leg vein quite likely, so we will scan the leg today. If the scan is delayed we will give you a blood thinning injection to be safe.","document":"Wells DVT score 3 (recent surgery, calf swelling 4 cm greater than contralateral, localised tenderness); DVT likely; CUS requested.","pair":{"with":"oedema","why":"Unilateral swelling with a calf difference over 3 cm is a thrombosis question, whereas symmetrical bilateral oedema is a systemic one."},"history":"Ask about active cancer, recent immobilisation or surgery within 12 weeks, and previous DVT, since these history items carry most of the weight in the score."},"egophony":{"vignette":"A 58-year-old man with three days of fever and cough has reduced breath sounds at the right base. When you ask him to say ee while listening there, the sound comes through as a nasal ay, a bleat. That E-to-A change over the base marks consolidation, so you start antibiotics for pneumonia on the spot rather than wait for the radiograph to come back.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to test it only where the chest already sounds abnormal and to compare with nothing; egophony is heard by comparing the same spot on both sides and the zone above. It also appears at the top of a pleural effusion where lung is compressed, so it does not by itself distinguish consolidation from effusion.","teachback":{"q":"Why does consolidated lung turn ee into ay?","a":"Air-filled lung acts as a low-pass filter that absorbs the higher frequencies of the voice, so spoken sounds arrive muffled and indistinct. Consolidated lung transmits those higher frequencies, and because the vowel ee relies on a high-frequency formant that the chest wall transmits selectively, the nasal ay quality emerges."},"analogy":"Normal lung is a thick duvet over a radio that muffles the treble; consolidation pulls the duvet off and the voice turns sharp and bleating.","number":"Egophony has a positive LR of 2.0 to 8.6 for pneumonia across studies, one of the few chest findings with a useful LR, while no single finding rules pneumonia out (Metlay et al, JAMA 1997, Does this patient have community-acquired pneumonia?).","patient":"When you spoke, your voice came through the stethoscope differently over the lower right lung, which tells me that part of the lung is filled with infection rather than air. That fits pneumonia, so we are starting antibiotics now.","document":"Egophony and bronchial breathing R base with dullness; L clear; consistent with R basal consolidation.","pair":{"with":"percussion-dull","why":"Both are dull to percussion, but consolidation transmits voice and breath sounds louder while an effusion silences them below its upper border."},"history":"Ask about the duration of fever and the colour of sputum, because a sign of consolidation in a patient with days of fever is pneumonia while the same sign without fever may be infarction or tumour."},"bronchial-breathing":{"vignette":"A 34-year-old woman with pleuritic chest pain and fever has a harsh, hollow breath sound at the left mid-zone with a gap between inspiration and expiration, the same quality you hear over her trachea. Bronchial breathing in the periphery means consolidation, and with her fever and raised heart rate you treat as pneumonia and admit rather than discharge as a viral illness.","trap":"The commonest error is to call loud vesicular breathing bronchial; true bronchial breathing has an expiratory phase as long and as loud as inspiration, a blowing quality and a pause between the phases. Listen over the trachea first to remind yourself what you are looking for.","teachback":{"q":"Why does consolidated lung transmit the sound of the large airways to the chest wall?","a":"Breath sounds are generated by turbulent flow in the large airways, and healthy alveolar tissue filters out the high frequencies before they reach the periphery, leaving the soft vesicular rustle. Fluid-filled alveoli with patent airways conduct sound like a solid, so the raw tracheal sound arrives unfiltered at the stethoscope."},"analogy":"It is like hearing a conversation through a brick wall rather than a padded one: the solid wall carries the voice clearly.","number":"Bronchial breath sounds have a positive LR of about 3.3 (sensitivity 14%, specificity 96%) for pneumonia (Metlay et al, JAMA 1997; McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis).","patient":"Over part of your left lung I can hear the breath sounds from your windpipe coming straight through, which only happens when the air sacs are full of fluid or infection. That means pneumonia and we are starting treatment.","document":"Bronchial breath sounds L mid-zone with increased vocal resonance and dullness; consistent with consolidation.","pair":{"with":"egophony","why":"They appear together over consolidation and are read with the same comparison across sides, but egophony tests voice transmission while bronchial breathing tests breath sound transmission."},"history":"Ask about recent aspiration risk, stroke, alcohol or vomiting, since consolidation in a dependent zone after such an event points to aspiration and changes the antibiotic."},"percussion-dull":{"vignette":"A 61-year-old woman with lung cancer is breathless and the right base is stony dull to percussion, with absent breath sounds and no vocal resonance. The combination of dullness and silence rather than dullness and bronchial breathing tells you this is a large pleural effusion, so you arrange ultrasound-guided drainage rather than antibiotics.","trap":"The commonest error is to percuss too gently, to percuss over the scapula, or to percuss in one spot only; dullness is detected by comparing the same level on the two sides with firm, consistent strikes. The other trap is to attribute dullness to consolidation without checking whether breath sounds and voice are increased or abolished.","teachback":{"q":"Why is a pleural effusion stony dull whereas consolidation is merely dull?","a":"Percussion sets up vibrations in the chest wall and the underlying tissue, and the note depends on how much air is present to resonate. Consolidated lung still contains some air in the airways and surrounding alveoli, giving a dull note, whereas pleural fluid between the wall and lung contains none, so the note is flat and the feel under the finger is solid."},"analogy":"Tapping a wall tells you where the studs are; a stony dull chest is tapping over a water tank built into the wall.","number":"Dullness to conventional percussion has a positive LR of 8.7 for pleural effusion, and asymmetric chest expansion 8.1 (Wong, Lee and Straus, JAMA 2009, Does this patient have a pleural effusion?).","patient":"Tapping on your chest sounds solid at the bottom of the right lung, which tells me fluid has collected around the lung and is stopping it expanding. Draining that fluid should make your breathing much easier.","document":"Stony dullness R base to T8 posteriorly, breath sounds absent, vocal resonance reduced, trachea central; large R effusion.","pair":{"with":"egophony","why":"Dullness is shared by effusion and consolidation; what separates them is whether voice and breath sounds are increased, as in consolidation, or abolished, as in effusion."},"history":"Ask about known cancer, heart failure or recent pneumonia, because the cause of an effusion decides whether you drain it, treat it medically or send fluid for cytology."},"wheeze":{"vignette":"A 28-year-old woman with known asthma arrives breathless, speaking in phrases, with widespread polyphonic expiratory wheeze. After two nebulisers her chest goes quiet and her peak flow is 30% of predicted. The silence is not improvement: it is a chest too tight to wheeze, so you call for senior help, start intravenous magnesium and prepare for intensive care.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to grade severity by how loud the wheeze is; the loudness depends on flow, so a silent chest with exhaustion is the most dangerous finding. Severity is read from the peak flow, speech, respiratory rate, pulse, oxygen saturation and the patient's work of breathing, not from the stethoscope.","teachback":{"q":"What generates a wheeze, and why does it disappear when obstruction becomes critical?","a":"Wheeze is produced when airflow through a narrowed airway makes the airway walls flutter, like a reed, and it requires enough flow to drive the vibration. When airways are nearly closed, or the patient is too tired to generate flow, there is not enough movement of air to make the walls oscillate, so the wheeze stops."},"analogy":"A wheeze is a whistle blown through a narrowed tube; stop blowing hard enough and the whistle goes silent, though the tube is just as narrow.","number":"Unforced wheezing on examination has a positive LR of 2.7 for obstructive airways disease, and in the CARE-COAD2 study wheezing gave an LR of 4.0 and forced expiratory time over 9 seconds an LR of 6.7, with all of history, wheeze and forced expiratory time present giving an LR of 33 (Straus et al, JAMA 2000 and J Gen Intern Med 2002).","patient":"The whistling noise in your chest is air squeezing through narrowed tubes. Your chest has gone quiet, but that means too little air is getting through rather than that things have improved, so we are stepping up treatment.","document":"Widespread polyphonic expiratory wheeze on arrival; after 2 nebulisers chest quiet with poor air entry, PEF 30% predicted; life-threatening asthma.","pair":{"with":"stridor-sign","why":"Wheeze is expiratory and comes from the lower airways, while stridor is inspiratory and comes from the larynx or trachea; both are noisy breathing but they lead to different airways and different emergencies."},"history":"Ask about previous intensive care admissions or intubation for asthma, which are the strongest predictors of a fatal attack."},"crackles":{"vignette":"An 80-year-old man presents breathless with fine late-inspiratory crackles at both bases. He also has a raised JVP and a third heart sound, so the crackles are read as pulmonary oedema and he gets intravenous furosemide; had the crackles been coarse, unilateral and accompanied by fever, the same stethoscope finding would have bought antibiotics instead.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat bibasal crackles as heart failure on their own; they occur in fibrosis, pneumonia, atelectasis and in healthy elderly people who have not taken a deep breath. Ask the patient to cough and breathe deeply first, since crackles that clear with a few breaths are atelectatic and meaningless.","teachback":{"q":"What produces a crackle, and why are fibrosis and oedema crackles late in inspiration while bronchiectasis crackles are early?","a":"A crackle is the sudden explosive opening of a small airway that collapsed on the previous breath, releasing a short pressure wave. Peripheral airways in stiff or oedematous lung open only at the end of inspiration when pressures are high, giving fine late crackles, whereas larger proximal airways full of secretions open early and pop coarsely."},"analogy":"Fine crackles are the sound of Velcro peeling apart as the last few collapsed air sacs are pulled open at the top of a breath.","number":"Crackles have a positive LR of 2.8 (sensitivity 60%, specificity 78%) for heart failure in dyspnoeic emergency patients, far weaker than S3 or JVP (Wang et al, JAMA 2005).","patient":"The crackling I can hear at the bottom of both lungs is fluid in the air sacs, and together with the full vein in your neck it tells me your heart is letting fluid back up into your lungs. A water tablet through the drip should clear it.","document":"Fine late-inspiratory crackles both bases to mid-zone, not clearing with cough; with raised JVP and S3, consistent with pulmonary oedema.","pair":{"with":"s3","why":"Crackles are common and non-specific, whereas the S3 is rare and specific; the two together make heart failure far more likely than either alone."},"history":"Ask about fever and sputum, because crackles with fever are pneumonia until proven otherwise and crackles with orthopnoea are heart failure."},"stridor-sign":{"vignette":"A 2-year-old with a barking cough has a harsh inspiratory noise audible from the doorway while sitting quietly on her mother's lap, with suprasternal recession. Stridor at rest means moderate croup, so she gets oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline and is observed, rather than being discharged as a mild cough.","trap":"The commonest error is to confuse stridor with wheeze or to count stridor that only appears when the child is crying as significant; stridor at rest is the marker of severity. The other trap is to examine the throat of a drooling, toxic, tripod-positioned child, which can precipitate complete obstruction in epiglottitis.","teachback":{"q":"Why is stridor inspiratory in laryngeal obstruction but expiratory or biphasic in tracheal obstruction?","a":"Extrathoracic airways narrow during inspiration because the negative pressure inside them pulls the walls inward, so a lesion at the larynx or upper trachea makes noise as the patient breathes in. Intrathoracic airways are compressed in expiration by positive pleural pressure, so a lower tracheal or bronchial lesion makes noise on breathing out, and a fixed lesion makes noise in both phases."},"analogy":"Sucking through a soft straw collapses it, while blowing inflates it; the upper airway is that straw, which is why the noise comes on the suck.","number":"In the Westley croup score stridor at rest scores 2 and places the child in moderate croup (score 3 to 5), where a single dose of dexamethasone reduces return visits and admissions (Westley et al 1978; Cochrane review, Gates et al 2018).","patient":"The harsh noise when your daughter breathes in is caused by swelling in her voice box from the virus. The steroid medicine shrinks that swelling within a few hours and we will keep her here until the noise settles.","document":"Inspiratory stridor at rest with suprasternal and intercostal recession, no cyanosis, alert; Westley score 4 (moderate croup); dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg given.","pair":{"with":"wheeze","why":"Stridor is inspiratory and from the larynx or trachea, wheeze is expiratory and from the bronchi; the phase of the noise tells you which airway and which emergency."},"history":"Ask about drooling, inability to swallow and a sudden onset without a cough prodrome, which point to epiglottitis or a foreign body rather than croup."},"peak-flow":{"vignette":"A 35-year-old man with asthma arrives breathless but able to speak in sentences. His peak flow is 210, which is 40% of his best of 520. That number alone makes this acute severe asthma, so he receives back-to-back nebulisers and oral steroids and is not discharged until his peak flow is above 75% of best.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to compare the reading with a predicted table when the patient knows their personal best, or to accept a single half-hearted blow; take the best of three short sharp blasts. The other error is to omit the measurement in a patient who looks well, because the number is what separates moderate from severe.","teachback":{"q":"What does peak expiratory flow measure, and why does it track asthma severity but not restrictive lung disease?","a":"Peak flow is the maximum flow in the first fraction of a forced expiration, which depends on the calibre of the large airways and the effort applied. Bronchoconstriction narrows those airways and lowers peak flow in proportion, whereas restrictive disease shrinks lung volume without narrowing airways, so peak flow can be near normal."},"analogy":"Peak flow is the width of the fire hose nozzle: it tells you how fast water can leave, not how much is in the tank.","number":"Peak flow 33 to 50% of best or predicted defines acute severe asthma, below 33% is life-threatening, and 50 to 75% is moderate (BTS/SIGN British guideline on the management of asthma).","patient":"Your blow is less than half of your usual, which tells me this attack is severe even though you can still talk. We will give you strong treatment now and keep you until the number comes back up.","document":"PEF 210 L/min, 40% of personal best 520 (acute severe); post-nebuliser PEF 330 (63%).","pair":{"with":"wheeze","why":"Wheeze tells you the airways are narrowed, while peak flow tells you by how much, and the number is what triggers escalation."},"history":"Ask for the patient's personal best peak flow and the number of salbutamol puffs used in the past 24 hours, since the percentage of best is the severity grade."},"wells-pe":{"vignette":"A 29-year-old woman has pleuritic chest pain for two days after a cold. Her pulse is 84, saturation 98%, she has no leg swelling, haemoptysis, oestrogen use, previous clot, surgery or cancer, and your gestalt says PE is unlikely. She meets all eight PERC criteria, so you send no D-dimer and no CT and treat her pleurisy.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to apply PERC to patients who are not low pretest probability, or to treat it as a score: any single positive criterion means the rule cannot be used. It was derived to avoid testing in those under about 15% probability and performs badly if applied to everyone.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a negative PERC safely avoid D-dimer testing when D-dimer is itself a rule-out test?","a":"In a low-probability patient a D-dimer is positive in about a third of cases for reasons unrelated to embolism, leading to CT scans whose radiation and false positives cause harm that outweighs the rare missed embolism. PERC identifies patients whose probability is already below the threshold at which testing improves outcomes, so the safest action is to stop."},"analogy":"PERC is the sign at the trailhead that says the mountain is closed: it stops you starting a climb whose risks are greater than what you would find at the top.","number":"A negative PERC rule in a low pretest probability patient has a pooled sensitivity of 97% and specificity 22%, with a miss rate below 2% (Singh et al, Ann Emerg Med 2012 meta-analysis; Kline et al, J Thromb Haemost 2008).","patient":"Based on your age, pulse, oxygen level and the absence of any risk factors, a clot in the lung is very unlikely, so unlikely that doing more tests would be more likely to harm than help. We will treat the inflammation of the lung lining instead.","document":"Low gestalt pretest probability for PE; PERC negative (age under 50, HR 84, SpO2 98%, no haemoptysis, oestrogen, prior VTE, surgery or unilateral leg swelling); no further PE testing.","pair":{"with":"wells-dvt","why":"Both are structured pretest probability tools, but the Wells DVT score ranks patients for a test while PERC decides whether to test at all."},"history":"Ask about oestrogen use including the combined pill, which is the PERC item most often forgotten in young women."},"curb65":{"vignette":"An 82-year-old woman with pneumonia on the X-ray is alert and oriented, with a urea of 9, respiratory rate 32 and BP 100/60. She scores 3 on CURB-65. That score tells you her 30-day mortality is around 15 to 20%, so she is admitted for intravenous antibiotics and a senior review rather than discharged on oral treatment.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to discharge a patient scoring 0 or 1 without looking at oxygen saturation, comorbidity or social circumstances; the score predicts death, not safe discharge, and hypoxaemia is not one of its items. It also underestimates risk in young patients, in whom a score of 1 can still mean severe disease.","teachback":{"q":"Why were these five items chosen, and what does each one tell you about the physiology of severe pneumonia?","a":"Confusion marks reduced cerebral perfusion or sepsis, raised urea marks hypovolaemia and renal hypoperfusion, respiratory rate 30 or more marks the ventilatory load of consolidated lung and acidosis, low blood pressure marks failed compensation, and age over 65 marks reduced reserve. Each item was retained because it independently predicted 30-day death in the derivation cohorts."},"analogy":"CURB-65 is five dials on an aircraft panel: each one on its own is just an instrument, but three in the red means you land now.","number":"30-day mortality in community-acquired pneumonia rises from 0.7% with a CURB-65 score of 0 to 3.2% with 1, 13% with 2, 17% with 3, 41.5% with 4 and 57% with 5 (Lim et al, Thorax 2003).","patient":"Your fast breathing, low blood pressure and kidney test results together tell me this pneumonia is serious, more so than it might feel. You need to stay in hospital for antibiotics through a drip and close monitoring.","document":"CURB-65 = 3 (RR 32, SBP 100, age 82; alert, urea 9 mmol/L); severe CAP, IV antibiotics, admit with senior review.","pair":{"with":"qsofa","why":"Both are three- to five-item mortality scores sharing confusion, respiratory rate and blood pressure, but CURB-65 is specific to pneumonia and adds urea and age."},"history":"Ask relatives whether the confusion is new, because new confusion is both a CURB-65 point and the item most often misattributed to dementia."},"rlq-tender":{"vignette":"A 21-year-old man with 18 hours of central abdominal pain that has moved to the right iliac fossa is maximally tender over McBurney's point with guarding. Migration plus focal tenderness is a high-probability picture, so in a young man you book him for surgery without a CT, after a short ultrasound to exclude an obvious alternative.","trap":"The commonest error is to expect tenderness exactly at McBurney's point; the appendix lies there in only a minority and retrocaecal or pelvic appendices give tenderness higher, lower or on rectal examination. Right lower quadrant pain is the finding that matters, wherever within the quadrant it is maximal.","teachback":{"q":"Why does appendicitis pain start centrally and only later localise to the right lower quadrant?","a":"Early inflammation distends the appendix and stimulates visceral afferents that enter the cord at T10, which the brain maps to the periumbilical region. As the inflamed serosa touches the parietal peritoneum, somatic afferents from that spot fire, and the pain moves to the exact location of the appendix, where tenderness is also found."},"analogy":"The first pain is a smoke alarm in the hallway that tells you something is burning somewhere; the localised tenderness is finding the hot door.","number":"Right lower quadrant pain has a positive LR of about 8 and a negative LR of 0 to 0.28 for appendicitis, the most powerful single finding (Wagner et al, JAMA 1996, Does this patient have appendicitis?).","patient":"The pain has settled over one spot low on the right side where the appendix sits, and that spot is very tender. That pattern is typical of appendicitis, and the surgeons will see you now.","document":"Maximal tenderness RIF over McBurney's point with voluntary guarding, Rovsing positive, no rebound; Alvarado 8.","pair":{"with":"cva","why":"Both are right-sided tenderness, but the appendix is tender anteriorly and the kidney posteriorly at the costovertebral angle, and the history of urinary symptoms separates them."},"history":"Ask whether the pain moved from the middle of the abdomen to the right side, since migration has a positive LR of about 3 and is the history item that most supports the sign."},"rebound":{"vignette":"A 45-year-old woman with lower abdominal pain winces when you slowly press and then quickly lift your hand from the left iliac fossa. Rebound tenderness tells you the peritoneum is inflamed, and together with fever it shifts your plan from analgesia and review to blood tests, CT and a surgical opinion for diverticulitis.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to elicit rebound in a frightened patient and read a flinch as peritonitis; it is painful, poorly reproducible and adds little over gentle percussion or asking the patient to cough. Use percussion tenderness or the cough test first and reserve rebound for when the answer is still unclear.","teachback":{"q":"What does rebound tenderness test, and why is it less specific than guarding?","a":"Sudden release of pressure lets the stretched abdominal wall snap back and drag the inflamed parietal peritoneum, which is richly innervated, producing sharp pain. Anxious patients react to the sudden movement regardless of inflammation, and the manoeuvre tests the whole wall rather than one spot, so it is less specific than involuntary guarding over the inflamed area."},"analogy":"It is like releasing a stretched rubber band against sunburnt skin: the snap hurts because the surface is already inflamed.","number":"Rebound tenderness has a positive LR of 1.1 to 6.3 and a negative LR of 0 to 0.86 for appendicitis across studies, so it is inconsistent and should not be relied on alone (Wagner et al, JAMA 1996).","patient":"The sharp pain when I let go of your tummy tells me the lining inside is inflamed. That means we need a scan and a surgeon to look at you rather than sending you home with painkillers.","document":"Rebound and percussion tenderness LIF with involuntary guarding; no generalised rigidity; localised peritonism.","pair":{"with":"cough-test","why":"Both test peritoneal irritation, but the cough test is kinder and comparably accurate, so rebound adds little once the cough test is positive."},"history":"Ask whether movement, coughing or the car journey over bumps made the pain worse, which is the patient's own description of peritoneal irritation."},"cough-test":{"vignette":"A 9-year-old boy with abdominal pain is hard to examine because he tenses at every touch. You ask him to cough, and he grabs his right side and winces. Pain on coughing points to peritoneal irritation without you touching him, and with his history of anorexia and migration it decides that the surgeons see him tonight.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to accept a vague grimace as positive; the cough test is positive when the patient localises sharp pain to a specific spot or clutches the abdomen, and it is unhelpful when the only pain is a sore throat or chest. Also, a negative test in a patient on strong opioid analgesia means little.","teachback":{"q":"Why does coughing hurt in peritonitis, and what does that mechanism share with rebound tenderness?","a":"A cough sharply raises intra-abdominal pressure and moves the viscera against the parietal peritoneum, which is supplied by somatic nerves and becomes exquisitely sensitive when inflamed. Rebound tenderness produces the same friction between viscera and parietal peritoneum by a different route, so both signs are tests of parietal peritoneal inflammation."},"analogy":"Coughing with peritonitis is like jumping up and down with a sprained ankle: the jolt finds the inflamed surface without anyone needing to touch it.","number":"A positive cough test had sensitivity 78% and specificity 79% for peritonitis in 150 emergency patients with abdominal pain (Bennett et al, BMJ 1994), and sensitivity 82% with specificity 52% for appendicitis in right lower quadrant pain (Golledge et al, Ann R Coll Surg Engl 1996).","patient":"When you coughed it hurt in one spot low on the right, which tells me the inside lining of your tummy is sore there. The children's surgeons are going to come and check you.","document":"Cough test positive, localised to RIF; percussion tenderness RIF; no rigidity.","pair":{"with":"rebound","why":"Both detect parietal peritoneal inflammation, but the cough test needs no painful manoeuvre and is the better first test in children and frightened patients."},"history":"Ask whether the journey in was painful over bumps, since the speed-bump sign is the same mechanism self-reported."},"rovsing":{"vignette":"A 30-year-old woman has right iliac fossa pain but is only mildly tender there and keeps saying it is probably a muscle. Pressing deep in the left iliac fossa reproduces pain on the right. A positive Rovsing's sign means the right-sided pain comes from an inflamed intraperitoneal structure rather than the wall, so you keep her for bloods and imaging rather than discharging her with reassurance.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to over-weight a positive sign in isolation; it is only moderately specific and is positive in any right-sided peritoneal irritation, and many clinicians elicit it incorrectly by releasing rather than pressing. Press steadily on the left and ask where it hurts.","teachback":{"q":"What is the proposed mechanism of Rovsing's sign, and what does a positive result actually tell you?","a":"Pressure in the left iliac fossa is thought to shift the colonic contents and the peritoneum towards the right, or to stretch the peritoneum, stimulating somatic pain fibres over an inflamed appendix. Whatever the precise mechanism, the result means the right-sided pain is intraperitoneal and inflamed, not abdominal wall pain."},"analogy":"Pressing one end of a waterbed makes the other end rise; Rovsing's sign presses the left side of the abdomen to nudge the inflamed right side.","number":"Rovsing's sign has a positive LR of about 2.5 for appendicitis in the pooled studies summarised in McGee's Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis, with sensitivity ranging widely between studies.","patient":"When I pressed on the left side of your tummy it hurt on the right, which tells me the pain is coming from something inflamed inside, not from a pulled muscle. We need blood tests and a scan before you go anywhere.","document":"Rovsing's sign positive (LIF pressure reproduces RIF pain); RIF tenderness; Carnett's negative.","pair":{"with":"psoas","why":"Rovsing's sign tests for an inflamed anterior appendix, while the psoas sign is positive when a retrocaecal appendix lies against the iliopsoas and the anterior signs are quiet."},"history":"Ask about the day of the menstrual cycle and the last period, because mid-cycle pain and ectopic pregnancy both give right iliac fossa signs that mimic appendicitis."},"psoas":{"vignette":"A 25-year-old man has a two-day history of vague right-sided abdominal and back pain with minimal anterior tenderness. He lies with the right hip flexed, and extending it on his left side reproduces the pain. A positive psoas sign in a patient with few anterior signs points to a retrocaecal appendix, so you order a CT and surgical review rather than attributing the pain to the back.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to expect the psoas sign in typical appendicitis; it is insensitive and is positive mainly when the appendix lies retrocaecal against the muscle. Its absence excludes nothing, but its presence in an atypical story is what redirects the diagnosis.","teachback":{"q":"Why does hip extension cause pain with a retrocaecal appendix but not with a pelvic one?","a":"The iliopsoas runs retroperitoneally from the lumbar spine to the femur immediately behind the caecum, so a retrocaecal appendix lies on it; stretching the muscle by extension, or contracting it against resistance, moves it against the inflamed tissue. A pelvic appendix lies against the obturator internus instead, so that one hurts with internal rotation of the flexed hip, the obturator sign."},"analogy":"The psoas is a rope running past the appendix; pulling the rope taut rubs whatever is lying on it.","number":"The psoas sign has a positive LR of 2.38 with sensitivity 16% and specificity 95% for appendicitis (Wagner et al, JAMA 1996).","patient":"Stretching the muscle at the back of your hip set off your pain, which suggests the appendix is lying against that muscle. That explains why your tummy itself is not very sore, and we need a scan to confirm it.","document":"Psoas sign positive on R (pain on passive hip extension in left lateral position); minimal anterior RIF tenderness; obturator negative.","pair":{"with":"rovsing","why":"Rovsing's sign is for the anterior appendix with peritoneal signs, while the psoas sign catches the retrocaecal appendix that hides behind the caecum."},"history":"Ask whether the pain is worse walking or straightening up and better lying curled, which is the history that a retrocaecal appendix produces."},"murphy":{"vignette":"A 52-year-old woman with 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal catches her breath and stops inspiring as your hand presses under the right costal margin, but not on the left. That inspiratory arrest with fever and a raised white count makes acute cholecystitis the working diagnosis and sends her for ultrasound and intravenous antibiotics rather than home with antacids.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to call any right upper quadrant tenderness a positive Murphy's sign; the sign is the arrest of inspiration as the descending gallbladder meets your fingers, with the same manoeuvre on the left negative. It is also unreliable in the elderly, in whom cholecystitis is often painless, and it is abolished by analgesia.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the patient stop breathing in when you press beneath the right costal margin?","a":"During inspiration the diaphragm pushes the liver and the attached gallbladder downward until the inflamed fundus meets the examining fingers. Contact with the inflamed parietal peritoneum over the gallbladder produces sudden pain, and the patient reflexively halts inspiration to avoid driving the organ further onto your hand."},"analogy":"It is like lowering a bruised foot onto a step: the moment the sore spot touches, the leg freezes.","number":"Murphy's sign has a positive LR of 2.8 with sensitivity 65% and specificity 87% for acute cholecystitis, and sensitivity falls to about 48% in the elderly (Trowbridge et al, JAMA 2003, Does this patient have acute cholecystitis?).","patient":"When you breathed in against my hand your gallbladder pressed on my fingers and the pain stopped you mid-breath, which is typical of an inflamed gallbladder. An ultrasound will confirm it and antibiotics will start now.","document":"Murphy's sign positive (inspiratory arrest R subcostal, L negative), RUQ tenderness, no jaundice, temp 38.1.","pair":{"with":"rlq-tender","why":"Both are localised right-sided peritoneal tenderness; the costal margin and the relation to inspiration separate the gallbladder from the appendix."},"history":"Ask whether the pain has lasted over 6 hours and whether previous episodes settled within an hour or two, since prolonged pain separates cholecystitis from biliary colic."},"shifting-dullness":{"vignette":"A 55-year-old man with alcohol excess has a distended abdomen and fever. Percussing from the umbilicus to the flank, dullness begins 8 cm from the midline, and when he rolls onto that side for a minute the dull area becomes resonant. Shifting dullness confirms ascites, so you tap the fluid to exclude spontaneous bacterial peritonitis before the antibiotics start.","trap":"The commonest error is to percuss once in the flank and call dullness ascites; flank dullness alone occurs in obese and constipated patients. The sign is the change in the dull-resonant boundary after the patient has lain on one side for 30 to 60 seconds, and it needs at least about 1.5 litres of fluid to be detectable.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the dull area move when the patient rolls over, and why does gas-filled bowel float?","a":"Free fluid obeys gravity and collects in the dependent flank, while bowel loops containing gas are lighter and float up to the uppermost surface, which is why the centre of the abdomen is resonant. Turning the patient moves the fluid to the new dependent side and the floating bowel to the top, so the resonant-dull boundary shifts with it."},"analogy":"The abdomen is a half-full bottle with a cork floating inside: tilt the bottle and the water level and the cork both move.","number":"Shifting dullness has a positive LR of 2.7 and a negative LR of 0.3, sensitivity 77% and specificity 72%, for ascites, while the fluid wave has a positive LR of 6.0 but misses more cases (Williams and Simel, JAMA 1992, Does this patient have ascites?).","patient":"When you rolled over, the dull sound in your tummy moved with you, which tells me there is free fluid inside. We will take a small sample with a needle to check it is not infected.","document":"Abdomen distended, flank dullness bilaterally, shifting dullness positive, fluid thrill present; diagnostic paracentesis performed.","pair":{"with":"spleen-palp","why":"Both are read in the alcoholic patient with portal hypertension; one is percussed and moves, the other is palpated and does not."},"history":"Ask about alcohol intake, known liver disease and recent weight gain, since the cause of ascites decides whether the tap is diagnostic for infection or malignancy."},"spleen-palp":{"vignette":"A 19-year-old student with sore throat and fever for a week has a tender, firm edge palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin that moves down on inspiration and that you cannot get above. A palpable spleen with a positive monospot means infectious mononucleosis with splenomegaly, so your decision is that she avoids contact sport for at least a month.","trap":"The commonest error is to start palpating near the costal margin and miss a grossly enlarged spleen whose edge is already in the pelvis, or to mistake a palpable left kidney for spleen. Begin in the right iliac fossa and move diagonally towards the left costal margin, and remember you cannot get above a spleen but you can above a kidney.","teachback":{"q":"Why is a normal spleen impalpable, and how much must it enlarge before you can feel it?","a":"The normal spleen lies under the left ninth to eleventh ribs with its long axis along the tenth, entirely protected by the rib cage and the diaphragm. It must usually enlarge to about two or three times normal size before its tip descends below the costal margin on inspiration, so a palpable spleen is always abnormal."},"analogy":"The spleen is a boat tucked under a pier; you only see its bow when it has grown long enough to stick out.","number":"A palpable spleen has a positive LR of 8.5 for splenomegaly, with sensitivity 18 to 78% and specificity 89 to 99%, and a non-palpable spleen a negative LR of 0.5, so palpation rules in but does not rule out (McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis; Grover et al, JAMA 1993).","patient":"Your spleen, an organ under the left ribs, is enlarged because of the glandular fever. It can tear if it is knocked, so no rugby, football or any contact sport for at least four weeks.","document":"Spleen palpable 3 cm below L costal margin, firm, mildly tender, moves with respiration, notch not felt, cannot get above; no hepatomegaly.","pair":{"with":"shifting-dullness","why":"Both are read in portal hypertension; the spleen is a palpable solid that stays put while ascites is percussed fluid that moves."},"history":"Ask about contact sport, fever, weight loss and night sweats, which set the difference between a viral spleen that needs rest and a lymphoma that needs a biopsy."},"aaa-palp":{"vignette":"A 72-year-old male smoker presents with back pain and a brief faint. Deep palpation above the umbilicus finds a mass that pushes your fingers apart with each beat, about 6 cm across. An expansile pulsatile mass with pain and syncope is a leaking aneurysm, so you alert the vascular surgeons and transfer him straight for CT or theatre rather than treating the back.","trap":"The commonest error is to be reassured by a non-palpable aorta; palpation misses most aneurysms under 4 cm and many larger ones in obese patients, so a normal examination excludes nothing. The other error is to call a transmitted pulsation an aneurysm, when expansile means the fingers are pushed apart, not just lifted.","teachback":{"q":"What is the difference between transmitted and expansile pulsation, and why does girth matter?","a":"A normal aorta or a mass lying on it lifts both fingers up and down together with each beat, whereas an aneurysm pushes two fingers placed on either side apart as it widens laterally. The sign depends on feeling the lateral walls through the abdominal wall, so in a patient with a girth over 100 cm the aorta is often too deep to assess at all."},"analogy":"Transmitted pulsation is a hand on a drum skin being bounced by the beat; expansile pulsation is a balloon being inflated between your fingers.","number":"Abdominal palpation has pooled sensitivity 29% for aneurysms of 3.0 to 3.9 cm, 50% for 4.0 to 4.9 cm and 76% for 5.0 cm or more, with specificity 75%, and sensitivity falls further when girth exceeds 100 cm (Lederle and Simel, JAMA 1999, Does this patient have abdominal aortic aneurysm?).","patient":"The main blood vessel in your abdomen has swollen like a balloon, and your pain and faint suggest it may be leaking. This needs the vascular surgeons straight away.","document":"Expansile pulsatile mass supra-umbilical, approx 6 cm wide, tender; femoral pulses present; suspected symptomatic AAA, vascular team called.","pair":{"with":"cva","why":"Both present as back or flank pain in older adults, but the aneurysm is a midline pulsating mass and the kidney is tender posteriorly without pulsation."},"history":"Ask about smoking and a first-degree relative with an aneurysm, which double the odds that a pulsation you are unsure about is real."},"bowel-sounds":{"vignette":"A 68-year-old woman with previous abdominal surgery has colicky pain, vomiting and distension. Listening for a full minute you hear high-pitched tinkling runs that coincide with her cramps. Those sounds with her story point to small bowel obstruction, so you pass a nasogastric tube and request CT rather than treating gastroenteritis.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to listen for 10 seconds, report bowel sounds as present and move on; a minute is the minimum and the interobserver agreement is poor even then. Absent sounds mean nothing unless you have listened for several minutes, and the decision about obstruction rests on the history, distension and imaging.","teachback":{"q":"Why are bowel sounds high-pitched and tinkling in mechanical obstruction and absent in ileus?","a":"In obstruction the proximal bowel contracts vigorously against a blockage, pushing fluid and gas through narrowed, distended loops, which produces high-pitched rushes that coincide with colic. In ileus the smooth muscle is quiescent, so no movement of contents occurs and the abdomen is silent."},"analogy":"Obstruction sounds like water forced through a kinked hose with a loud hiss, whereas ileus is a hose that has been turned off at the tap.","number":"Auscultation of bowel sounds for obstruction has a median sensitivity of 42% and specificity of 78%, with interobserver agreement kappa 0.29, across 100 recorded abdominal sounds judged by 177 clinicians (Breum et al, World J Gastroenterol 2015).","patient":"The gurgling sounds in your tummy are high-pitched and come with the cramps, which happens when the bowel is pushing against a blockage. We will put a tube into your stomach to relieve the vomiting and do a scan to find the block.","document":"Abdomen distended, tympanitic, high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds coinciding with colic over 60 s; no peritonism; previous laparotomy scar.","pair":{"with":"hernia-cough","why":"When obstruction is suspected the groin must be examined, since an irreducible hernia is a common and surgically urgent cause that bowel sounds never reveal."},"history":"Ask about previous abdominal surgery and whether flatus has stopped, since adhesions are the commonest cause and absolute constipation marks complete obstruction."},"dre":{"vignette":"A 57-year-old man presents with sudden low back pain, bilateral leg numbness and difficulty passing urine. Rectal examination shows reduced anal tone and a numb perianal region, and a bladder scan shows 600 mL retained. Those findings make cauda equina syndrome the diagnosis and buy an emergency MRI tonight rather than a GP review in the morning.","trap":"The commonest misuse is the rectal examination in suspected appendicitis, where it adds nothing and causes distress; the examination has real value in GI bleeding, faecal impaction, prostate assessment and cauda equina, and should be targeted to those questions. Reduced anal tone is also subjective, so document saddle sensation and a post-void residual alongside it.","teachback":{"q":"Which questions does the digital rectal examination answer well, and why does it fail for appendicitis?","a":"It detects blood and melaena in the rectum, assesses prostate size and texture, finds impacted stool and tests sacral nerve function through anal tone and perianal sensation, all of which are direct examinations of structures within reach. The appendix is rarely within reach of the finger, and rectal tenderness is non-specific pelvic peritoneal irritation, so the test cannot separate appendicitis from its mimics."},"analogy":"The rectal examination is a hand through a letterbox: it tells you a great deal about what is right behind the door and nothing about the rooms upstairs.","number":"Digital rectal examination has pooled sensitivity 49%, specificity 61%, positive LR 1.24 and negative LR 0.85 for acute appendicitis, so it should not be performed for that question (Takada et al, PLoS One 2015 meta-analysis of 19 studies).","patient":"The examination of your back passage shows that the nerves to your bladder and bottom are being squeezed, which is why you cannot pass water properly. This needs an urgent scan tonight, because the nerves can be freed if we act quickly.","document":"DRE: reduced anal tone, reduced perianal sensation bilaterally, no blood; post-void residual 600 mL; suspected cauda equina, emergency MRI.","pair":{"with":"cauda","why":"The rectal examination is one component of the cauda equina triad, and tone alone is unreliable without saddle sensation and a residual volume."},"history":"Ask whether the patient can feel the paper when wiping and whether urinary flow has changed, since the history of saddle numbness and retention is what the rectal examination is confirming."},"hernia-cough":{"vignette":"A 79-year-old woman presents with vomiting and a small tender lump below and lateral to the pubic tubercle in the right groin. It has no cough impulse and will not reduce. An irreducible, tender femoral hernia with vomiting is strangulation until proven otherwise, and she goes to theatre that evening rather than to a surgical clinic.","trap":"The commonest error is to examine only lying down and only when the lump is visible; examine standing with a cough, and never attempt forceful reduction of a tender irreducible hernia, which can push dead bowel back into the abdomen. The other trap is to miss a femoral hernia in an elderly woman because it is small and lies below the inguinal ligament.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a reducible hernia expand on coughing, and what does the loss of the cough impulse mean?","a":"Coughing raises intra-abdominal pressure, which pushes abdominal contents through the defect into the sac, producing a visible and palpable impulse. When the neck of the sac has trapped the contents and oedema has swollen them, the sac is already full and fixed, so no further impulse is transmitted; that loss, with tenderness, means incarceration and possible strangulation."},"analogy":"A reducible hernia is a bubble that pops in and out of a hole in a tyre with each squeeze; once the bubble twists and swells it jams in the hole and no squeeze can move it.","number":"Clinical examination for inguinal hernia has sensitivity 75% and specificity 96% against surgical findings (van den Berg et al, Invest Radiol 1999), and femoral hernias present as emergencies in roughly 40% of cases, far more than inguinal ones (NICE guidance and large registry data).","patient":"The lump in your groin is a piece of bowel that has slipped through a weak spot and become stuck, and the vomiting means it is blocked. It needs an operation tonight to free it before the blood supply is damaged.","document":"R groin lump 2 cm below and lateral to pubic tubercle, tender, irreducible, no cough impulse, overlying skin erythematous; vomiting; suspected strangulated femoral hernia.","pair":{"with":"bowel-sounds","why":"The hernia is a cause of obstruction that the abdominal examination misses unless the groins are checked, so the two are read together in any vomiting distended patient."},"history":"Ask how long the lump has been irreducible and when the pain changed from aching to constant, since a hernia stuck for hours with new constant pain is strangulating."},"cva":{"vignette":"A 30-year-old woman with dysuria for two days now has fever, rigors and vomiting. A firm thump with the ulnar border of your fist over the left costovertebral angle makes her cry out, while the right side is painless. That tenderness with fever changes the diagnosis from cystitis to pyelonephritis, and she gets intravenous antibiotics and blood cultures instead of three days of oral treatment.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to thump hard enough to hurt a healthy back and then read the grimace as positive; press first with the flat of the hand, then percuss once with moderate force, and compare the two sides. Costovertebral tenderness also occurs with renal stones, musculoskeletal pain and retrocaecal appendicitis.","teachback":{"q":"Why does an infected kidney hurt with percussion over the costovertebral angle?","a":"The kidney lies retroperitoneally just below the twelfth rib, enclosed in a tight capsule that is stretched by inflammatory oedema. A jolt transmitted through the overlying muscle moves the swollen kidney within its capsule and against the inflamed perinephric tissue, firing somatic pain fibres in the capsule."},"analogy":"The inflamed kidney is an over-ripe fruit packed tight in its skin; a tap that a firm fruit ignores makes the swollen one burst with pain.","number":"Costovertebral angle tenderness has a positive LR of 1.7 for urinary tract infection in women with urinary symptoms, and no finding reliably separates upper from lower tract infection at the bedside (Bent et al, JAMA 2002, Does this woman have an acute uncomplicated urinary tract infection?).","patient":"Tapping over your kidney on the left was very painful, and with your fever this means the bladder infection has travelled up to the kidney. That needs stronger antibiotics, given through a drip to begin with.","document":"L CVA tenderness to percussion, R negative; temp 38.9, rigors; no loin mass; suspected acute pyelonephritis.","pair":{"with":"aaa-palp","why":"Flank pain in an older patient has two different emergencies: the tender kidney posteriorly and the pulsating aneurysm anteriorly, and the age and sex set which to check first."},"history":"Ask whether there have been rigors and vomiting, which mark upper tract infection better than the loin examination does."},"asterixis":{"vignette":"A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is brought in drowsy and confused. Asked to hold his arms out with wrists extended, his hands flap irregularly every few seconds. That flap in a cirrhotic patient means hepatic encephalopathy, so you look for the precipitant, give lactulose and check for bleeding, infection and constipation rather than organising a CT for a stroke first.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat asterixis as diagnostic of liver disease; it occurs equally with carbon dioxide retention, uraemia and some drugs, so it tells you there is a metabolic encephalopathy and the context tells you which. It also disappears in deep coma, so its absence in a comatose patient is not reassuring.","teachback":{"q":"Why is asterixis called a negative myoclonus, and what does that imply about where the lesion is?","a":"The flap is not a contraction but a brief lapse of the sustained postural tone that holds the wrist up, after which the hand drops and is caught by a corrective jerk. This loss of tonic output arises from diffuse dysfunction of the brainstem reticular and thalamocortical systems that maintain posture, which is why it is a sign of a global metabolic insult rather than a focal lesion."},"analogy":"It is a tired sentry nodding off at his post: the arm drops when attention lapses and snaps back when he wakes.","number":"Asterixis typically appears at West Haven grade 2 hepatic encephalopathy, is usually present through grade 3 and disappears in grade 4 coma (AASLD/EASL practice guideline on hepatic encephalopathy 2014).","patient":"Your hands are flapping because toxins that the liver normally clears have built up and are affecting the brain. We will clear the bowel with lactulose and treat whatever has set this off.","document":"Asterixis present bilaterally, confused and drowsy, West Haven grade 2; no focal neurology; no fetor; cirrhosis known.","pair":{"with":"gcs","why":"The flap tells you the cause is metabolic while the GCS tells you how deep the encephalopathy is; the sign is lost when GCS falls into coma."},"history":"Ask about constipation, black stools, sedatives, infection symptoms and alcohol, which are the precipitants you must find and reverse."},"alvarado":{"vignette":"A 17-year-old boy with 20 hours of abdominal pain has migration, anorexia, nausea, right lower quadrant tenderness, rebound, a temperature of 37.6 and a white cell count of 14 with neutrophilia, an Alvarado score of 9. With that score you refer him to surgery directly, and in a boy you can reasonably skip the CT.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to send a patient home with a score of 4 and no further thought; a low score makes appendicitis unlikely but does not exclude it, especially in women and children, and the score performs worse in those groups. Use it to sort patients for imaging, observation or surgery, not as a stand-alone decision.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a score built from history, examination and blood count outperform any single sign?","a":"Each item of appendicitis carries only a modest likelihood ratio and some are present in mimics, but their errors are largely independent, so combining them multiplies the evidence. The weighting of tenderness and leukocytosis at two points reflects their greater individual likelihood ratios in the derivation data."},"analogy":"A single sign is one witness; the Alvarado score is a jury of eight whose combined verdict is harder to fool.","number":"An Alvarado score below 5 has sensitivity 99% for excluding appendicitis, and a score of 7 or more has sensitivity 82% and specificity 81% for ruling it in, with poorer calibration in women and children (Ohle et al, BMC Medicine 2011 meta-analysis).","patient":"Putting your symptoms, my examination and your blood test together gives a very high score for appendicitis, so the surgeons will take over from here. They may decide to operate without a scan because the picture is clear.","document":"Alvarado 9 (migration, anorexia, nausea, RIF tenderness 2, rebound, temp 37.6, WCC 14 with left shift 2); surgical referral.","pair":{"with":"rlq-tender","why":"Tenderness is the heaviest single item in the score, and the score is what turns it into a probability."},"history":"Ask about anorexia and migration of pain, the two history items that carry points and are often omitted from the initial clerking."},"cremasteric":{"vignette":"A 14-year-old boy has 3 hours of sudden severe pain in the left testis with vomiting. The left testis is high-riding and swollen, and stroking the inner thigh produces no retraction on that side while the right testis rises normally. An absent cremasteric reflex with that story is torsion, and you phone urology for immediate exploration without waiting for an ultrasound.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to use a present cremasteric reflex to exclude torsion; up to a third of torsions keep the reflex, especially in neonates and in intermittent torsion. The reflex is also normally weak or absent in boys under 2 and in some men, so it only means something when it is clearly present on the other side.","teachback":{"q":"What is the reflex arc of the cremasteric reflex, and why does torsion abolish it?","a":"Stroking the upper inner thigh stimulates the ilioinguinal and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve, which synapse at L1 to L2 and send a motor signal through the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve to the cremaster muscle, lifting the testis. Twisting of the cord stretches and ischaemically injures that nerve and the cremaster fibres running within the cord, so the reflex is lost on that side."},"analogy":"The cremaster is a lift cable running up the cord; twist the cord and the cable jams.","number":"In the TWIST score, an absent cremasteric reflex scores 1 (with testicular swelling 2, hard testis 2, nausea or vomiting 1 and high-riding testis 1); a score of 0 had a negative predictive value of 100% and a score of 5 or more a positive predictive value of 100% for torsion (Barbosa et al, J Urol 2013).","patient":"Your testicle has twisted on its cord and the blood supply is cut off, which is why it hurts so much and sits higher than the other one. It needs an operation in the next hour or two to save it.","document":"L testis high-riding, swollen, hard, exquisitely tender; L cremasteric reflex absent, R present; TWIST 7; urology called for exploration.","pair":{"with":"rlq-tender","why":"Torsion can present as lower abdominal pain in a boy who is too embarrassed to mention the testis, so the groin and scrotum must be examined in every young male with right iliac fossa pain."},"history":"Ask exactly when the pain started, because salvage falls sharply after 6 hours and the history sets the urgency more than any sign."},"cmt":{"vignette":"A 22-year-old woman has four days of lower abdominal pain, a new vaginal discharge and a temperature of 38. On bimanual examination, moving the cervix makes her wince and both adnexae are tender. Cervical motion tenderness with fever and discharge meets the criteria for pelvic inflammatory disease, so you treat empirically today rather than waiting for swabs, and you have already excluded ectopic pregnancy with a urine test.","trap":"The commonest error is to treat cervical motion tenderness as specific for pelvic inflammatory disease; it is positive in ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion, appendicitis and endometriosis, and it is the pregnancy test and the ultrasound that separate them. The other error is to withhold treatment because swabs are pending, when the criteria are deliberately set low to protect fertility.","teachback":{"q":"Why does moving the cervix cause pain in pelvic inflammatory disease?","a":"The cervix is attached through the uterus and the broad and uterosacral ligaments to the pelvic peritoneum and the adnexae. Moving it stretches the inflamed tubes, ovaries and the peritoneum covering them, which are supplied by somatic pain fibres, so the pain is the pelvic equivalent of rebound tenderness."},"analogy":"Wiggling the cervix is like tugging a tent pole: every guy rope attached to it pulls on the inflamed ground it is pegged to.","number":"Treatment is recommended when a sexually active woman with pelvic pain has cervical motion, uterine or adnexal tenderness and no other cause; the positive predictive value of that clinical diagnosis against laparoscopy is 65 to 90% depending on the population (CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines 2021).","patient":"The examination shows your womb and tubes are tender and inflamed, most likely from an infection that has travelled up. Starting antibiotics today, before the swab results, is the best way to protect your fertility.","document":"Cervical motion tenderness, bilateral adnexal tenderness, mucopurulent cervical discharge, temp 38.0; urine hCG negative; PID criteria met, empirical treatment started.","pair":{"with":"rlq-tender","why":"Right-sided adnexal tenderness and right iliac fossa tenderness overlap in young women, and the bimanual examination with a pregnancy test is what separates tubal from appendiceal disease."},"history":"Ask the date of the last period and about a new partner or unprotected intercourse, because a missed period converts the same examination into a possible ectopic pregnancy."},"pronator-drift":{"vignette":"A 66-year-old woman reports 40 minutes of clumsiness in her right hand that she thinks has resolved. Power testing is normal, but with eyes closed and arms outstretched the right arm slowly pronates and sinks. That drift is an objective corticospinal sign, so you treat this as a stroke with a persisting deficit, not a resolved TIA, and activate the stroke pathway.","trap":"The commonest error is to test with eyes open, which lets vision correct the drift, or to count a downward drift without pronation as positive; pronation and flexion at the elbow are the corticospinal pattern, while an upward or wandering drift suggests a proprioceptive or cerebellar problem. Hold the posture for a full 20 to 30 seconds.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a mild corticospinal lesion cause the arm to pronate rather than simply fall?","a":"The corticospinal tract preferentially drives the supinators and extensors that hold the arm palm-up against gravity, while the pronators and flexors are relatively stronger and have more subcortical support. When cortical drive weakens, the balance tips towards pronation and flexion before any weakness is measurable on confrontation testing."},"analogy":"The arm is a tug-of-war held level by two teams; weaken the supinator side slightly and the rope turns in the direction of the stronger side long before anyone falls over.","number":"Pronator drift with Barré testing had sensitivity 92% and specificity 90% for mild unilateral cerebral lesions, outperforming formal strength testing (Teitelbaum, Eliasziw and Garner, Can J Neurol Sci 2002).","patient":"When your eyes were closed your right arm drifted and turned over without you noticing, which tells me the signal from the brain to that arm is still weaker than the other side. That means the stroke has not fully resolved and we need to treat it urgently.","document":"Pronator drift R (pronation and downward drift at 15 s, eyes closed), L stable; no drift on confrontation power testing; NIHSS motor arm 1.","pair":{"with":"babinski","why":"Both are corticospinal signs, but drift appears with the subtlest lesions while an extensor plantar needs a larger or more established one."},"history":"Ask exactly when the patient was last known to be well, since a positive drift means the deficit is ongoing and the time of onset decides thrombolysis."},"babinski":{"vignette":"A 45-year-old man has a week of worsening stiffness and tripping in both legs. Stroking the lateral sole produces a slow fanning extension of the great toe on both sides, with brisk knee reflexes and clonus. Bilateral extensor plantars with spasticity put the lesion in the spinal cord, so he gets an urgent MRI of the cord rather than a referral for his feet.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to call a withdrawal response positive; a ticklish patient pulls the whole foot away rapidly with hip and knee flexion, whereas the true sign is a slow, deliberate extension of the great toe usually with fanning of the others. Stroke the lateral border from heel forward with a blunt object and watch the first movement of the great toe.","teachback":{"q":"What is the extensor plantar response a release of, and why is it normal in infants?","a":"The flexion withdrawal reflex of the leg normally includes the great toe extensor, but the mature corticospinal tract suppresses that component so that the toes flex to a noxious stimulus. Before myelination of the corticospinal tracts is complete, in the first year of life, the primitive extension is unsuppressed, and any lesion of the tract later in life releases it again."},"analogy":"The corticospinal tract is a lid on a jack-in-the-box; damage the lid and the primitive toe extension pops out.","number":"The Babinski sign has sensitivity 51% and specificity 99%, positive LR 52, for pyramidal tract dysfunction, so its presence is near-definitive but its absence excludes nothing (Isaza Jaramillo et al, Arq Neuropsiquiatr 2014).","patient":"When I stroked the sole of your foot your big toe went up instead of curling down, which is a sign that the nerve pathways from the brain down the spinal cord are being affected. We need a scan of your spine today to find the cause.","document":"Plantars extensor bilaterally (slow great toe extension with fanning), knee jerks 3+, sustained ankle clonus; bilateral UMN signs in legs.","pair":{"with":"reflexes","why":"An extensor plantar with brisk reflexes is an upper motor neuron picture, while absent reflexes with a flexor plantar points to nerve or root; the pairing localises before any imaging."},"history":"Ask whether bladder control or sensation below a level has changed, which converts a positive plantar into a cord compression emergency."},"romberg":{"vignette":"A 70-year-old man with numb feet is steady standing with his eyes open but sways and has to be caught within seconds of closing them. A positive Romberg's test means he is standing on vision because his proprioception has gone, so you check B12 and consider a cervical cord or peripheral nerve cause rather than labelling him as cerebellar.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to call Romberg's test positive for cerebellar disease; a patient with cerebellar ataxia is unsteady with eyes open and closing them makes little difference. A positive test requires stability with eyes open and loss of balance with eyes closed, and mild sway alone is normal.","teachback":{"q":"Which three systems keep you upright, and what does Romberg's test remove to expose a deficit?","a":"Balance depends on vision, vestibular input and proprioception from the feet and joints, and any two can compensate for loss of the third. Closing the eyes removes vision, so a patient who was compensating for lost proprioception or vestibular function with sight will fall, while a patient with cerebellar disease was already unable to integrate the inputs and is unsteady regardless."},"analogy":"Standing is a three-legged stool of vision, inner ear and joint sense; Romberg's test kicks away the vision leg to see whether the other two can hold the weight.","number":"Romberg's test is observed for 30 seconds with feet together and eyes closed, and a fall or a step to recover is positive; the test is specific for proprioceptive or vestibular loss and is not a cerebellar sign (Lanska and Goetz, Neurology 2000; McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis).","patient":"You are steady when you can see your feet but not when you close your eyes, which tells me the nerves that sense where your feet are have stopped working. Your eyes have been doing their job, so we need to find out why the nerves are affected.","document":"Romberg positive (steady eyes open, fall within 5 s eyes closed); vibration absent at ankles, proprioception impaired at great toes; gait steady with vision.","pair":{"with":"finger-nose","why":"Romberg's test is positive with sensory or vestibular loss but not cerebellar disease, while finger-nose ataxia is cerebellar and unchanged by closing the eyes."},"history":"Ask whether the unsteadiness is far worse in the dark or in the shower with eyes shut, which is the patient's own Romberg test."},"finger-nose":{"vignette":"A 58-year-old man with sudden vertigo and vomiting has nystagmus, and when he reaches for your finger his left hand overshoots and shakes as it arrives, while heel-shin on the left wobbles across the shin. Left-sided limb ataxia with acute vertigo is a cerebellar stroke, not vestibular neuritis, and he goes for urgent imaging and stroke admission.","trap":"The commonest error is to test too close, so that the arm never fully extends and mild dysmetria is hidden; hold your finger at the limit of the patient's reach and move it between trials. The other trap is to attribute ataxia to weakness or to the vertigo itself, when a weak limb is slow but accurate and a vertiginous patient with a normal cerebellum is accurate too.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a cerebellar hemisphere lesion cause ipsilateral limb ataxia?","a":"The cerebellar hemisphere refines the timing and force of movement of the same-side limbs, because its output crosses to the contralateral motor cortex, whose corticospinal fibres cross back again. A lesion removes the predictive braking of each movement, so the limb overshoots, decomposes into segments and oscillates as it approaches the target."},"analogy":"The cerebellum is the brakes on a car; without them you reach the junction but overshoot it and then reverse to correct.","number":"About 10% of isolated cerebellar infarctions present as isolated vertigo mimicking vestibular neuritis, with limb ataxia and other signs absent at presentation (Lee et al, Neurology 2006), which is why a normal finger-nose test does not exclude a cerebellar stroke.","patient":"Your left hand and leg are missing the target and wobbling, which points to the part of the brain that coordinates movement, not to the balance organ in your ear. That is why we are treating this as a stroke and scanning you urgently.","document":"L finger-nose dysmetria with intention tremor, L heel-shin ataxia, dysdiadochokinesia L; R normal; with acute vestibular syndrome, cerebellar stroke suspected.","pair":{"with":"hints","why":"Limb ataxia catches cerebellar hemisphere strokes but a third of posterior circulation strokes have none, so HINTS is the test that separates central from peripheral when the limbs are normal."},"history":"Ask about new headache or neck pain with the vertigo, which raises the probability of vertebral artery dissection and cerebellar infarction."},"hints":{"vignette":"A 52-year-old woman has had continuous vertigo, vomiting and unsteadiness for 6 hours with horizontal nystagmus. When you turn her head rapidly to the right her eyes stay locked on your nose without a corrective saccade, her nystagmus changes direction on lateral gaze and covering each eye in turn reveals a vertical skew. A normal head impulse with direction-changing nystagmus and skew is central, so she is treated as a posterior circulation stroke and an MRI is booked even though her CT is normal.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to apply HINTS to patients with intermittent or positional vertigo, where it is uninterpretable; it belongs only to the acute vestibular syndrome of continuous vertigo with nystagmus. The other error is to be reassured by an abnormal head impulse without checking the other two components, since any one central finding wins.","teachback":{"q":"Why is a normal head impulse test dangerous in acute vertigo?","a":"The head impulse tests the vestibulo-ocular reflex: a damaged labyrinth or vestibular nerve cannot keep the eyes on target during a rapid head turn, so a catch-up saccade appears. In a stroke the labyrinth is intact and the reflex works normally, so a patient with continuous vertigo whose eyes stay on target has a central cause until proven otherwise."},"analogy":"The head impulse checks whether the camera gimbal in the inner ear is working; if the gimbal is fine but the picture is still spinning, the fault is in the computer, not the camera.","number":"HINTS had sensitivity 100% and specificity 96% for stroke in acute vestibular syndrome, better than early MRI, which was falsely negative in 12% within the first 48 hours (Kattah et al, Stroke 2009).","patient":"The three eye tests we did point to the balance centre in the back of the brain rather than the inner ear as the cause of your dizziness. A stroke there can be missed on the first scan, so we are admitting you and doing a more detailed scan.","document":"Acute vestibular syndrome; HINTS: head impulse normal bilaterally, direction-changing horizontal nystagmus, skew deviation present; central pattern, stroke pathway.","pair":{"with":"dix-hallpike","why":"HINTS is for continuous vertigo with spontaneous nystagmus, while Dix-Hallpike is for brief positional vertigo with none at rest; using one in the other's syndrome gives wrong answers."},"history":"Ask whether the vertigo is continuous even lying still or only triggered by moving, which decides whether HINTS or Dix-Hallpike is the right test."},"dix-hallpike":{"vignette":"A 48-year-old woman has 10-second spells of spinning when she rolls over in bed and no symptoms sitting still. Lying her back quickly with the head turned 45 degrees to the right and hanging below the couch, there is a short latency and then upbeating torsional nystagmus with vertigo that fades in 20 seconds. That response diagnoses right posterior canal BPPV, so she gets an Epley manoeuvre and goes home with no scan.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to test a patient who has spontaneous nystagmus at rest, where positional testing cannot be interpreted and HINTS is needed, or to abandon the test early; the nystagmus has a latency of several seconds. Keep the eyes open and the position held for at least 30 seconds, and treat a positive side immediately.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the nystagmus of posterior canal BPPV have latency, a torsional direction and fatigability?","a":"Loose otoconia in the posterior canal must first begin to sink before they drag endolymph and deflect the cupula, producing the latency. The posterior canal is wired to the ipsilateral superior oblique and contralateral inferior rectus, giving the torsional upbeating movement, and once the debris settles at the bottom the stimulus stops, so the nystagmus fades and is smaller on repetition."},"analogy":"It is a snow globe with gravel in the wrong channel: tip it and the gravel takes a moment to start falling, swirls the fluid as it goes and then settles.","number":"The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre has sensitivity 79% and specificity 75% for posterior canal BPPV against a reference of treatment response or repeated testing (Halker et al, J Am Osteopath Assoc 2008 meta-analysis; AAO-HNS BPPV guideline 2017).","patient":"Tiny crystals in the balance canal of your right inner ear have come loose and are swirling the fluid when you move, which is what makes the room spin. We can move them back into place with a series of head positions right now.","document":"Dix-Hallpike R positive (latency 5 s, upbeating torsional nystagmus 20 s, fatigable), L negative; R posterior canal BPPV; Epley performed.","pair":{"with":"hints","why":"Dix-Hallpike is for brief positional vertigo with no nystagmus at rest, while HINTS is for continuous vertigo with spontaneous nystagmus."},"history":"Ask whether each spell lasts seconds and is triggered by lying down or turning over, since that history is what makes the manoeuvre worth doing."},"kernig":{"vignette":"A 19-year-old student with fever and headache has a stiff neck, and when you flex her hip to 90 degrees and try to straighten the knee she resists and the hamstrings spasm. A positive Kernig's sign with fever raises meningitis from possible to probable, so she gets ceftriaxone and dexamethasone before the CT and lumbar puncture.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on a negative Kernig's or Brudzinski's sign to exclude meningitis; both are absent in the great majority of proven cases, and their value lies only in what a positive result adds. Also, a positive sign appears in subarachnoid haemorrhage and in any meningeal irritation, not just infection.","teachback":{"q":"Why does straightening the knee with the hip flexed cause pain and resistance in meningitis?","a":"The manoeuvre stretches the sciatic nerve and its roots, which drags on the inflamed lumbosacral meninges and the nerve root sleeves. The spinal cord and dura resist that traction with reflex hamstring spasm, which is the resistance you feel; Brudzinski's sign is the same traction applied from the top by flexing the neck."},"analogy":"The meninges are a sore sock around the spinal cord; pulling the leg straight tugs the sock and the leg flinches.","number":"Kernig's sign has sensitivity 5% and Brudzinski's sign 5%, nuchal rigidity 30%, each with specificity about 68 to 95%, for meningitis proven by CSF (Thomas et al, Clin Infect Dis 2002).","patient":"Straightening your leg pulled on the inflamed lining of your spine and brain, which is why it hurt so much. That fits meningitis, so we are giving antibiotics now and then a test of the spinal fluid.","document":"Kernig's sign positive bilaterally, Brudzinski's negative, nuchal rigidity present, GCS 15, no rash; empirical antibiotics given 03:20.","pair":{"with":"neck-stiffness","why":"Both are meningeal signs with poor sensitivity; nuchal rigidity is the more sensitive of the two, and neither should be used to withhold treatment."},"history":"Ask about photophobia and the speed of onset of the headache, since a thunderclap headache with meningism is subarachnoid haemorrhage until the CT says otherwise."},"rapd":{"vignette":"A 28-year-old woman has three days of blurred vision and pain on moving the right eye. Acuity is 6/18 right and 6/6 left, and swinging the torch from left to right makes the right pupil dilate as the light arrives. That relative afferent pupillary defect puts the lesion in the optic nerve, not the retina or cornea, and with the pain it is optic neuritis, so she gets an MRI brain rather than an eye clinic appointment in a month.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to look for unequal pupils; in an afferent defect the pupils are equal because both are driven by the better eye. Also, a dense cataract or refractive error never produces a relative afferent defect, so a defect in a patient blamed on cataract means something else is wrong behind it.","teachback":{"q":"Why are the pupils equal in a relative afferent pupillary defect, and why does the affected pupil dilate when the light swings onto it?","a":"The light reflex is consensual: input from each eye is distributed equally to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, so both pupils are always the same size and reflect the stronger signal. When the torch moves from the healthy eye to the damaged one the total afferent signal falls, so both pupils dilate, and the one you are watching appears to dilate in the light."},"analogy":"Two dimmer switches wired to the same pair of lights: the room brightness reflects the stronger switch, and moving the lamp to the faulty switch makes both lights dim.","number":"A relative afferent pupillary defect is detectable clinically when the afferent asymmetry is around 0.3 log units or more on the neutral density filter scale, and it is never caused by media opacity or refractive error (Thompson, Corbett and Cox, Surv Ophthalmol 1981).","patient":"When I moved the light to your right eye the pupil opened up instead of closing, which tells me the nerve carrying signals from that eye is inflamed. That needs a scan of the brain and a specialist today.","document":"RAPD right (dilates on swinging torch), VA R 6/18 L 6/6, pain on eye movement, red desaturation R, fundi normal; suspected optic neuritis.","pair":{"with":"visual-acuity","why":"Reduced acuity that corrects with a pinhole is refractive, whereas reduced acuity with an afferent pupillary defect is optic nerve or retina; the two tests together localise the problem."},"history":"Ask about pain on eye movement and whether colours look washed out in that eye, which point to optic neuritis over other causes of a pupillary defect."},"visual-fields":{"vignette":"A 64-year-old man says he keeps bumping into things on the left. Confrontation testing with a wiggling finger in each quadrant shows he misses it in both left quadrants of both eyes. A left homonymous hemianopia localises the lesion behind the optic chiasm on the right, and with abrupt onset it is a stroke, so he is admitted for imaging and must not drive.","trap":"The commonest error is to test with both of the patient's eyes open, which lets the intact field of one eye cover a monocular defect; cover one eye at a time and test each quadrant. The other error is to assume a normal test excludes a defect, since confrontation misses most partial defects.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a lesion behind the chiasm cause the same-sided defect in both eyes?","a":"At the chiasm the nasal retinal fibres cross, so each optic tract carries the fibres from the temporal retina of the same side and the nasal retina of the other, which together see the opposite half of the visual world. A lesion in one tract, radiation or occipital lobe therefore removes the same half of the visual field from both eyes."},"analogy":"The chiasm is a motorway interchange that sorts traffic by destination rather than by origin: everything bound for the left world ends up on the right carriageway.","number":"Confrontation visual field testing is insensitive: the best single method, kinetic testing with a red target, had sensitivity 74% and specificity 93%, and combining it with static finger wiggle raised sensitivity to 78%, so a normal confrontation test does not exclude a defect (Kerr et al, Neurology 2010).","patient":"You cannot see anything on the left side with either eye, which means the part of the brain that processes that side of vision has been damaged, most likely by a stroke. Please do not drive until this has been fully assessed.","document":"L homonymous hemianopia on confrontation (finger wiggle and red target, each eye separately), macula apparently spared; no neglect; stroke pathway.","pair":{"with":"rapd","why":"A hemianopia respects the vertical midline and is behind the chiasm, while an afferent pupillary defect is in front of it; together they place the lesion."},"history":"Ask whether the problem is in one eye or on one side of the world, since patients with hemianopia usually blame the eye on the side of the lost field."},"facial-palsy":{"vignette":"A 38-year-old man wakes with a drooping right face. He cannot close the right eye or raise the right eyebrow, and taste is altered. Forehead involvement means a lower motor neuron lesion, so he gets prednisolone within 72 hours and eye protection rather than a stroke scan; had he been able to wrinkle his forehead you would have sent him down the stroke pathway.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to diagnose Bell's palsy without checking the ear, the parotid and the other cranial nerves; vesicles in the ear mean Ramsay Hunt, a parotid mass means cancer and other cranial nerve signs mean a brainstem or skull base lesion. Bell's palsy is a diagnosis of exclusion that happens to be the commonest.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the forehead keep working in an upper motor neuron facial weakness?","a":"The part of the facial nucleus supplying the upper face receives corticobulbar input from both hemispheres, whereas the lower face is driven mainly by the contralateral hemisphere. A cortical or capsular lesion leaves the forehead supplied by the intact side, but a lesion of the nucleus or the nerve itself takes out every branch on that side."},"analogy":"The forehead has two power lines and the mouth only one; cut a cable upstream and the forehead stays lit, cut the house's own fuse and everything goes dark.","number":"Untreated Bell's palsy recovers completely in about 70% of patients, and prednisolone started within 72 hours raises complete recovery to around 80 to 83% at a year (Peitersen, Acta Otolaryngol 2002; Sullivan et al, N Engl J Med 2007).","patient":"The whole of the right side of your face is weak including the forehead, which tells me the problem is in the nerve to the face rather than in the brain. Steroids started now give the best chance of full recovery, and you must tape the eye shut at night.","document":"R LMN facial palsy, House-Brackmann grade IV, forehead involved, incomplete eye closure; ears clear, no vesicles, parotid normal, other cranial nerves intact; prednisolone and eye care.","pair":{"with":"befast","why":"A facial droop is an F in BE-FAST, and forehead sparing is what separates the stroke that needs the pathway from the nerve palsy that needs steroids."},"history":"Ask about ear pain, rash, hearing change and tick exposure, which turn a presumed Bell's palsy into zoster, Lyme disease or something else."},"gcs":{"vignette":"A 24-year-old motorcyclist arrives after a crash opening his eyes only to pain, groaning without words and flexing abnormally to pressure, a Glasgow Coma Scale of 2 plus 2 plus 3, which is 7. A score of 8 or below means he cannot protect his airway, so he is intubated before CT rather than scanned first.","trap":"The commonest error is to report a total without the components or to score the best response from a limb affected by injury or pain; record eyes, verbal and motor separately and use the best motor response from any limb. Alcohol, hypoglycaemia and hypoxia also depress the score and must be corrected before the number is read as brain injury.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the motor component carry the most prognostic weight, and what does it tell you about the depth of the lesion?","a":"The motor responses are graded in a sequence that follows deepening brainstem dysfunction: obeying needs cortex, localising needs the cortex to find the stimulus, withdrawal and abnormal flexion show loss of cortical control with midbrain intact, extension shows midbrain damage, and no response shows pontine or lower failure. Because it maps onto anatomy, it predicts outcome better than eyes or voice."},"analogy":"The motor scale is a lift descending floor by floor through the brain; each lower response tells you how far the damage has reached.","number":"A Glasgow Coma Scale of 8 or less defines coma and is the conventional threshold for securing the airway in trauma; the scale was described by Teasdale and Jennett in 1974 and the motor score is its strongest single predictor of outcome (Teasdale et al, Lancet Neurol 2014).","patient":"Your son is deeply unconscious and cannot protect his breathing, so we are putting him on a breathing machine to keep him safe while we scan his head.","document":"GCS 7 (E2 V2 M3) at 14:05, pupils 3 mm equal and reactive, intubated for airway protection; GCS 8 (E2 V2 M4) at 13:45 pre-hospital.","pair":{"with":"ct-head-rule","why":"The scale is the entry point of the rule, which applies to GCS 13 to 15; below that the scan is automatic."},"history":"Ask the paramedics for the first recorded score and whether it has fallen, since a drop of 2 points matters more than any single value."},"clonus":{"vignette":"A 35-year-old woman at 34 weeks of pregnancy has a headache and a blood pressure of 168/108. Sharply dorsiflexing her ankle sets off six rhythmic beats before it stops. Sustained clonus in that setting is a sign of cortical irritability, so she is treated as pre-eclampsia at risk of eclampsia and started on magnesium sulphate rather than observed on the ward.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to call one or two beats abnormal; a few beats of clonus can occur in anxious, cold or athletic patients with brisk reflexes. Clonus that continues for 5 or more beats while you hold the foot is sustained and pathological.","teachback":{"q":"What produces the rhythmic beating of clonus, and why does it signify an upper motor neuron lesion?","a":"A sudden stretch of the calf fires the muscle spindles and a monosynaptic reflex contraction follows; in a limb with heightened reflex gain that contraction itself stretches the antagonist spindles and relaxes the muscle enough to be stretched again, so the loop repeats rhythmically. The gain is normally held down by descending corticospinal and reticulospinal inhibition, so loss of that inhibition is what allows the oscillation."},"analogy":"Clonus is a microphone held too close to its loudspeaker: the feedback howls until something damps the loop.","number":"Sustained clonus is conventionally defined as 5 or more beats, with 1 or 2 beats considered within normal limits in patients with brisk reflexes (McGee, Evidence-Based Physical Diagnosis).","patient":"When I flicked your foot upward it kept beating on its own, which with your blood pressure and headache tells me your nervous system is becoming irritable from the pregnancy condition. We are starting magnesium through the drip to prevent a seizure.","document":"Sustained ankle clonus bilaterally (over 5 beats), reflexes 3+ throughout; BP 168/108, headache; magnesium sulphate commenced.","pair":{"with":"babinski","why":"Both are released upper motor neuron phenomena, but clonus measures reflex gain while the plantar response measures loss of a specific cortical suppression."},"history":"Ask about visual flashes and upper abdominal pain, which with clonus and hypertension complete the picture of severe pre-eclampsia."},"reflexes":{"vignette":"A 40-year-old woman has sciatica down the back of the right leg. Her right ankle jerk is absent while the left is brisk, her knee jerks are equal and plantar responses are flexor. The asymmetric ankle reflex places the root at S1, so her MRI request names the L5-S1 level and she is examined for foot plantarflexion power rather than treated for non-specific back pain.","trap":"The commonest error is to call reflexes absent without reinforcement; a Jendrassik manoeuvre of clenching the teeth or pulling the hands apart brings out many absent reflexes. Also, symmetrically brisk or symmetrically sluggish reflexes are often normal, and it is asymmetry or a mismatch with the plantars that matters.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a disc herniation abolish a reflex while a stroke exaggerates it?","a":"The tendon reflex is a monosynaptic arc through a single spinal segment, so compression of that segment's root interrupts the arc and the reflex is lost. A stroke leaves the arc intact but removes the descending inhibition that normally damps it, so the reflex is exaggerated and the plantar becomes extensor."},"analogy":"A reflex is a doorbell circuit: a cut wire at the root means silence, while a broken volume control upstream means it rings far too loud.","number":"Reflexes are graded 0 to 4+ on the NINDS scale with 2+ as normal, and the Cochrane review found that reflex, strength and sensory deficits used alone perform poorly for disc herniation, so reflex findings localise a root but do not diagnose or exclude a herniation (van der Windt et al, Cochrane 2010).","patient":"The reflex at your right ankle has gone while the left is normal, which fits a nerve root at the bottom of the spine being pinched by a disc. The scan will be aimed at that level.","document":"Reflexes: knees 2+ equal, R ankle 0 (absent with reinforcement), L ankle 2+, plantars flexor; S1 root pattern on R.","pair":{"with":"babinski","why":"The plantar response is read with the reflexes to decide between upper and lower motor neuron patterns before any imaging."},"history":"Ask about diabetes and age, since a symmetrically absent ankle jerk in an older diabetic is neuropathy rather than a root lesion."},"water-swallow":{"vignette":"A 74-year-old man admitted after a stroke is thirsty and wants a cup of tea. Given 3 oz of water to drink without stopping, he coughs halfway through and his voice sounds wet afterwards. The failed swallow test means he is kept nil by mouth with intravenous fluids until speech and language therapy assess him, instead of being allowed to drink and aspirate.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to accept a sip or two without coughing as a pass; the validated test uses 3 oz (90 mL) drunk continuously, and a cough, wet voice or inability to finish are all failures. The other trap is to treat a pass as proof of safety, when silent aspiration without cough is common after stroke.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a wet or gurgly voice after swallowing mean aspiration risk even without coughing?","a":"Material left in the laryngeal vestibule or on the vocal cords after a swallow changes the voice quality, and it is sitting at the entrance to the airway ready to be inhaled with the next breath. Coughing requires an intact laryngeal sensory reflex, which strokes often blunt, so the voice change may be the only sign of penetration."},"analogy":"The wet voice is water sloshing in the gutter above the drain: it has not gone down yet and the next gust will carry it the wrong way.","number":"The 3-oz water swallow test has sensitivity 96.5% and specificity 48.7% for aspiration on instrumental testing in mixed inpatients, so a pass is reassuring and a fail needs formal assessment (Suiter and Leder, Dysphagia 2008).","patient":"You coughed while drinking and your voice went wet afterwards, which means some of the water is going towards your lungs instead of your stomach. Until the swallowing specialist has seen you we will give fluids through the drip to keep you safe.","document":"3-oz water swallow test failed (cough mid-swallow, wet voice after), kept nil by mouth, IV fluids, SLT referral.","pair":{"with":"gag","why":"A present gag reflex says nothing about swallowing safety, whereas a failed water swallow test does, so the gag should not be used to clear a patient to eat."},"history":"Ask whether the patient has coughed on food or drink or had a chest infection in recent months, since aspiration before the stroke changes the risk after it."},"hoover-functional":{"vignette":"A 29-year-old woman has sudden weakness of the left leg with no pain. Asked to push the left heel down into your hand she produces almost nothing, but when she lifts the right leg against resistance your hand under the left heel feels a strong downward push. A positive Hoover's sign tells you the pathways are intact, and she gets a positive diagnosis of functional weakness and an explanation that day rather than a wait for an MRI.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat a positive Hoover's sign as proof the patient is malingering or that nothing else is wrong; functional weakness is a genuine disorder and can coexist with organic disease, and the sign is also positive in some patients with neglect or pain inhibition. Use it as a positive sign to make the diagnosis, not as an accusation.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the weak leg push down involuntarily when the other leg is lifted?","a":"Flexing one hip against resistance automatically recruits extension of the opposite hip to stabilise the pelvis, a synergy that runs below conscious attention. In functional weakness the voluntary command to push is disrupted by attention towards the symptom, but the automatic synergy is not, so the hidden strength appears."},"analogy":"It is like a stuck door that swings freely when you stop looking at it: the hinge was fine, the problem was the way the push was being made.","number":"Hoover's sign had sensitivity 63% and specificity 100% for functional leg weakness in a prospective study of patients with unilateral leg weakness (McWhirter et al, J Psychosom Res 2011).","patient":"When you lifted the good leg, the weak leg pushed down strongly on its own, which proves the nerves and muscles are intact. The problem is in how the brain is sending the message, which is a real and treatable condition called functional weakness.","document":"Hoover's sign positive L (hip extension strong with contralateral hip flexion, weak on direct testing), reflexes symmetrical, plantars flexor; functional leg weakness.","pair":{"with":"pronator-drift","why":"Drift with pronation is an organic corticospinal sign, while a positive Hoover's sign shows intact pathways; the two together separate stroke from functional weakness at the bedside."},"history":"Ask whether the weakness fluctuates, is better when distracted, and whether there were any dissociative symptoms at onset, which support the diagnosis alongside the sign."},"neck-stiffness":{"vignette":"A 44-year-old man with 24 hours of fever and headache cannot bring his chin to his chest, with a sense of resistance rather than pain in the muscles. Nuchal rigidity with fever raises meningitis above a viral illness, so he gets empirical antibiotics and a lumbar puncture rather than paracetamol and discharge.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to accept painful neck movement as rigidity; the sign is resistance to passive flexion specifically, with rotation preserved, whereas cervical spondylosis and muscle spasm restrict all directions. Also, its sensitivity is low, so a supple neck does not exclude meningitis, especially in the elderly, infants and the immunosuppressed.","teachback":{"q":"Why is rotation preserved in meningism when flexion is lost?","a":"Flexion of the neck stretches the inflamed meninges and the nerve roots along the whole length of the spinal cord, which the paraspinal muscles resist by reflex spasm. Rotation moves the vertebrae around the cord without stretching the dura appreciably, so it is painless and free, which separates meningeal irritation from a stiff cervical spine."},"analogy":"The dura is a tight inflamed stocking around the cord: bending forward pulls it taut while turning the head just twists it.","number":"Nuchal rigidity has sensitivity 30% and specificity 68% for meningitis in adults with suspected meningitis, so its absence must not be used to withhold a lumbar puncture (Thomas et al, Clin Infect Dis 2002).","patient":"Your neck will not bend forward because the lining around the brain is inflamed, which with your fever makes meningitis likely. We are starting antibiotics now and then taking a sample of fluid from your back to confirm it.","document":"Nuchal rigidity present (resistance to passive flexion, rotation free), Kernig negative, GCS 15, no rash, no focal signs; LP after CT.","pair":{"with":"kernig","why":"Both are meningeal irritation signs; rigidity is the more sensitive and should be tested first, but neither can rule meningitis out."},"history":"Ask whether the headache is worse on jolting the head or bright light, which supports meningeal irritation when the neck signs are equivocal."},"befast":{"vignette":"A 59-year-old man suddenly becomes unsteady and his wife notices he is slurring his words, though his face and arms seem fine. Because balance and speech are two items of BE-FAST, you treat this as a posterior circulation stroke and activate the pathway, when the older FAST screen would have called him negative.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to wait for all items or to rely on the arm and face only; one new item is enough, and posterior circulation strokes often present with balance or vision loss alone. The other trap is to forget the T: the time last seen well, not the time found, is what decides treatment.","teachback":{"q":"Why were balance and eyes added to FAST, and which vascular territory do they represent?","a":"FAST was built from middle cerebral artery strokes, which cause face, arm and speech deficits, but posterior circulation strokes from the vertebrobasilar system cause vertigo, ataxia, diplopia and visual field loss without those features. Adding balance and eyes captures that territory, which includes the brainstem and cerebellum where missed strokes are most dangerous."},"analogy":"FAST watched only the front door of the brain; BE-FAST puts a camera on the back door as well.","number":"Among confirmed strokes, FAST missed 14% but BE-FAST missed only 4.4%, mainly by capturing posterior circulation strokes (Aroor et al, Stroke 2017).","patient":"Your sudden unsteadiness and slurred speech can be the signs of a stroke in the back part of the brain, even though your face and arm look fine. We are treating it as a stroke straight away because the first few hours matter most.","document":"BE-FAST positive: new gait ataxia and dysarthria, no facial droop or arm drift, visual fields full; last seen well 11:30; stroke call 12:05.","pair":{"with":"hints","why":"BE-FAST screens for stroke in general while HINTS answers the harder question of whether isolated vertigo is a stroke."},"history":"Ask exactly when the patient was last seen normal, since that time, not the time of discovery, opens or closes the thrombolysis and thrombectomy windows."},"epley":{"vignette":"A 55-year-old woman has a positive right Dix-Hallpike. You keep her head turned 45 degrees to the right and extended, rotate it to the left, roll her onto her left shoulder looking at the floor and sit her up, holding each position for 30 seconds until the nystagmus stops. Her vertigo is largely gone on retesting, so she leaves without a scan, with instructions on recurrence and no need for vestibular sedatives.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to hurry through the positions or to give sedatives instead, which do not move the otoconia and prolong recovery; each position must be held until the nystagmus settles. The other error is to perform it when the Dix-Hallpike was negative or when the nystagmus was horizontal, which points to a horizontal canal needing a different manoeuvre.","teachback":{"q":"Describe what happens to the otoconia during the Epley manoeuvre and why each position matters.","a":"The debris sits in the posterior semicircular canal, and each head position rotates the canal so that gravity moves the particles further along it towards the common crus and back into the utricle, where they can no longer deflect a cupula. Holding each position until nystagmus stops ensures the particles have finished moving before the next turn, so none are left behind."},"analogy":"It is rolling a marble through a curved pipe by tilting the pipe one step at a time until it drops into the tank at the end.","number":"Epley manoeuvre versus sham gives complete symptom resolution with an odds ratio of 4.42 and conversion of a positive Dix-Hallpike to negative with an odds ratio of 9.62 (Hilton and Pinder, Cochrane 2014).","patient":"We have moved the loose crystals out of the balance canal and back where they belong, which is why the spinning has eased. Carry on as normal, and if it comes back the same manoeuvre can be repeated.","document":"Epley manoeuvre R performed x1, each position held 30 to 60 s; post-manoeuvre Dix-Hallpike R negative; discharged with advice.","pair":{"with":"dix-hallpike","why":"The Dix-Hallpike finds the canal and the side, and the Epley is its treatment in the same sitting."},"history":"Ask about neck or vascular disease that limits extension, since a modified position or a different manoeuvre may be needed."},"horner":{"vignette":"A 42-year-old man has a sudden left-sided headache and neck pain after a yoga class, and you notice his left pupil is 1 mm smaller than the right, more obvious in the dark, with a slight ptosis. A painful Horner's syndrome is a carotid dissection until proven otherwise, so he goes for CT angiography of the neck that evening rather than home with analgesia.","trap":"The commonest error is to miss the small anisocoria in a bright room; the difference is greatest in the dark and the affected pupil dilates slowly, the dilatation lag. Another trap is to expect anhidrosis, which is absent in post-ganglionic lesions such as carotid dissection.","teachback":{"q":"Trace the oculosympathetic pathway and explain why a carotid dissection produces a Horner's syndrome without anhidrosis.","a":"The pathway runs from the hypothalamus down to the ciliospinal centre at C8 to T2, then over the lung apex to the superior cervical ganglion, and the post-ganglionic fibres to the eye travel up the wall of the internal carotid artery. A dissection of that artery damages the fibres running along it, but the sudomotor fibres to the face have already left with the external carotid, so sweating is spared."},"analogy":"The sympathetic supply to the eye hitchhikes on the outside of the carotid pipe; a tear in the pipe wall tears off the passengers.","number":"Painful Horner's syndrome was present in 44% of 146 patients with internal carotid artery dissection and was the only sign in half of those (Biousse et al, Am J Ophthalmol 1998).","patient":"Your left pupil is smaller and the eyelid droops slightly, and with the pain in your neck this suggests a tear in the lining of the main neck artery. We need a scan of the blood vessels tonight because treatment prevents a stroke.","document":"L Horner's: ptosis 2 mm, miosis greater in dark with dilatation lag, no anhidrosis; L neck pain post-exertion; CTA neck for carotid dissection.","pair":{"with":"rapd","why":"Horner's syndrome is an efferent sympathetic pupil sign with anisocoria, whereas the afferent defect has equal pupils; the first question in any pupil abnormality is which of the two you are looking at."},"history":"Ask about recent neck manipulation, trauma, heavy exertion or a new headache, since any of these with a Horner's syndrome points to dissection."},"ct-head-rule":{"vignette":"A 70-year-old man slipped on ice, hit his head and was briefly confused but is now GCS 15 with no vomiting or amnesia. His age alone meets a high-risk criterion of the Canadian CT Head Rule, so he gets a CT tonight; had he been 40 with the same story you could have discharged him with written advice.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to apply the rule outside its inclusion criteria, such as patients on anticoagulants, with seizures, under 16 or with GCS below 13; these patients were excluded and need imaging on other grounds. It is a rule for minor head injury with loss of consciousness, amnesia or disorientation, not for every bump.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a rule with such low specificity still reduce scanning, and what does its sensitivity target?","a":"Without a rule, clinicians scan most minor head injuries, so a rule that identifies the half of patients who can safely go home still cuts scanning substantially. The high-risk criteria were designed for 100% sensitivity for injuries needing neurosurgery, which is the outcome whose miss is catastrophic, accepting many unnecessary scans to achieve it."},"analogy":"The rule is a coarse sieve: it lets through plenty of sand to be sure not a single stone is missed.","number":"The high-risk criteria of the Canadian CT Head Rule had sensitivity 100% and specificity 68.7% for neurosurgical intervention, and the medium-risk criteria sensitivity 98.4% and specificity 49.6% for clinically important brain injury (Stiell et al, Lancet 2001).","patient":"At your age a knock on the head carries a higher chance of bleeding inside the skull even when you feel well, so we are going to scan you tonight before letting you go.","document":"Minor head injury with brief confusion, GCS 15, no anticoagulant; CCHR high-risk criterion met (age 65 or over); CT head requested.","pair":{"with":"gcs","why":"The rule applies only to GCS 13 to 15 after injury, so the score is checked first and the rule second."},"history":"Ask about anticoagulants and antiplatelets, and about a seizure, which take the patient outside the rule and towards a scan regardless."},"monofilament":{"vignette":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes attends with a blister on the sole he did not notice until it bled. He cannot feel the 10 g monofilament at three of four plantar sites on either foot and has no vibration sense at the great toes. Loss of protective sensation puts him in a high-risk foot category, so the blister is treated as an ulcer with offloading, same-week podiatry and a vascular check rather than a plaster.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to press the filament on callus or an ulcer, or to let the patient see where it is applied; test on intact skin at the plantar hallux and the first, third and fifth metatarsal heads with the eyes closed, and buckle the filament for about a second. A single site missed already counts as loss of protective sensation on most protocols.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a 10 g force define protective sensation, and which fibres is the filament testing?","a":"A 10 g filament buckles at a force that the skin should be able to detect to notice a pebble in a shoe or a tight seam before it causes damage, so inability to feel it means injuries will go unnoticed. It tests large myelinated fibres carrying pressure and touch, the same fibres as vibration, which are lost early in diabetic neuropathy."},"analogy":"The filament is a smoke alarm test button: if the alarm does not sound at 10 g, the house will burn before anyone wakes.","number":"Insensitivity to the 10 g monofilament had sensitivity 91% but specificity 34% for future foot ulceration in diabetes, so it is a screening test whose normal result is reassuring (Pham et al, Diabetes Care 2000; ADA Standards of Care recommend annual testing at four plantar sites).","patient":"You could not feel the fine fibre pressing on your feet, which means the nerves no longer warn you when something is damaging the skin. That is why this blister got so bad without pain, and why we need to check your feet every day and get the foot team involved.","document":"10 g monofilament: unable to detect at 3 of 4 sites bilaterally; vibration 128 Hz absent at great toes; loss of protective sensation, high-risk foot; pedal pulses present.","pair":{"with":"pedal-pulses","why":"The two foot screens answer different questions: the filament says whether the patient will feel the injury and the pulses whether it will heal."},"history":"Ask whether the patient inspects the feet daily and what footwear is worn, since the finding only changes outcomes when it changes behaviour."},"centor":{"vignette":"A 24-year-old woman has two days of sore throat with a temperature of 38.4, tender anterior cervical nodes, tonsillar exudate and no cough, a Centor score of 4. That score puts her probability of streptococcal pharyngitis above 50%, so you test or treat according to local guidance rather than reassuring her it is viral.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to prescribe antibiotics for a score of 2 or to treat the score as a diagnosis; even a score of 4 is wrong nearly half the time and most sore throats are viral and self-limiting. The score is for deciding who merits testing, and it does not detect glandular fever or quinsy.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a cough lower the probability of streptococcal pharyngitis?","a":"Group A streptococcus infects the pharynx and tonsils locally and rarely involves the lower airway, so cough implies a viral infection spreading through the respiratory tract. Each of the four criteria is a feature that separates bacterial tonsillitis from a viral upper respiratory infection, and cough is the negative one."},"analogy":"The Centor score is a four-piece identikit: the more features match the bacterial face, the more likely the suspect is streptococcus.","number":"The probability of a positive throat culture for group A streptococcus is about 2.5% with 0 criteria, 6.5% with 1, 15% with 2, 32% with 3 and 56% with 4 (Centor et al, Med Decis Making 1981; McIsaac modification adds age).","patient":"Your fever, swollen glands, white spots and absence of a cough mean there is a good chance this is a strep throat, roughly even odds, so it is worth testing or treating. Most sore throats are viral and settle on their own, which is why we check first.","document":"Centor 4 (temp 38.4, tender anterior cervical nodes, tonsillar exudate, no cough); no trismus or uvular deviation; rapid strep test sent.","pair":{"with":"otoscopy","why":"Both are upper respiratory infection decisions in the same consultation, and in both the score or sign exists to limit antibiotics to those likely to benefit."},"history":"Ask about drooling, trismus and a muffled voice, which mean peritonsillar abscess and override the score."},"otoscopy":{"vignette":"A 2-year-old with fever and a night of screaming has a right tympanic membrane that bulges outward like a bagel with the landmarks lost and a yellow colour. A bulging drum is acute otitis media, so she gets antibiotics as a child under 2 with a bulging drum and fever, whereas a red but flat drum would have had analgesia and review.","trap":"The commonest error is to diagnose otitis media from redness alone; a crying child has a pink drum, and redness without bulging has a weak likelihood ratio. Position, translucency and mobility are what matter, and wax must be cleared before any verdict.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the tympanic membrane bulge in acute otitis media but retract in eustachian tube dysfunction?","a":"In acute infection pus accumulates under pressure in the middle ear and pushes the drum outward, erasing the handle of the malleus and the light reflex. When the eustachian tube is blocked without infection, air is absorbed from the middle ear and the resulting negative pressure sucks the drum inward, giving a retracted drum with prominent landmarks."},"analogy":"A bulging drum is a balloon being inflated from behind, while a retracted one is a balloon with the air sucked out.","number":"A bulging tympanic membrane has a positive LR of 51 for acute otitis media, cloudiness 34 and distinctly impaired mobility 31, while redness alone adds little (Rothman et al, JAMA 2003, Does this child have acute otitis media?).","patient":"The eardrum on the right is bulging out with infected fluid behind it, which is why she has been in so much pain. Antibiotics will help at her age, and the pain relief tonight is just as important.","document":"R TM bulging, opaque, yellow, landmarks lost, no perforation; L TM normal; acute otitis media R.","pair":{"with":"centor","why":"Both are antibiotic decisions in febrile children, and in both the specific sign, bulging or the four criteria, matters more than redness or fever."},"history":"Ask about discharge from the ear, which means the drum has perforated and changes both the examination and the follow-up."},"red-eye":{"vignette":"A 68-year-old woman has a painful red right eye with vomiting and halos around lights. Her acuity is 6/36, the cornea is hazy and the pupil is mid-dilated and fixed. Reduced acuity, corneal haze and an unreactive pupil are danger signs, and the picture is acute angle-closure glaucoma, so she goes to ophthalmology that hour rather than home with antibiotic drops.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to prescribe chloramphenicol for any red eye without checking acuity; the five danger signs are reduced acuity, photophobia or deep pain, ciliary flush, corneal opacity and an abnormal pupil, and any one needs same-day ophthalmology. Contact lens wearers are the other trap, since their red eye is a corneal ulcer until proven otherwise.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a mid-dilated pupil precipitate angle closure, and why does that explain the evening onset?","a":"In an anatomically narrow anterior chamber the iris at mid-dilatation is bunched against the trabecular meshwork and the lens-iris contact blocks aqueous flow through the pupil, raising pressure behind the iris and closing the angle. Dim light and the evening produce exactly that mid-dilated state, and the acute rise in pressure makes the cornea oedematous and the pupil fixed."},"analogy":"The drainage angle is a sink plughole beside a folding curtain; half-draw the curtain and it bunches over the hole and the sink overflows.","number":"Normal intraocular pressure is 10 to 21 mmHg, and in acute angle closure it is typically above 40 and often 50 to 80 mmHg, with permanent optic nerve damage within hours if untreated (Royal College of Ophthalmologists and AAO Primary Angle-Closure guidance).","patient":"The pressure inside your eye has risen sharply because the drainage inside the eye has closed off, and that is what is causing the pain, the sickness and the blurred vision. It can damage your sight within hours, so the eye team will lower the pressure urgently.","document":"R eye: VA 6/36 (L 6/6), ciliary injection, hazy cornea, pupil 5 mm fixed mid-dilated, globe hard to palpation; suspected acute angle closure, urgent ophthalmology.","pair":{"with":"rapd","why":"Both decide whether a visual complaint is urgent; the red eye danger signs assess the front of the eye and the afferent defect the nerve behind it."},"history":"Ask about contact lens wear and whether the vision is affected, the two history items that most reliably separate the sight-threatening red eye from conjunctivitis."},"battle":{"vignette":"A 30-year-old cyclist is brought in a day after a fall, now with a bruise over the right mastoid and clear fluid dripping from the nose. Battle's sign with rhinorrhoea means a basal skull fracture, so he gets a CT, no nasogastric tube, and a neurosurgical opinion rather than reassurance about a bump on the head.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to expect Battle's sign or raccoon eyes at the time of injury; they take hours to days to appear, so their absence in the first hours excludes nothing. The other trap is to pass a nasogastric or nasopharyngeal tube in a patient with these signs.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a fracture of the skull base cause bruising over the mastoid and around the eyes?","a":"Blood from a fracture of the petrous temporal bone tracks through the mastoid emissary veins to the skin behind the ear, and blood from an anterior fossa fracture tracks forward into the orbital soft tissues contained by the orbital septum. Both take time to migrate to the surface, which is why the signs lag behind the injury."},"analogy":"The bruises are damp patches appearing on a ceiling the day after a pipe bursts in the loft: they mark where the water drained, not where the pipe is.","number":"Any sign of basal skull fracture is a high-risk criterion of the Canadian CT Head Rule, which had sensitivity 100% for injuries needing neurosurgery (Stiell et al, Lancet 2001); the bruising typically appears 1 to 3 days after the injury.","patient":"The bruise behind your ear and the clear fluid from your nose tell me the bottom of the skull has cracked and the fluid around the brain is leaking. We are going to scan it and the brain surgeons will advise, and please do not blow your nose.","document":"R Battle's sign, no periorbital ecchymosis, clear rhinorrhoea positive for glucose, no haemotympanum; CT head and base of skull, neurosurgical referral; no NG tube.","pair":{"with":"ct-head-rule","why":"The sign is one of the rule's high-risk criteria, so finding it ends the question of whether to scan."},"history":"Ask when the injury happened, since the sign appearing a day later is expected and a patient presenting late may have been sent home before it showed."},"trousseau":{"vignette":"A 50-year-old woman one day after a total thyroidectomy complains of tingling around the mouth. Inflating a cuff on her arm above systolic for three minutes produces a carpal spasm with the thumb adducted and the fingers extended. A positive Trousseau's sign after thyroid surgery is hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury, so she gets intravenous calcium while the level is checked rather than waiting for the laboratory.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on Chvostek's sign instead, which is present in about 10% of normal people and absent in a third of hypocalcaemic patients; Trousseau's sign is far more specific. Also, the cuff must stay inflated for a full three minutes and the sign can be masked by sedation.","teachback":{"q":"Why does low ionised calcium make nerves hyperexcitable, and why does ischaemia bring out the spasm?","a":"Extracellular calcium stabilises the voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve membranes, so when it falls the threshold for firing drops and nerves discharge spontaneously. Ischaemia from the cuff further lowers the threshold of the nerves in the forearm, so the already irritable motor nerves fire repetitively and the hand contracts into the characteristic posture."},"analogy":"Calcium is the damping on a guitar string; take it away and the string hums at the slightest touch, and the cuff is the finger that plucks it.","number":"Trousseau's sign has sensitivity 94% and specificity 99% for hypocalcaemia, whereas Chvostek's sign is present in around 10% of normocalcaemic people and absent in 29% of hypocalcaemic patients (figures cited in Cooper and Gittoes, BMJ 2008, Diagnosis and management of hypocalcaemia).","patient":"The cuff on your arm made your hand cramp into a particular shape, which happens when the calcium in the blood is low after thyroid surgery. We are giving calcium now and will keep checking your levels.","document":"Trousseau's sign positive at 2 min (carpal spasm), Chvostek's positive; perioral paraesthesia; post-thyroidectomy day 1; IV calcium given, Ca and PTH sent.","pair":{"with":"chvostek","why":"Both are neuromuscular excitability signs for hypocalcaemia, but Trousseau's is specific while Chvostek's is often a normal variant."},"history":"Ask about recent neck surgery, and about the symptoms of tingling or cramps, which precede the sign and prompt the test."},"fundoscopy":{"vignette":"A 26-year-old woman with obesity has daily headaches worse on lying down and transient blurring when she stands. Through dilated pupils both optic discs are elevated with blurred margins and no spontaneous venous pulsation. Bilateral papilloedema makes this raised intracranial pressure, so she gets urgent imaging and a lumbar puncture for opening pressure rather than a migraine prescription.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to call a blurred disc margin papilloedema in isolation; drusen, hypermetropia and myelinated fibres mimic it, and early papilloedema usually leaves acuity normal. Look for venous engorgement, loss of spontaneous venous pulsation, obscured vessels at the disc margin and peripapillary haemorrhages, and dilate the pupil if you cannot see.","teachback":{"q":"Why does raised intracranial pressure swell the optic disc, and why is acuity preserved early?","a":"The subarachnoid space extends around the optic nerve, so raised pressure compresses the nerve behind the globe and blocks axoplasmic flow in the retinal ganglion cell axons, which swell where they pass through the disc. The macular fibres are affected late, so central acuity survives until the swelling becomes chronic, which is why papilloedema must be found before the patient notices a change in vision."},"analogy":"The optic nerve is a hose with a clamp just behind the tap; the water backs up and the tap swells before the flow at the far end falls.","number":"Spontaneous venous pulsation at the disc indicates an intracranial pressure below about 190 mmH2O, so its presence argues against papilloedema, although it is also absent in around 10% of normal people (Levin, Arch Neurol 1978).","patient":"The nerves at the back of both your eyes are swollen, which means the pressure of the fluid around the brain is too high. We need scans and a test of the fluid pressure today, because untreated it can damage your sight.","document":"Bilateral disc swelling, Frisén grade 2, no spontaneous venous pulsation, peripapillary haemorrhages R; VA 6/6 both, enlarged blind spots; urgent CT venogram and LP with opening pressure.","pair":{"with":"rapd","why":"Papilloedema is bilateral and spares acuity and the pupils early, while optic neuritis is unilateral with reduced acuity and an afferent defect."},"history":"Ask about pulsatile tinnitus, transient visual obscurations and recent weight gain or tetracycline use, which make idiopathic intracranial hypertension the likely cause."},"nikolsky":{"vignette":"A 47-year-old man started allopurinol three weeks ago and now has fever, sore eyes and a painful dusky rash with blisters covering 15% of his skin. Sliding a thumb firmly across apparently normal skin next to a blister peels the epidermis away. A positive Nikolsky sign with mucosal involvement and a new drug makes this Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis, so the drug stops now and he goes to a burns or intensive care unit.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to press on an existing blister and call its extension positive, or to expect the sign in bullous pemphigoid where the split is deeper and the sign is negative. Test on apparently normal skin near a lesion, and remember that a positive sign is a surface marker of a disease that may be rapidly progressing.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the epidermis shear off in pemphigus and toxic epidermal necrolysis but not in bullous pemphigoid?","a":"In pemphigus the autoantibodies destroy desmoglein adhesion between keratinocytes within the epidermis, and in toxic epidermal necrolysis cytotoxic cells kill keratinocytes throughout its full thickness, so the sheet has lost its internal cohesion and slides with lateral pressure. In pemphigoid the split is at the dermal-epidermal junction with intact keratinocyte adhesion above, so the roof is tough and does not shear."},"analogy":"Pemphigus skin is a jigsaw with the tabs cut off: push the picture and the pieces slide apart, whereas pemphigoid is a jigsaw lifted off a wet table in one piece.","number":"Epidermal detachment below 10% of body surface area is Stevens-Johnson syndrome, 10 to 30% is overlap and above 30% is toxic epidermal necrolysis; mortality by SCORTEN rises from 3% with a score of 0 to 1 to 35% with 3 and 58% with 4 (Bastuji-Garin et al, Arch Dermatol 1993 and J Invest Dermatol 2000).","patient":"The top layer of your skin is coming away because of a severe reaction to the new tablet, and this can spread quickly over the body. We have stopped the tablet and you need the specialist burns unit, where the skin is treated much like a burn.","document":"Painful dusky macules with flaccid bullae, approx 15% BSA detachment, Nikolsky positive on uninvolved skin, oral and conjunctival erosions; SJS/TEN overlap, SCORTEN 2; allopurinol stopped.","pair":{"with":"purpura-glass","why":"Both are rashes that mean a systemic emergency; one tests whether the epidermis holds together and the other whether blood has leaked into the dermis."},"history":"Ask for every drug started in the past 8 weeks, especially allopurinol, anticonvulsants, sulphonamides and NSAIDs, since finding and stopping the culprit is the single most effective treatment."},"slr":{"vignette":"A 38-year-old warehouse worker has three weeks of pain from the right buttock to the outer calf. Lifting his straight right leg reproduces that leg pain at 40 degrees and it eases when you bend the knee. A positive straight leg raise confirms the pain is radicular rather than mechanical back pain, so you check his L5 and S1 function, reassure him about the natural history and set a review date rather than requesting an urgent scan.","trap":"The commonest error is to call back pain or hamstring tightness at 70 degrees a positive test; it is positive only when it reproduces the patient's radiating leg pain below the knee, usually between 30 and 70 degrees. Because its specificity is low, a positive test does not prove a disc herniation, but a negative test makes one unlikely.","teachback":{"q":"Why does raising the straight leg reproduce radicular pain at 30 to 70 degrees but not before?","a":"The sciatic nerve and its L5 and S1 roots are slack through the first 30 degrees of hip flexion, and from 30 to 70 degrees they are drawn 2 to 6 mm through the foramina. If a root is tethered over a herniated disc that traction stretches and compresses it, firing the ectopic pain of radiculopathy; beyond 70 degrees the nerve has stopped moving and further pain is hamstring or joint."},"analogy":"The nerve root is a rope running over a rock in a stream: pulling the rope from the far end only hurts where it rubs on the rock.","number":"The straight leg raise has pooled sensitivity 92% and specificity 28% for lumbar disc herniation in surgical populations, so it rules out better than it rules in (van der Windt et al, Cochrane 2010).","patient":"Lifting your straight leg stretched the nerve that runs down the back of your leg and reproduced your pain, which tells me the nerve is being irritated by a bulging disc. Most of these settle over six to twelve weeks without surgery.","document":"SLR R positive at 40 degrees (reproduces leg pain to lateral calf, relieved by knee flexion), L negative to 80 degrees; crossed SLR negative.","pair":{"with":"crossed-slr","why":"The straight leg raise is sensitive but non-specific, while the crossed test is specific but insensitive; together they bracket the probability."},"history":"Ask whether the leg pain is worse than the back pain and whether it goes below the knee, which is the history that makes the test worth doing and interpretable."},"crossed-slr":{"vignette":"A 45-year-old woman has left-sided sciatica, and raising her right leg reproduces the left leg pain. A positive crossed straight leg raise is highly specific for a large central or medial disc herniation, so when her pain fails to improve after six weeks she is referred for MRI and a surgical opinion rather than another course of physiotherapy.","trap":"The commonest error is to forget to test the contralateral leg at all; the sign is uncommon and will be missed unless the asymptomatic leg is lifted and the patient is asked where the pain is felt. It is also one of the few examination findings that predicts a sizeable herniation.","teachback":{"q":"Why does raising the opposite leg cause pain in the affected leg?","a":"Lifting the contralateral leg pulls its roots caudally and tilts the dural sac, which shifts the affected root medially and downwards against a herniation that sits in its path. Only a large or medially placed disc fragment can be contacted by that modest movement, which is why the sign is rare but strongly predictive."},"analogy":"It is a hammock with two ropes: pulling one side tilts the whole hammock and rubs the other rope against the tree.","number":"The crossed straight leg raise has pooled sensitivity 28% and specificity 90% for lumbar disc herniation (van der Windt et al, Cochrane 2010).","patient":"Lifting your good leg set off the pain in the bad one, which only happens when the disc is pressing quite firmly on the nerve. That makes it more likely that a scan and a specialist opinion will be useful.","document":"Crossed SLR positive (R leg raise to 50 degrees reproduces L leg pain); SLR L positive at 35 degrees.","pair":{"with":"slr","why":"The crossed test is the specific partner of the sensitive ipsilateral test; a positive crossed test with a positive straight leg raise strongly supports a herniation."},"history":"Ask about bilateral leg symptoms, saddle numbness and bladder change, since a central herniation large enough to give this sign is also the type that causes cauda equina syndrome."},"cauda":{"vignette":"A 50-year-old man with a known disc prolapse develops bilateral leg pain, numbness when wiping and a weak urinary stream. A bladder scan after voiding shows 450 mL, perianal sensation is reduced and anal tone is lax. Those findings make cauda equina syndrome the diagnosis, and he goes for emergency MRI and surgery within hours rather than a routine scan.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on anal tone alone, which is unreliable, or to wait for painless retention and overflow incontinence, which are late and often irreversible. Any patient with back pain and new bladder symptoms, saddle numbness or bilateral sciatica needs a post-void residual and an emergency MRI on the same day.","teachback":{"q":"Why do bladder symptoms, saddle numbness and bilateral leg pain cluster together, and why is timing critical?","a":"The sacral roots S2 to S4 that supply the bladder detrusor, the external sphincters and the perianal skin travel together in the centre of the cauda equina, with the L5 and S1 roots alongside, so a central compression hits all of them. The roots are peripheral nerves that tolerate compression for hours but then infarct, so decompression within 24 to 48 hours gives the best chance of recovering bladder function."},"analogy":"The cauda equina is a bundle of telephone cables in one duct: a central crush cuts the lines to the bladder, the saddle and both legs together.","number":"Urinary retention has sensitivity 90% and specificity 95% for cauda equina syndrome, and a post-void residual over 200 mL should trigger emergency imaging (Deyo, Rainville and Kent, JAMA 1992; British Association of Spine Surgeons cauda equina pathway).","patient":"The nerves at the bottom of your spine that control the bladder and the feeling around your bottom are being squashed, which is an emergency. Surgery today gives the best chance of getting that control back.","document":"Bilateral sciatica, reduced perianal sensation S3 to S5, lax anal tone, post-void residual 450 mL; suspected cauda equina syndrome, emergency MRI whole spine.","pair":{"with":"dre","why":"The rectal examination is one strand of the diagnosis and the least reliable, so it is read together with saddle sensation and the residual volume."},"history":"Ask whether the patient can feel the urine passing and the paper when wiping, and whether both legs are now involved, since these answers decide the emergency before any examination."},"lumbar-roots":{"vignette":"A 42-year-old man with sciatica has weakness of great toe extension, numbness over the dorsum of the foot and intact reflexes. That pattern is an L5 root, usually from an L4-L5 disc, so the MRI request names the level and his physiotherapist is told to watch for foot drop rather than treating the leg generically.","trap":"The commonest error is to test only the ankle and knee reflexes and conclude the examination is normal when the L5 root has no reliable reflex; L5 is tested by great toe and ankle dorsiflexion and sensation on the dorsum of the foot. Also, dermatomes overlap, so sensory change alone is the weakest localiser.","teachback":{"q":"Why does an L4-L5 disc usually compress the L5 root rather than L4?","a":"Each root leaves the canal through the foramen below its own vertebra, so the L4 root exits above the L4-L5 disc and has already gone by the time a posterolateral herniation at that level bulges into the canal. The L5 root is still inside the canal descending to its own foramen and lies directly in the path of the disc, so it is the root caught."},"analogy":"The roots leave the spinal canal like cars taking motorway exits: a crash on the carriageway just past junction 4 hits the cars still heading for junction 5.","number":"About 95% of lumbar disc herniations occur at L4-L5 or L5-S1, so L5 and S1 root signs account for nearly all sciatica (Deyo and Weinstein, N Engl J Med 2001).","patient":"Your weak big toe and the numb patch on the top of your foot point to one particular nerve root, the one that usually gets caught by the disc between the fourth and fifth bones. That tells us where to look on the scan and what to watch for in the clinic.","document":"L5 root signs R: EHL 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5, reduced sensation dorsum of foot; ankle and knee jerks 2+ symmetrical; SLR positive 45 degrees.","pair":{"with":"reflexes","why":"Reflexes give the S1 and L4 levels but nothing for L5, so the root screen must add toe extension and foot dorsum sensation."},"history":"Ask where the pain and numbness run on the leg, since the patient's map of the symptoms usually names the root before you test it."},"c-spine-rule":{"vignette":"A 28-year-old driver in a rear-end collision at low speed is sitting in the waiting room, has no midline neck tenderness, walked in and can rotate the neck 45 degrees each way. He meets the Canadian C-spine rule for no imaging, so he leaves with analgesia and advice instead of a collar and a CT.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to apply the rule to patients outside its scope, including those under 16, intoxicated, with GCS below 15 or with distracting injuries, or to skip the rotation step because the collar is on. The rule requires the full three steps, and any high-risk factor such as age 65 or over, a dangerous mechanism or paraesthesia in the limbs mandates imaging.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the ability to rotate the neck 45 degrees each way safely exclude a clinically important injury?","a":"An unstable fracture or ligamentous injury causes muscle spasm and pain that prevent active rotation through that range, because the patient's own protective reflexes limit movement. The rule only reaches this step in patients with no high-risk factor and a low-risk feature such as simple rear-end collision or ambulation, in whom a missed injury is already rare."},"analogy":"The rule is three gates in a row: the first turns back the high-risk, the second checks that it is safe to test, and the third asks the patient to prove the neck is stable by moving it.","number":"The Canadian C-spine rule had sensitivity 100% and specificity 42.5% for clinically important cervical spine injury in 8,924 alert, stable trauma patients (Stiell et al, JAMA 2001).","patient":"Because of the type of accident, your age, the lack of tenderness in the middle of the neck and the fact you can turn your head freely, the chance of a serious neck injury is very low and an X-ray would not add anything. Expect some muscle soreness for a few days.","document":"Canadian C-spine rule: no high-risk factors, simple rear-end MVC, ambulatory, no midline tenderness, active rotation 45 degrees bilaterally; no imaging indicated.","pair":{"with":"ottawa-ankle","why":"Both are high-sensitivity rules that let you skip imaging safely, and both fail when applied outside their inclusion criteria."},"history":"Ask about the mechanism, specifically a fall from over 1 metre, an axial load, a high-speed or rollover crash or ejection, since the dangerous mechanism is a high-risk factor that ends the rule at step one."},"spurling":{"vignette":"A 47-year-old woman has neck pain radiating into the thumb and index finger with tingling. Extending her neck, tilting it to the right and pressing down on the head reproduces the arm pain. A positive Spurling's test makes C6 radiculopathy the diagnosis rather than carpal tunnel syndrome, so you check her biceps reflex and plan conservative management with a nerve root label.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to accept neck pain alone as positive; the test is positive only when it reproduces the radiating arm symptoms. It should also not be performed when a fracture, instability or myelopathy is possible, and a negative test does not exclude radiculopathy because sensitivity is low.","teachback":{"q":"Why does extension with lateral flexion and compression provoke a cervical root?","a":"Extension and lateral flexion narrow the intervertebral foramina on the side of the tilt by up to a third, and axial compression narrows them further. A root already compromised by a disc or an osteophyte is squeezed in that narrowed foramen, reproducing its pain and paraesthesia along the dermatome."},"analogy":"The foramen is a doorway that gets narrower when you lean the frame; a bag already jammed in it gets squeezed harder.","number":"Spurling's test has sensitivity 30% and specificity 93% for cervical radiculopathy confirmed by electrodiagnosis, so it rules in rather than out (Tong et al, Spine 2002).","patient":"Pressing down on your head with the neck tilted set off the pain into your arm, which tells me a nerve is being pinched where it leaves the neck rather than at the wrist. Most of these settle over a couple of months with the right exercises.","document":"Spurling's positive R (reproduces C6 pain to thumb and index), ULTT median positive, biceps reflex 1+ R; C6 radiculopathy.","pair":{"with":"phalen","why":"Thumb and index finger tingling is shared by C6 radiculopathy and carpal tunnel syndrome, and Spurling's test versus the wrist tests separates the neck from the wrist."},"history":"Ask whether the tingling is worse with neck positions or at night with the wrist flexed, which points to the neck or the carpal tunnel before any provocation."},"ottawa-ankle":{"vignette":"A 30-year-old man twisted his ankle playing football and limps in. He can bear weight for four steps, and there is no tenderness over the posterior edge of either malleolus, the navicular or the base of the fifth metatarsal. Because he fails no Ottawa criterion, you skip the X-ray and discharge him with ice, support and early mobilisation.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to palpate the tip or anterior edge of the malleolus rather than the posterior edge and the distal 6 cm, or to count a painful limp as inability to bear weight; four steps, even limping, counts as weight bearing. The rules do not apply under 18 in some versions, in intoxication, in neuropathy or in multiple injuries.","teachback":{"q":"Why were the posterior malleolar edges, the navicular and the fifth metatarsal base chosen as the tender points?","a":"These are the sites where fractures occur after inversion and eversion injuries: the posterior distal malleoli for malleolar fractures, the navicular for midfoot fractures and the fifth metatarsal base for the avulsion fracture caused by peroneus brevis. Ligament sprains cause tenderness anteriorly and inferiorly over the ligaments, so palpating bone edges rather than ligaments separates fracture from sprain."},"analogy":"The rule is a metal detector swept over four known spots where the coins are buried; if it stays silent there, digging anywhere else is a waste.","number":"The Ottawa ankle rules have sensitivity of 96 to 99% with a pooled negative likelihood ratio of 0.08 for ankle fractures, and reduce ankle radiography by 30 to 40% (Bachmann et al, BMJ 2003 meta-analysis).","patient":"You can put weight on the ankle and the bones are not tender at the places where they break, so the chance of a fracture is tiny and an X-ray is not needed. This is a sprain, and moving it early helps it heal.","document":"Ottawa ankle rules negative (weight bearing 4 steps, no tenderness posterior edge lateral or medial malleolus, navicular or 5th MT base); no X-ray; lateral ankle sprain.","pair":{"with":"squeeze","why":"The Ottawa rules exclude fracture but say nothing about the syndesmosis, which needs its own squeeze and external rotation tests in a high-energy or rotational injury."},"history":"Ask whether the patient could walk immediately after the injury, since inability to take four steps both at the time and in the department is a criterion."},"ottawa-knee":{"vignette":"A 70-year-old woman tripped and fell onto her knee. She can walk four steps but her age alone meets an Ottawa knee criterion, so she is X-rayed and found to have a tibial plateau fracture; a 40-year-old with the same examination and no patellar or fibular head tenderness would have been discharged without a film.","trap":"The commonest error is to apply the rule outside blunt trauma within a week, or to test flexion to 90 degrees casually; the patient must be unable to flex, not merely unwilling. The rule also does not detect ligament or meniscal injuries, which need the examination that follows the X-ray decision.","teachback":{"q":"Why is age 55 a criterion in the knee rule but 65 in the C-spine rule?","a":"Each threshold was derived from the data: the age at which fracture prevalence after blunt trauma rose sharply enough to justify imaging everyone above it. Bone around the knee is largely cancellous and osteoporotic by 55, so low-energy falls fracture the tibial plateau and patella at a younger age than the cervical spine fails."},"analogy":"Thresholds in a decision rule are the tide marks left by the data: they mark where the risk actually came in, not a round number someone liked.","number":"The Ottawa knee rule has pooled sensitivity 98.5% and specificity 48.6% for knee fractures after blunt trauma (Bachmann et al, Ann Intern Med 2004 meta-analysis).","patient":"At your age a fall onto the knee can crack the bone even when you can still walk, so we will X-ray it before deciding on anything else.","document":"Ottawa knee rule positive (age 70); X-ray knee requested; able to flex to 90 degrees, weight bearing 4 steps, no isolated patellar or fibular head tenderness.","pair":{"with":"knee-effusion","why":"The Ottawa rule decides the X-ray, while a tense effusion within hours of injury decides whether to suspect an anterior cruciate rupture that the X-ray cannot show."},"history":"Ask the mechanism and when it happened, since the rule applies only to blunt trauma within the past 7 days."},"thompson":{"vignette":"A 44-year-old squash player felt a blow to the back of the ankle and heard a snap. Kneeling on a chair, squeezing his calf produces no plantarflexion of the foot, while the other side moves briskly. The absent response confirms a complete Achilles rupture even though he can still weakly plantarflex using the other tendons, so he goes into an equinus cast and to the orthopaedic clinic within days rather than being told he has a sprain.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to accept active plantarflexion against gravity as proof the tendon is intact; the long toe flexors, tibialis posterior and peroneals can plantarflex the foot without the Achilles. The squeeze test isolates the gastrocnemius-soleus tendon unit and must be compared with the uninjured side.","teachback":{"q":"Why does squeezing the calf plantarflex the foot, and why does this bypass voluntary movement?","a":"Compressing the calf muscles deforms them and shortens the distance between their origin and the heel through the Achilles tendon, so the foot plantarflexes mechanically without any nerve impulse. If the tendon is ruptured the shortening is not transmitted to the calcaneus and the foot does not move, regardless of what the patient can do voluntarily with other muscles."},"analogy":"Squeezing the calf is tugging the bell rope from the middle: if the rope is cut below your hand, the bell stays silent however hard you pull.","number":"The calf squeeze test has sensitivity 96% and specificity 93% for complete Achilles tendon rupture (Maffulli, Am J Sports Med 1998).","patient":"When I squeezed your calf your foot did not move, which shows the big tendon at the back of the ankle has snapped. You can still push a little with other muscles, which is why it felt like a sprain, but it needs a cast or surgery to heal properly.","document":"Thompson test positive R (no plantarflexion on calf squeeze, prone knee flexed), palpable gap 4 cm above calcaneal insertion, L normal; Achilles rupture.","pair":{"with":"ottawa-ankle","why":"The Ottawa rules address bone while the Thompson test addresses the tendon, and a patient who passes the first can still have a complete rupture found by the second."},"history":"Ask about recent fluoroquinolone or steroid use and whether there was a sudden snap felt as a kick from behind, which are the classic antecedents of rupture."},"squeeze":{"vignette":"A 25-year-old rugby player has ankle pain after an external rotation injury, with swelling above the ankle rather than over the lateral ligament. Squeezing the tibia and fibula together at mid-calf produces pain at the distal tibiofibular joint. A positive squeeze test suggests a syndesmosis injury, so he gets weight-bearing views and a specialist referral rather than the routine sprain advice.","trap":"The commonest error is to treat any lateral ankle pain as a lateral ligament sprain and miss the high ankle sprain, which recovers far more slowly and may need fixation. The squeeze test has low sensitivity, so a negative test does not exclude syndesmosis injury, and the external rotation stress test should be added.","teachback":{"q":"Why does compressing the fibula against the tibia at mid-calf cause pain at the ankle?","a":"The tibia and fibula are joined distally by the syndesmotic ligaments, so squeezing the bones together proximally separates them distally like a lever, stretching the anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament and the interosseous membrane. If those structures are torn, the stretch reproduces pain exactly at the joint above the ankle."},"analogy":"Pinching the handles of a pair of tongs together at one end opens the jaws at the other; the syndesmosis is the hinge being forced apart.","number":"The syndesmosis squeeze test has specificity of about 88% but low sensitivity, while the dorsiflexion-external rotation stress test has sensitivity 71%, so the two are used together (Sman, Hiller and Refshauge, Br J Sports Med 2013 systematic review).","patient":"The pain is coming from the strong ligament that holds the two shin bones together just above the ankle, which is a more serious kind of sprain than the usual one. It takes longer to heal and sometimes needs an operation, so the specialist should see you.","document":"Squeeze test positive at distal tibiofibular joint, external rotation stress test positive, tenderness over AITFL, no Ottawa criteria met; suspected syndesmosis injury, weight-bearing radiographs.","pair":{"with":"ottawa-ankle","why":"A negative Ottawa examination excludes fracture but not syndesmosis, which needs its own provocation tests after a rotational injury."},"history":"Ask about an external rotation or dorsiflexion mechanism with the foot planted, which is the injury that tears the syndesmosis rather than the lateral ligaments."},"lachman":{"vignette":"A 22-year-old footballer felt a pop and his knee swelled within an hour. With the knee at 20 to 30 degrees and the femur stabilised, pulling the tibia forward produces a soft, mushy endpoint with increased travel compared with the other knee. That Lachman test is an anterior cruciate rupture, so he gets a brace, crutches and a sports knee clinic referral rather than reassurance.","trap":"The commonest error is to perform the test at 90 degrees or with the hamstrings guarding; at 90 the posterior horn of the meniscus blocks translation, so the anterior drawer misses most ruptures. The Lachman is done near full extension with the patient relaxed, and the quality of the endpoint matters as much as the amount of movement.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the Lachman test more sensitive than the anterior drawer for anterior cruciate rupture?","a":"At 20 to 30 degrees of flexion the anterior cruciate ligament is the only structure resisting anterior translation of the tibia, and the hamstrings are relaxed and mechanically disadvantaged. At 90 degrees the meniscal horns and the capsule block translation and the hamstrings can guard, so a torn ligament is hidden."},"analogy":"Testing at 90 degrees is like checking a door chain with the door wedged by a chair; near extension the chair is gone and only the chain holds.","number":"The Lachman test has pooled sensitivity 85% and specificity 94% for anterior cruciate rupture, compared with 55% and 92% for the anterior drawer (Benjaminse, Gokeler and van der Schans, J Orthop Sports Phys Ther 2006 meta-analysis).","patient":"The main ligament in the centre of your knee has torn, which is why it swelled so quickly and feels loose. It will need a specialist to decide between rehabilitation and reconstruction, depending on how much sport you want to return to.","document":"Lachman positive R (grade 2 translation, soft endpoint), anterior drawer positive, pivot shift not tolerated; tense haemarthrosis; suspected ACL rupture.","pair":{"with":"ant-drawer-knee","why":"Both test anterior translation, but the Lachman at 20 degrees catches the ruptures that the drawer at 90 degrees misses."},"history":"Ask whether the patient heard or felt a pop and whether the swelling came within two hours, the two history items that most predict a cruciate rupture."},"ant-drawer-knee":{"vignette":"A 35-year-old woman has a knee that gives way on stairs months after a skiing fall. With the knee at 90 degrees and you sitting on her foot, the tibia slides forward more than on the other side. A positive anterior drawer in a chronic knee confirms anterior cruciate deficiency, so she is referred for rehabilitation and surgical assessment rather than a brace alone.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to rely on the anterior drawer in the acute knee, where pain, haemarthrosis and hamstring spasm hide the laxity; it is more useful in the chronic knee. It is also falsely positive when the posterior cruciate is torn and the tibia starts from a sagging position, so always check for a posterior sag first.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a posterior cruciate rupture produce a false-positive anterior drawer?","a":"When the posterior cruciate is torn the tibia sags backwards under gravity at 90 degrees, so the test starts from a posteriorly displaced position. Pulling the tibia forward simply returns it to its neutral position, which feels like excess anterior movement but is actually the reduction of a posterior sag."},"analogy":"It is like measuring how far a drawer opens without noticing it was already pushed in too far: the extra travel is the drawer coming back to flush.","number":"The anterior drawer test has pooled sensitivity 55% and specificity 92% for anterior cruciate rupture (Benjaminse et al, J Orthop Sports Phys Ther 2006).","patient":"The shin bone slides forward more than it should, which means the ligament in the centre of the knee that stops that movement is not working. Strengthening the muscles around the knee helps, and the surgeon can discuss repairing the ligament.","document":"Anterior drawer positive R at 90 degrees (grade 2, no posterior sag), Lachman positive, pivot shift positive; chronic ACL deficiency.","pair":{"with":"lachman","why":"The drawer is the chronic-knee partner of the Lachman and is read only after excluding a posterior sag."},"history":"Ask about giving way during pivoting rather than on straight-line walking, which is the symptom of cruciate deficiency that distinguishes it from a meniscal tear."},"mcmurray":{"vignette":"A 32-year-old man has knee pain and clicking since twisting it, with intermittent locking. Flexing the knee fully, externally rotating the tibia and extending it produces a palpable clunk with pain at the medial joint line. A positive McMurray's test with joint line tenderness and a locking history makes a medial meniscal tear likely, so he is referred for MRI and arthroscopy consideration rather than discharged with analgesia.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to count pain alone as positive; the classic sign is a palpable or audible click or clunk, and painful crepitus in an arthritic knee is a false positive. The test is also insensitive, so a negative result does not exclude a tear, and it should not be forced in an acutely locked or very painful knee.","teachback":{"q":"What produces the click in McMurray's test?","a":"With the knee fully flexed the posterior horn of the meniscus is compressed between the femur and tibia, and rotating the tibia drags the torn fragment under the femoral condyle. As the knee extends the condyle rides over the displaced fragment and snaps it back into place, producing the clunk that is felt at the joint line."},"analogy":"It is a loose rug under a heavy chair: drag the chair across it and you feel the thud as the leg drops over the fold.","number":"McMurray's test has pooled sensitivity 70.5% and specificity 71.1% for meniscal tears, so neither result settles the question (Hegedus et al, J Orthop Sports Phys Ther 2007 meta-analysis).","patient":"The click I felt when bending and twisting your knee comes from a torn piece of the cartilage cushion catching between the bones, which also explains the locking. A scan will show the tear and whether it needs trimming.","document":"McMurray's positive medial (clunk with external rotation), medial joint line tenderness, Thessaly positive, small effusion, locking history; suspected medial meniscal tear.","pair":{"with":"thessaly","why":"Both provoke a torn meniscus, but Thessaly loads it in standing and McMurray in lying; neither is accurate enough alone and together with joint line tenderness they build the case."},"history":"Ask about true locking, meaning the knee gets stuck short of full extension and has to be wiggled free, which is the history that most strongly suggests a displaced tear."},"thessaly":{"vignette":"A 28-year-old woman has medial knee pain with a sense of catching. Standing on the affected leg with the knee bent to 20 degrees and holding your hands, she rotates her body three times each way and feels a catch and pain at the medial joint line that is absent on the other side. A positive Thessaly test supports a meniscal tear, and with joint line tenderness it tips the balance towards MRI.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat the original sensitivity and specificity figures as settled; later studies found the test far less accurate, so it is a provocation to combine with the history and joint line tenderness rather than a stand-alone diagnosis. It must be done on the normal knee first and never on an acutely locked or unstable knee.","teachback":{"q":"What does standing rotation at 20 degrees do to the meniscus that supine tests do not?","a":"Weight bearing at 20 degrees of flexion compresses the menisci under the full body load and rotation shears them between the femoral condyle and tibial plateau, which reproduces the loading of walking and turning. A torn fragment is caught and displaced under that load, giving the catch or locking that the patient recognises as their symptom."},"analogy":"It is turning on a planted foot on a loose doormat: the fold catches under your heel only when your full weight is on it.","number":"The Thessaly test at 20 degrees had sensitivity 89 to 92% and specificity 96 to 97% in its original description (Karachalios et al, J Bone Joint Surg Am 2005), but a later prospective study found sensitivity 64% and specificity 53% (Goossens et al, J Orthop Sports Phys Ther 2015).","patient":"Twisting on the bent knee reproduced the catching you have been feeling, which fits a torn cartilage cushion inside the joint. We will arrange a scan to confirm it and decide whether physiotherapy or a keyhole operation is best.","document":"Thessaly test positive R at 20 degrees (medial joint line pain and catching), L negative; medial joint line tenderness; McMurray negative.","pair":{"with":"mcmurray","why":"Both provoke a torn meniscus, but Thessaly loads it in standing and McMurray in lying; neither is accurate enough alone."},"history":"Ask whether the pain and catching come with twisting on a planted foot or squatting, which is the same mechanism the test reproduces."},"joint-line":{"vignette":"A 55-year-old man twisted his knee getting out of a car and has pain on squatting. There is exquisite tenderness along the medial joint line but none over the collateral ligament above or below it, and he has a small effusion. Joint line tenderness with a twisting mechanism points to a meniscal tear, so he is sent for MRI rather than being managed as a ligament sprain.","trap":"The commonest error is to palpate too high or too low and confuse ligament or pes anserinus tenderness with joint line tenderness; find the joint line with the knee flexed to 90 degrees by rolling the thumb across the tibial plateau edge. In older knees it is also tender from osteoarthritis, which lowers its specificity.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the meniscus tender at the joint line when most of it has no nerve supply?","a":"The inner two thirds of the meniscus are avascular and insensate, but the outer rim and its capsular attachments are innervated and vascularised. A tear that reaches or tugs on that peripheral rim, or the synovitis it provokes, causes tenderness exactly at the joint line where the meniscus meets the capsule."},"analogy":"The meniscus is a rubber washer glued at its rim: only the glued edge can feel it when the washer is torn.","number":"Joint line tenderness has pooled sensitivity 63% and specificity 77% for meniscal tears (Hegedus et al, J Orthop Sports Phys Ther 2007).","patient":"The tenderness runs along the line where the cartilage cushion sits between the bones, and with the twisting injury that suggests the cushion is torn. A scan will show us how large the tear is.","document":"Medial joint line tenderness R at 90 degrees, no MCL tenderness, small effusion, McMurray equivocal, Thessaly positive; suspected medial meniscal tear.","pair":{"with":"valgus-stress","why":"Medial knee pain after injury is either the meniscus, tender at the joint line, or the medial collateral ligament, tender above or below it and lax on valgus stress."},"history":"Ask about pain on squatting and twisting, which loads the posterior horn where most degenerative and traumatic tears sit."},"knee-effusion":{"vignette":"A 19-year-old woman injured her knee skiing two hours ago and it is already tense and swollen. A patellar tap is positive and the knee is too tight to examine for ligaments. A haemarthrosis within two hours means an anterior cruciate rupture in most cases, so she gets a brace, crutches and an early sports knee review rather than a diagnosis of sprain.","trap":"The commonest error is to miss a small effusion by going straight to the patellar tap, which needs a large volume; the bulge sign, milking fluid from the medial side and watching it reappear with a stroke on the lateral side, detects small effusions. The other error is to call synovial thickening or prepatellar bursitis an effusion.","teachback":{"q":"Why does swelling within two hours of injury mean blood, and why does blood in the joint point to a cruciate tear or fracture?","a":"Synovial fluid accumulates slowly over 12 to 24 hours as the inflamed synovium weeps, whereas blood pours in from a torn vascular structure within minutes to a couple of hours. The cruciate ligaments, the peripheral meniscus and bone are the vascular structures inside the joint, so a rapid tense effusion means one of them has torn."},"analogy":"A slow puddle under the sink is a weeping seal; a flood within the hour is a burst pipe.","number":"In acute traumatic haemarthrosis of the knee, an anterior cruciate ligament tear was found at arthroscopy in 72% of cases (DeHaven, Am J Sports Med 1980).","patient":"The knee has filled with blood very quickly, which usually means one of the main ligaments inside has torn. We will support the knee now and the specialist will examine it properly once the swelling has eased.","document":"Tense R knee effusion within 2 h of injury, patellar tap positive, bulge sign positive, ROM 10 to 60 degrees limited by swelling; ligament examination deferred; suspected haemarthrosis.","pair":{"with":"lachman","why":"The effusion tells you something vascular has torn, and the Lachman test, once the knee allows it, tells you whether it was the cruciate."},"history":"Ask how soon after the injury the swelling appeared, since under 2 hours means blood and over 12 hours means synovial fluid."},"valgus-stress":{"vignette":"A 24-year-old footballer was tackled from the side and has medial knee pain. With the knee at 30 degrees a valgus force opens the medial joint about 8 mm with a firm endpoint, but at full extension the knee is stable. That grade 2 isolated medial collateral injury is treated in a hinged brace with early motion rather than surgery, which would be considered if it had also opened in full extension.","trap":"The commonest error is to test only at full extension, where the cruciates and posterior capsule stabilise the knee and hide an isolated collateral tear; test at 30 degrees to isolate the collateral and then at 0 degrees to detect the combined injury. Grade the gap and the endpoint, not the pain.","teachback":{"q":"Why is the valgus stress test done at both 30 degrees and full extension?","a":"At 30 degrees of flexion the posterior capsule and cruciate ligaments are relaxed, so the medial collateral ligament is the main restraint and opening there means it is injured. In full extension those secondary restraints are tight, so opening in extension means the collateral and at least one cruciate or the capsule have gone, which is a different injury with a different treatment."},"analogy":"It is testing a gate with and without its extra bolt: unbolted it swings if the hinge is broken, but if it swings even when bolted the whole frame is damaged.","number":"Medial collateral injuries are graded by medial joint opening at 30 degrees: grade 1 under 5 mm, grade 2 5 to 10 mm and grade 3 over 10 mm with no firm endpoint (American Medical Association Committee on the Medical Aspects of Sports classification).","patient":"The ligament on the inside of your knee has been stretched and partly torn, but the knee is stable when straight, which means the ligaments inside are intact. A hinged brace and early movement will let it heal over about six weeks.","document":"Valgus stress R: 8 mm opening at 30 degrees with firm endpoint, stable at 0 degrees; varus stable; Lachman negative; grade 2 MCL injury.","pair":{"with":"joint-line","why":"Both present as medial knee pain after injury; collateral tenderness is above or below the joint line with laxity on stress, meniscal tenderness is on the line without laxity."},"history":"Ask about the direction of the blow and whether the knee gave way immediately, since a blow to the outside of the knee loads the medial ligament."},"scaphoid":{"vignette":"A 20-year-old fell onto an outstretched hand and has pain at the base of the thumb. Pressing in the anatomical snuffbox is painful, as is the scaphoid tubercle, and the X-ray is normal. Because snuffbox tenderness carries a real risk of occult fracture and avascular necrosis, she leaves in a scaphoid cast with a repeat X-ray or MRI in 10 to 14 days rather than being told it is a sprain.","trap":"The commonest error is to rely on snuffbox tenderness alone, which is almost always present after any wrist injury and so is nearly useless on its own; combine it with scaphoid tubercle tenderness and pain on longitudinal thumb compression. The other error is to discharge a tender wrist with a normal film, since initial radiographs miss a proportion of fractures.","teachback":{"q":"Why is a missed scaphoid fracture so damaging compared with other small wrist fractures?","a":"The scaphoid receives its blood supply from branches entering at its distal pole and running backwards, so a fracture through the waist or proximal pole cuts off the proximal fragment. Untreated, that fragment undergoes avascular necrosis and non-union, which leads to a predictable pattern of wrist arthritis in a young person."},"analogy":"The scaphoid is a village fed by a single road from the far end; a break across the road starves everything beyond it.","number":"Anatomical snuffbox tenderness has sensitivity 100% but specificity only 9% for scaphoid fracture at presentation, and combining it with scaphoid tubercle tenderness and longitudinal compression raises specificity to 74% while keeping sensitivity at 100% (Parvizi et al, J Hand Surg Br 1998).","patient":"The small bone at the base of your thumb is tender in a way that can mean a crack that does not show on the first X-ray. Because that bone heals badly if missed, we will put you in a cast and look again in two weeks.","document":"ASB tenderness, scaphoid tubercle tenderness and positive longitudinal thumb compression R; initial scaphoid series normal; scaphoid cast, repeat imaging day 10 to 14.","pair":{"with":"elbow-ext","why":"Both are post-fall upper limb decisions where the clinical sign decides imaging, and in both the sensitivity is high while the specificity is low."},"history":"Ask whether the fall was onto the outstretched hand with the wrist extended, which is the mechanism that loads the scaphoid waist."},"elbow-ext":{"vignette":"An 8-year-old fell off a climbing frame and is holding her elbow. Asked to put both arms out with palms up and straighten the elbows fully, she cannot extend the injured side. Inability to extend means a probable fracture, so she is X-rayed; a child who extended fully would have gone home with analgesia and advice.","trap":"The commonest error is to accept extension with the arm hanging down or supported; the validated test is active extension with the arms held out and supinated, compared with the other side. The test does not exclude radial head and olecranon fractures completely, so a patient with full extension and persisting pain still needs review.","teachback":{"q":"Why does an elbow fracture prevent full extension when it may not prevent flexion?","a":"Blood from a fracture fills the joint, and the capsule has its greatest capacity at about 70 to 80 degrees of flexion, so an effusion pushes the elbow into that position and makes full extension painful and mechanically blocked. Flexion remains possible because it does not stretch the distended anterior capsule or impact the olecranon into a swollen fossa."},"analogy":"The joint is a balloon inside a hinge: inflate it and the hinge can fold but cannot open flat.","number":"The elbow extension test has sensitivity 96.8% and specificity 48.5% for fracture after acute elbow injury, with a negative predictive value of 98.4% in adults and 95.8% in children (Appelboam et al, BMJ 2008).","patient":"She cannot straighten the elbow fully, which after a fall usually means there is a crack in the bone, so we will X-ray it. If she could straighten it completely, a fracture would be very unlikely.","document":"Elbow extension test positive L (unable to fully extend, arms outstretched supinated, R full); X-ray elbow requested; neurovascular status intact.","pair":{"with":"scaphoid","why":"Both are post-fall upper limb decisions where a sensitive clinical sign decides imaging and a negative result allows safe discharge."},"history":"Ask whether the child has been using the arm since the fall, since a child who has been playing normally and extends fully is unlikely to have a fracture."},"phalen":{"vignette":"A 45-year-old woman wakes at night with numb tingling in the thumb, index and middle fingers that she shakes off. Holding her wrists fully flexed for 60 seconds reproduces the tingling, but so does a flick of the hand in the history. The Phalen's test is only weakly supportive, and it is the nocturnal story, the flick sign and the hypalgesia over the index finger that let you diagnose carpal tunnel syndrome and refer for a splint and nerve conduction studies.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to make the diagnosis on Phalen's and Tinel's tests alone; both have likelihood ratios near 1 and are positive in many normal wrists. The diagnosis rests on the symptom pattern, the hand diagram, hypalgesia in the median distribution and thenar weakness, with the provocation tests as supporting evidence.","teachback":{"q":"Why does wrist flexion provoke median nerve symptoms, and why is the test so non-specific?","a":"Flexing the wrist raises pressure within the carpal tunnel and kinks the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum, which in a nerve already compressed and ischaemic triggers paraesthesia within a minute. Normal nerves can also become symptomatic under sustained pressure, so the test produces tingling in some healthy wrists and many clinicians perform it longer or more forcefully than described."},"analogy":"Phalen's test is stepping on a garden hose that already has a kink: it stops the flow quickly, but stepping hard enough stops a healthy hose too.","number":"Phalen's sign has a positive LR of 1.3 and Tinel's sign 1.4 for carpal tunnel syndrome, whereas the flick sign had a positive LR of 21 in a single study and hypalgesia in the median distribution 3.1 (D'Arcy and McGee, JAMA 2000, Does this patient have carpal tunnel syndrome?).","patient":"Holding your wrists bent brought on the tingling, which fits the nerve at the wrist being squeezed, and your night-time symptoms and the way you shake the hand tell the same story. A night splint is the first treatment, and a nerve test will confirm it.","document":"Phalen's positive bilaterally at 45 s, Tinel's positive R, flick sign positive, hypalgesia index finger pulp R, APB 4/5 R; classic hand diagram; CTS, splint and NCS.","pair":{"with":"spurling","why":"Thumb and index tingling is shared by carpal tunnel syndrome and a C6 root, and the wrist tests versus Spurling's test separate them."},"history":"Ask whether the patient shakes or flicks the hand to relieve the tingling, since that single answer carries more weight than either provocation test."},"tinel-wrist":{"vignette":"A 52-year-old man has tingling in the median fingers at night. Tapping over the median nerve at the wrist crease produces tingling into the fingers on the right. A positive Tinel's sign at the wrist supports carpal tunnel syndrome but, because it is nearly as common in normal wrists, you confirm the diagnosis from the symptom pattern and sensory testing before organising a splint and nerve conduction studies.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to tap hard enough to provoke tingling in anyone, or to tap distal to the retinaculum where the nerve is superficial and sensitive in all; tap lightly with a finger over the nerve just proximal to the crease. A positive test alone has a likelihood ratio close to 1.","teachback":{"q":"What produces the paraesthesia of Tinel's sign, and what does the sign actually indicate about the nerve?","a":"Percussion mechanically depolarises axons that are regenerating or demyelinated and therefore hyperexcitable, sending a volley of impulses perceived as tingling in the nerve's distribution. It indicates sensory axons in an irritable state at that point, which occurs in compression but also in minor trauma and in some normal nerves, so it localises rather than diagnoses."},"analogy":"Tapping an irritable nerve is like flicking a frayed cable: a sound cable stays silent and a frayed one crackles.","number":"Tinel's sign at the wrist has a positive LR of 1.4 and a negative LR of 0.8 for carpal tunnel syndrome, so it neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis (D'Arcy and McGee, JAMA 2000).","patient":"Tapping over the nerve at your wrist sent tingling into your fingers, which fits the nerve being squeezed there, though the test is not proof on its own. Your symptoms and the numbness test together make the diagnosis.","document":"Tinel's positive R at wrist (paraesthesia to median digits), L negative; Phalen's positive 50 s; hypalgesia index pulp R; CTS.","pair":{"with":"phalen","why":"The two provocation tests are usually done together and share the same weakness: both are weak evidence and the diagnosis rests on the history and sensory signs."},"history":"Ask about pregnancy, hypothyroidism, diabetes and repetitive wrist work, which are the causes a positive sign should prompt you to look for."},"finkelstein":{"vignette":"A 33-year-old mother of a six-month-old has pain at the radial side of the wrist when lifting the baby. Holding her thumb and deviating the wrist ulnarward reproduces sharp pain over the radial styloid, and there is tenderness and swelling over the first dorsal compartment. A positive Finkelstein's test with that story is de Quervain tenosynovitis, so she gets a thumb spica splint and a steroid injection offer rather than an X-ray for a sprain.","trap":"The commonest error is to perform the Eichhoff manoeuvre, with the thumb clenched inside the fist, and call it Finkelstein's; the fist version is painful in many normal wrists and has poor specificity. Also, pain at the thumb base rather than the styloid suggests first carpometacarpal arthritis, which the grind test separates.","teachback":{"q":"Why does ulnar deviation with the thumb held cause pain in de Quervain tenosynovitis?","a":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis run through the first dorsal compartment under a tight retinacular sheath at the radial styloid. Ulnar deviation with the thumb flexed drags the thickened, inflamed tendons through that narrowed tunnel, stretching the tendon sheath and reproducing the pain at the styloid."},"analogy":"It is pulling a frayed rope through a tight eyelet: the rope moves, but every fibre snags at the ring.","number":"The WHAT test (wrist hyperflexion and abduction of the thumb against resistance) had sensitivity 99% and specificity 29%, while the Eichhoff manoeuvre had sensitivity 89% and specificity 14%, for de Quervain tenosynovitis confirmed by ultrasound and surgery (Goubau et al, J Hand Surg Eur 2014).","patient":"The two tendons that lift your thumb are inflamed where they pass through a tight tunnel at the wrist, which is common with lifting a baby. A splint rests them, and a steroid injection into the tunnel settles most cases.","document":"Finkelstein's positive R (examiner-held thumb, ulnar deviation, pain at radial styloid), tenderness and swelling over 1st dorsal compartment, CMC grind negative; de Quervain tenosynovitis.","pair":{"with":"scaphoid","why":"Both give radial wrist pain, but de Quervain pain is at the styloid with a tendon story, while scaphoid tenderness is in the snuffbox after a fall."},"history":"Ask about new repetitive lifting, especially of an infant, and about pregnancy or recent delivery, which are the classic settings."},"froment":{"vignette":"A 58-year-old man with elbow pain after months of leaning on a desk has numbness in the ring and little fingers. Asked to grip a sheet of paper between thumb and index finger while you pull it away, his thumb flexes sharply at the interphalangeal joint. A positive Froment's sign shows adductor pollicis weakness, confirming ulnar neuropathy, so you check for clawing and intrinsic wasting and refer for nerve conduction studies to localise it to the elbow.","trap":"The commonest error is to count any difficulty holding the paper as positive; the sign is the specific substitution of flexor pollicis longus, seen as flexion of the thumb tip, when adductor pollicis cannot hold the pinch. Compare with the other hand, and check the median nerve too, since combined lesions and C8 root lesions mimic it.","teachback":{"q":"Why does the thumb tip flex in Froment's sign, and which nerve is doing the compensating?","a":"Key pinch depends on adductor pollicis, which is supplied by the ulnar nerve; when it is paralysed, the patient recruits flexor pollicis longus, supplied by the anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve, to hold the paper. That muscle flexes the interphalangeal joint, so the thumb bends at the tip instead of pressing flat."},"analogy":"It is a bridge whose main cable has failed, so a thinner side cable takes the load and the deck visibly sags.","number":"Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow is the second commonest entrapment neuropathy, with an incidence of about 25 per 100,000 person-years (Mondelli et al, J Neurol 2005).","patient":"Your thumb bends at the tip when you pinch because the muscle that should hold it straight is run by the nerve at the elbow that is being squeezed. Avoiding leaning on the elbow helps, and a nerve test will show how badly it is affected.","document":"Froment's sign positive R (FPL substitution on key pinch), first dorsal interosseous wasting, hypoaesthesia little and ulnar ring finger, Tinel positive at cubital tunnel; ulnar neuropathy at elbow, NCS.","pair":{"with":"phalen","why":"Both are hand nerve entrapments, and the digits involved, thumb to middle for median and ring to little for ulnar, decide which tests to run."},"history":"Ask about leaning on the elbow, prolonged elbow flexion at night and previous elbow fracture, which are the usual causes and the first things to correct."},"painful-arc":{"vignette":"A 50-year-old painter has shoulder pain reaching overhead. Abducting the arm actively, he winces between 60 and 120 degrees and is comfortable above and below. That painful arc, with his age and overhead work, points to rotator cuff tendinopathy rather than a frozen shoulder or an arthritic joint, so he gets physiotherapy and a steroid injection option rather than imaging.","trap":"The commonest error is to confuse a painful arc in mid-range with pain at the top of abduction, which comes from the acromioclavicular joint, or with the global restriction of a frozen shoulder. The arc is active, mid-range and improves with lowering, and it is one of the few shoulder tests with a useful likelihood ratio.","teachback":{"q":"Why is pain confined to the middle of the abduction arc in rotator cuff disease?","a":"Between about 60 and 120 degrees of abduction the greater tuberosity, with the supraspinatus tendon and the subacromial bursa on it, passes beneath the acromion and coracoacromial ligament, narrowing the subacromial space to its minimum. An inflamed or torn tendon is compressed there and hurts, then clears the acromion above 120 degrees and the pain eases."},"analogy":"It is a lorry passing under a low bridge: the roof scrapes only while it is directly beneath the arch.","number":"A painful arc has a positive LR of 3.7 for rotator cuff disease, the only single test to show a moderate increase in likelihood, and a normal result a small decrease (Hermans et al, JAMA 2013, Does this patient with shoulder pain have rotator cuff disease?).","patient":"Your shoulder hurts only through the middle of the lifting movement, which is where the tendon on top of the shoulder is pinched under the bony arch. That tendon is irritated rather than torn through, and exercises are the main treatment.","document":"Painful arc R 60 to 120 degrees active abduction, full passive ROM, Hawkins positive, drop arm negative, ER lag negative; subacromial pain syndrome.","pair":{"with":"frozen","why":"Both limit abduction, but the painful arc has full passive range with pain in the middle, while the frozen shoulder loses passive external rotation and has no pain-free zone."},"history":"Ask about overhead work and whether the pain is worse lying on that shoulder at night, which are the typical features of subacromial pain."},"drop-arm":{"vignette":"A 68-year-old woman fell onto her shoulder a week ago and cannot lift the arm. Passively abducted to 90 degrees and asked to lower it slowly, the arm drops uncontrollably with pain. A positive drop arm sign after trauma in an older patient means a full-thickness rotator cuff tear, so she gets an urgent ultrasound and orthopaedic referral because early repair of acute tears gives better results.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to read inability to abduct because of pain as a positive test; the drop arm is positive when the arm cannot be lowered smoothly from 90 degrees, and pain alone with controlled lowering is negative. It is specific but insensitive, so most cuff tears have a negative test.","teachback":{"q":"Why can a patient with a full-thickness supraspinatus tear often raise the arm but not lower it under control?","a":"The deltoid can abduct the arm, especially once the humeral head is centred by the remaining cuff, so active elevation is often preserved. Controlled lowering requires eccentric work by supraspinatus to resist gravity through the arc where deltoid leverage is poorest, and without the tendon the arm falls through that range."},"analogy":"It is a crane whose main cable has snapped: the jib can be pushed up by hand but there is nothing to pay the load out slowly.","number":"A positive drop arm test has a positive LR of 3.3 for rotator cuff disease, while the external rotation lag sign has a positive LR of 7.2 for a full-thickness tear (Hermans et al, JAMA 2013).","patient":"Your arm drops rather than lowering smoothly, which means one of the tendons that holds the shoulder together has torn through in the fall. Tears from an injury are best repaired early, so the shoulder surgeon should see you soon.","document":"Drop arm positive R, ER lag sign positive, weak resisted ER, painful arc; post-fall; suspected full-thickness supraspinatus and infraspinatus tear, urgent USS.","pair":{"with":"empty-can","why":"The empty can test provokes supraspinatus pain and weakness in tendinopathy as well as tears, while the drop arm is positive only when the tendon has failed."},"history":"Ask about a fall or sudden pull with immediate weakness, since an acute traumatic tear is the one where urgent referral changes the outcome."},"empty-can":{"vignette":"A 55-year-old man has shoulder pain with overhead work and weakness lifting a kettle. With his arm at 90 degrees in the scapular plane, thumb down, resisting downward pressure is weak and painful compared with the other side. The empty can test points to the supraspinatus, so he is given a structured rotator cuff rehabilitation programme and, if weakness persists, an ultrasound.","trap":"The commonest error is to read pain as proof of a tear; pain alone is common in tendinopathy and bursitis, and it is weakness that raises the probability of a tear. Many clinicians also perform the test with the arm in the coronal plane rather than 30 degrees forward in the scapular plane, which changes the muscle loaded.","teachback":{"q":"Why does internal rotation with the thumb down isolate supraspinatus, and what is the argument for the full can position?","a":"Elevation in the scapular plane with internal rotation lines the supraspinatus tendon up with the direction of resistance and reduces the contribution of deltoid and the other cuff muscles, so weakness reflects that tendon. The full can position with the thumb up loads supraspinatus almost as well with less subacromial impingement, so it is less painful and gives a cleaner test of strength."},"analogy":"It is pulling on one specific guy rope of a tent to see whether it holds: the position of the arm chooses which rope you are pulling.","number":"The empty can test has sensitivity 89% for supraspinatus tears when pain or weakness is counted positive and specificity 68% when weakness is the criterion, with the full can test at 86% and 74% (Itoi et al, Am J Sports Med 1999).","patient":"Pressing your arm down in that position was weak and painful, which points to the tendon on top of the shoulder. Targeted exercises strengthen the tendon and the muscles around it, and we will scan it if the weakness does not improve.","document":"Empty can positive R (pain and 4/5 weakness), full can 4/5, painful arc, drop arm negative, ER lag negative; supraspinatus tendinopathy or partial tear.","pair":{"with":"drop-arm","why":"Empty can weakness raises the probability of a supraspinatus tear, and a positive drop arm or lag sign is what confirms a full-thickness one."},"history":"Ask whether the weakness is from pain or from true loss of power, which the patient can often tell you and which changes the reading of the test."},"hawkins":{"vignette":"A 30-year-old swimmer has anterior shoulder pain with training. Forward flexing the arm to 90 degrees and internally rotating it reproduces her pain. A positive Hawkins-Kennedy test supports subacromial impingement, and with a painful arc and no weakness she is treated with a rehabilitation programme and training modification rather than imaging.","trap":"The commonest misuse is to treat a positive Hawkins test as diagnostic; it is positive in most painful shoulders and changes the probability very little. Its value is as a provocation that, with a painful arc and intact strength, fits a subacromial pattern, and a negative test makes impingement less likely.","teachback":{"q":"What does internal rotation at 90 degrees of forward flexion do to the subacromial structures?","a":"Forward flexion brings the greater tuberosity under the coracoacromial arch, and internal rotation drives it further under the acromion and the coracoacromial ligament, pinching the supraspinatus tendon and the subacromial bursa between bone and ligament. Pain reproduced in that position therefore implicates the structures in the subacromial space, but any inflamed tissue there hurts."},"analogy":"It is turning a key in a lock that is already too tight: whatever is in the way gets squeezed.","number":"The Hawkins-Kennedy test has pooled sensitivity 80% and specificity 56%, positive LR 1.8 and negative LR 0.35, for subacromial impingement (Hegedus et al, Br J Sports Med 2012 meta-analysis).","patient":"Turning your arm inward in that position squashes the tendon and its cushion under the bony arch of the shoulder, which is what has been hurting during your stroke. Changing the training and strengthening the shoulder blade muscles usually settles it.","document":"Hawkins-Kennedy positive R, Neer positive, painful arc 70 to 110 degrees, full strength, no instability signs; subacromial impingement syndrome.","pair":{"with":"painful-arc","why":"The painful arc is the shoulder test with a useful likelihood ratio, and the Hawkins test is a provocation that adds little once the arc is known."},"history":"Ask about the sport and the phase of movement that hurts, since overhead athletes with impingement often have underlying instability that changes the treatment."},"frozen":{"vignette":"A 52-year-old woman with diabetes has three months of worsening shoulder pain and stiffness with no injury. With her elbow at her side you cannot externally rotate the shoulder beyond 10 degrees passively, whereas the other side goes to 60. Loss of passive external rotation with a normal X-ray makes this adhesive capsulitis, so she gets an explanation of the long natural history and an intra-articular steroid injection rather than cuff rehabilitation.","trap":"The commonest error is to assess only active movement, which is reduced in cuff disease as well; the frozen shoulder is defined by loss of passive range, with external rotation lost first and most. The other trap is to miss glenohumeral arthritis or a locked posterior dislocation, which also lose external rotation, so an X-ray is needed before the diagnosis.","teachback":{"q":"Why is external rotation lost first and most in adhesive capsulitis?","a":"The disease is a fibrotic contracture of the joint capsule that begins in the rotator interval and the coracohumeral ligament, the anterosuperior structures that are stretched by external rotation. As those tighten, external rotation is restricted before abduction and internal rotation, giving the pattern that separates capsulitis from cuff disease, where passive movement remains free."},"analogy":"The capsule is a leather sleeve that has shrunk in the wash: the seam that shrinks first stops the arm turning outward.","number":"Adhesive capsulitis affects 2 to 5% of the general population and 10 to 20% of people with diabetes, and the clinical definition requires loss of passive external rotation of at least 50% compared with the other side or below 30 degrees, with a normal radiograph (Hand et al, J Shoulder Elbow Surg 2008; British Elbow and Shoulder Society guideline 2015).","patient":"The lining of your shoulder joint has thickened and shrunk, which is why I cannot turn your arm outward even when you relax. It is common with diabetes, it does get better but slowly, over a year or more, and an injection can ease the painful stage.","document":"Passive ER R 10 degrees (L 60), passive abduction 70 degrees, global capsular pattern, strength normal within range, X-ray normal; adhesive capsulitis stage 2.","pair":{"with":"painful-arc","why":"Cuff disease keeps full passive range with pain in a mid-arc, while the frozen shoulder loses passive range, external rotation first, with no pain-free zone."},"history":"Ask about diabetes and thyroid disease and whether the stiffness came after a period of pain without injury, which is the typical sequence."},"log-roll":{"vignette":"An 84-year-old woman fell at home and has groin pain, but she can lift the leg and the X-ray is reported normal. Gently rolling her thigh into internal and external rotation while she lies flat reproduces severe groin pain and she will not let you continue. A positive log roll with that story is an occult hip fracture until an MRI or CT says otherwise, so she is not sent home with a walking frame.","trap":"The commonest error is to accept a normal X-ray and the ability to lift the leg as excluding fracture; impacted femoral neck fractures can be invisible on plain films and allow straight leg raise. The log roll is also positive in septic arthritis and any intra-articular pathology, so it says the joint is the source but not why.","teachback":{"q":"Why does gentle rotation of the thigh provoke intra-articular hip pain when flexion and extension may not?","a":"Rolling the leg rotates the femoral head within the acetabulum without loading the joint, which moves a fractured neck or an inflamed synovium against the capsule and the capsular pain fibres. Because it uses no muscle effort and no axial load, it isolates the joint itself from the surrounding muscles and the pelvis."},"analogy":"It is turning a ball in a socket without lifting it: if the ball is cracked you feel it grind, however gently you turn.","number":"Plain radiographs miss 2 to 10% of hip fractures, and MRI detects occult fractures with sensitivity approaching 100%, which is why a positive log roll with a normal film mandates further imaging (Cannon et al, J Emerg Med 2009 review; NICE hip fracture guideline CG124).","patient":"Rolling your leg gently caused pain deep in the hip, and even though the X-ray looks normal that can mean a crack that the X-ray cannot see. We will do a more detailed scan before you put weight on it.","document":"Log roll positive R (groin pain on passive rotation), able to SLR with pain, no shortening or external rotation, AP and lateral hip X-ray normal; suspected occult NOF, MRI or CT requested.","pair":{"with":"patellar-pubic","why":"The log roll shows the hip joint is the source and the patellar-pubic percussion test specifically suggests a fracture; together they make the case for further imaging."},"history":"Ask whether the patient has been able to bear weight since the fall, since inability to weight bear with a normal film is the classic occult fracture story."},"patellar-pubic":{"vignette":"A 79-year-old man fell and has hip pain with a normal X-ray. Placing the diaphragm of your stethoscope on the pubic symphysis and tapping each patella, the note on the painful side is dull and muffled compared with the other. That asymmetry suggests a fracture is interrupting sound conduction through the femur, so he is kept non-weight-bearing for an MRI rather than discharged.","trap":"The commonest error is to compare the sides with the legs in different positions or with a prosthetic hip on one side, which both change the note; the legs must be straight and symmetrical and a prosthesis makes the test uninterpretable. A positive test is a difference in pitch or volume, not a specific sound.","teachback":{"q":"Why does a fracture change the sound transmitted from the patella to the pubis?","a":"Tapping the patella sends a sound wave along the continuous bone of the femur to the pelvis, where it is heard at the symphysis. A fracture, even an impacted one, breaks that continuity and the wave is dampened and scattered at the fracture line, so the note arrives duller and quieter than on the intact side."},"analogy":"It is tapping one end of a long rail and listening at the other: a crack in the rail muffles the ring.","number":"The patellar-pubic percussion test has pooled sensitivity of about 95% and specificity of about 86% for hip fracture, with its main role in patients with normal initial radiographs (Tiru, Goh and Lim, Singapore Med J 2002; Adams and Yarnold, Am J Emerg Med 1997).","patient":"Tapping your kneecap and listening at the pelvis gave a duller sound on the painful side, which can mean a crack in the thigh bone near the hip that the X-ray has not shown. We will scan it properly before you walk on it.","document":"Patellar-pubic percussion test positive R (dull, reduced note vs L), log roll positive, X-rays normal, no hip prosthesis; non-weight-bearing pending MRI.","pair":{"with":"log-roll","why":"The log roll shows the joint is the source and the percussion test points to a fracture; the two together make the case for further imaging."},"history":"Ask about previous hip replacement on either side, which makes the comparison invalid before you begin."}};
